  @a_cet4_2023_12_1_02, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2023-12-1.mp3', 'News Report One\n新闻一\n\nHave you noticed how similar you are to your friends?\n你有没有注意到你和你的朋友有多么相似？\n\nIt may be because your brains operate in harmony with each other.\n这可能是因为你们的大脑运作是相互协调的。\n\nWe know that friends are more likely to be the same age, gender and ethnic background as each other.\n我们知道，朋友之间更可能在年龄、性别和种族背景方面相同。\n\nNow it seems their brains are alike, too.\n现在看来，大脑也是一样的。\n\nResearchers at the University of California scanned the brains of 42 classmates while they watched videos intended to provoke varying responses.\n在42名同学观看旨在引发不同反应的视频时，加州大学的研究人员对他们的大脑进行了扫描。\n\nSome people might find a romantic scene touching, for instance, while others would feel it was embarrassing.\n例如，有些人可能会觉得浪漫的场景很感人，而另一些人则会觉得尴尬。\n\nThe activity of friends'' brains was more similar than that of people who didn''t know each other, particularly in regions involved in attention, emotion and language.\n朋友间的大脑活动和相互不认识的人的大脑活动相比更为相似，尤其是在涉及注意力、情绪和语言的区域。\n\nThis similarity was strong enough that it could be used to predict whether two people were already friends or not.\n这种相似性足够强，可以用来预测两个人是否已经是朋友。\n\nThe relationship probably goes two ways.\n这种关系可能是双向的。\n\nWe are drawn to people who think like us.\n我们被那些和我们想法相似的人所吸引。\n\nWe then influence their thoughts over time, which may push their brain activity into more closely resembling our own.\n随着时间的推移，我们会影响他们的想法，这可能会促使他们的大脑活动与我们的更相似。\n\nQuestions 1 and 2 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的新闻报道回答第1题和第2题。\n\n1. What may be the reason that friends are similar to each other, according to the recent research?\n1.根据最近的研究，朋友彼此相似的原因可能是什么？\n\n2. What does the news report say about the relationship between friends?\n2.关于朋友之间的关系，新闻报道说了什么？', 77, 1413.12, 40.82, 117.62, 144.79, 166.79, NULL, NULL, 'cet4-2023-12-1-g01', 'NEWS', '[00:40.82]News Report One｜新闻一\n[00:43.39]Have you noticed how similar you are to your friends?｜你有没有注意到你和你的朋友有多么相似？\n[00:46.72]It may be because 