SELECT
  @a_cet4_2023_12_1_14, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2023-12-1.mp3', 'Conversation Two\n对话二\n\nW: Our guest on today''s Book Talk is John Black, the author of the new bestseller Retire Early.\n女：我们今天的图书谈话节目嘉宾是约翰·布莱克，他是最新畅销图书《提前退休》的作者。\n\nM: Thanks for having me on the show, Lisa.\n男：感谢邀请我参加节目，丽莎。\n\nW: John, your book is getting a lot of attention, partly because you write that most people can retire by 40. Is that realistic?\n女：约翰，你的书受到了很多关注，部分原因是你说大多数人都能在40岁时退休。这现实吗？\n\nM: Definitely. When researching this book, I interviewed hundreds of people who retired by\n40. 男：当然。在写这本书时，我采访了数百名40岁退休的人。\n\nW: Okay. But how much money does a person need to retire?\n女：好吧，但是一个人需要多少钱才能退休？\n\nI''ve read articles recommending $1 million as a good figure for retirement.\n我读过一些文章，推荐100万美元是退休时的理想存款。\n\nM: While most financial planners do give numbers, I don''t think that''s useful, as people are living in different areas with different costs of living and have different needs and wants, which is why I suggest that people aim for financial independence.\n男：虽然大多数财务规划师确实给出了数字，但我认为这没有用，因为人们生活在不同的地区，生活成本不同，有不同的需求和愿望，这就是为什么我建议人们以财务独立为目标。\n\nW: What do you mean by financial independence?\n女：你所说的财务独立是什么意思？\n\nM: Having investment income that''s greater than monthly expenses.\n男：有大于每月支出的投资收益。\n\nW: That sounds risky to me. What if an accident happens or you get ill and need medical treatment?\n女：这听起来很冒险。如果发生事故或者你生病需要治疗怎么办？\n\nShouldn''t people have extra cash for emergencies?\n人们不应该有额外的现金以备不时之需吗？\n\nM: Too many people spend their lives working on jobs they hate because they''re afraid.\n男：太多人一生都在做他们讨厌的工作，因为他们害怕。\n\nSo I counsel people to take risks.\n所以我建议人们要冒险。\n\nRetirement doesn''t have to be permanent.\n退休不一定是永久性的。\n\nAnd if people need to, they can go back to work.\n如果人们需要，他们可以回去工作。\n\nW: But it''s not always easy to get back into the workforce after an extended absence.\n女：但在长期不工作后重返职场并不总是那么容易。\n\nM: True. But if you keep current with your skills, you will be attractive to employers.\n男：没错。但如果你能掌握最新的技能，这对雇主会很有吸引力。\n\nW: Maybe. But how can anyone save enough to retire by 40 when most people can''t retire at 60?\n女：也许吧。但是，大多数人在60岁时还不能退休，怎么能有人挣到足够的钱40岁退休呢？\n\nM: Simple. By cutting housing, food and transport expenses and investing half your monthly income.\n男：很简单。削减住房、食品和交通费用，并将月收入的一半用于投资。\n\nW: That sounds impossible.\n女：这听起来不可能。\n\nM: I admit it''s difficult, but it has worked for thousands.\n男：我承认这很难，但这已经在数千人身上得到印证。\n\nQuestions 12 to 15 are based on the conversation you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的对话回答第12至15题。\n\n12. What do we learn about the man''s book published recently?\n12.关于男士最近出版的书，我们了解到了什么？\n\n13. What do the articles the woman read recommend?\n13.女士读过的文章推荐什么？\n\n14. What does the man say about retirement?\n14.关于退休，男士怎么说？\n\n15. How does the man say his proposal about retirement can be carried out?\n15. 男士说他关于退休的建议如何实施？', 118, 1413.12, 611.44, 729.00, 773.16, 794.81, 692.66, 698.20, 'cet4-2023-12-1-g05', 'LONG_DIALOGUE', '[10:11.44]Conversation Two｜对话二\n[10:15.43]W: Our guest on today''s Book Talk is John Black, the author of the new bestseller Retire Early.｜女：我们今天的图书谈话节目嘉宾是约翰·布莱克，他是最新畅销图书《提前退休》的作者。\n[10:23.85]M: Thanks for having me on the show, Lisa.｜男：感谢邀请我参加节目，丽莎。\n[10:25.86]W: John, your book is getting a lot of attention, partly because you write that most people can retire by 40. Is that realistic?｜女：约翰，你的书受到了很多关注，部分原因是你说大多数人都能在40岁时退休。这现实吗？\n[10:35.46]M: Definitely. When researching this book, I interviewed hundreds of people who retired by｜40. 男：当然。在写这本书时，我采访了数百名40岁退休的人。\n[10:41.64]W: Okay. But how much money does a person need to retire?｜女：好吧，但是一个人需要多少钱才能退休？\n[10:45.85]I''ve read articles recommending $1 million as a good figure for retirement.｜我读过一些文章，推荐100万美元是退休时的理想存款。\n[10:51.71]M: While most financial planners do give numbers, I don''t think that''s useful, as people are living in different areas with different costs of living and have different needs and wants, which is why I suggest that people aim for financial independence.｜男：虽然大多数财务规划师确实给出了数字，但我认为这没有用，因为人们生活在不同的地区，生活成本不同，有不同的需求和愿望，这就是为什么我建议人们以财务独立为目标。\n[11:06.72]W: What do you mean by financial independence?｜女：你所说的财务独立是什么意思？\n[11:10.00]M: Having investment income that''s greater than monthly expenses.｜男：有大于每月支出的投资收益。\n[11:13.85]W: That sounds risky to me. What if an accident happens or you get ill and need medical treatment?｜女：这听起来很冒险。如果发生事故或者你生病需要治疗怎么办？\n[11:21.08]Shouldn''t people have extra cash for emergencies?｜人们不应该有额外的现金以备不时之需吗？\n[11:24.99]M: Too many people spend their lives working on jobs they hate because they''re afraid.｜男：太多人一生都在做他们讨厌的工作，因为他们害怕。\n[11:30.39]So I counsel people to take risks.｜所以我建议人们要冒险。\n[11:32.66]Retirement doesn''t have to be permanent.｜退休不一定是永久性的。\n[11:35.05]And if people need to, they can go back to work.｜如果人们需要，他们可以回去工作。\n[11:38.50]W: But it''s not always easy to get back into the workforce after an extended absence.｜女：但在长期不工作后重返职场并不总是那么容易。\n[11:44.22]M: True. But if you keep current with your skills, you will be attractive to employers.｜男：没错。但如果你能掌握最新的技能，这对雇主会很有吸引力。\n[11:49.56]W: Maybe. But how can anyone save enough to retire by 40 when most people can''t retire at 60?｜女：也许吧。但是，大多数人在60岁时还不能退休，怎么能有人挣到足够的钱40岁退休呢？\n[11:56.30]M: Simple. By cutting housing, food and transport expenses and investing half your monthly income.｜男：很简单。削减住房、食品和交通费用，并将月收入的一半用于投资。\n[12:03.13]W: That sounds impossible.｜女：这听起来不可能。\n[12:05.10]M: I admit it''s difficult, but it has worked for thousands.｜男：我承认这很难，但这已经在数千人身上得到印证。\n[12:09.00]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the conversation you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的对话回答第12至15题。\n[12:14.57]12. What do we learn about the man''s book published recently?｜12.关于男士最近出版的书，我们了解到了什么？\n[12:34.06]13. What do the articles the woman read recommend?｜13.女士读过的文章推荐什么？\n[12:53.16]14. What does the man say about retirement?｜14.关于退休，男士怎么说？\n[13:15.11]15. How does the man say his proposal about retirement can be carried out?｜15. 男士说他关于退休的建议如何实施？', NOW(), NOW()
WHERE @a_cet4_2023_12_1_14 IS NOT NULL
