  @a_cet4_2023_12_1_20, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2023-12-1.mp3', 'Passage Two\n短文二\n\nFor college students under pressure, a dog may be the best stress fighter around, according to the finding of a recent study.\n根据最近的一项研究发现，对于承受压力的大学生来说，狗可能是最好的压力斗士。\n\n“It''s a really powerful finding,” said Patricia Pendry, who led the study.\n这项研究的负责人帕特丽夏·彭德里说：“这是一个非常有用的发现。”\n\n“Universities are doing a lot of great work trying to help students succeed academically, especially those who may be at risk due to a history of mental health issues or academic and learning issues.“\n大学正在做很多工作，试图帮助学生在学业上取得成功，尤其是那些可能因心理健康问题、学术和学习问题而面临风险的学生。\n\nThis study shows that traditional stress management approaches aren''t as effective for this population compared with programs that focus on providing opportunities to interact with therapy dogs.”\n这项研究表明，与专注于提供与治疗犬互动机会的项目相比，传统的压力管理方法对这一人群并不同样有效。”\n\nThe researchers measured executive functioning in the students involved in the study.\n研究人员测量了参与研究的学生的执行能力。\n\nExecutive function is a term for the skills one needs to plan, organize, motivate, concentrate, and memorize.\n执行能力是指一个人需要计划、组织、激励、集中注意力和记忆的技能。\n\nThese are skills students need to succeed in college.\n这些都是学生在大学取得成功所需要的技能。\n\nThe researchers found that students who were most at risk had the most improvements in executive functioning after interacting with therapy dogs.\n研究人员发现，风险最大的学生在与治疗犬互动后，执行功能改善最多。\n\nThese results remained when researchers followed up six weeks later.\n当研究人员在六周后进行随访时，这些结果仍然如此。\n\nMany universities provide academic stress management programs and workshops.\n许多大学提供学业压力管理课程和研讨会。\n\nThese are traditionally very similar to college classes.\n这些课程与传统上的大学课程非常相似。\n\nThey often talk about ways to get more sleep, set goals, or manage stress or anxiety.\n他们经常谈论如何获得更多的睡眠、设定目标或管理压力或焦虑。\n\nPendry acknowledges that these are really important topics, and these workshops help typical students succeed, but they''re less helpful for struggling students.\n彭德里承认，这些都是非常重要的话题，这些研讨会帮助一般的学生取得成功，但对陷入困境的学生帮助不大。\n\nPendry believes those students may experience the programs as another lecture and feel even more stressed.\n彭德里认为，这些学生可能会将这些课程作为另一场讲座来体验，并感到压力更大。\n\nQuestions 19 to 21 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的短文回答第19至21题。\n\n19. What does the recent study led by Patricia Pendry aim to examine?\n19. 由帕特丽夏·彭德里领导的最近这项研究旨在验证什么？\n\n20. What did the new study measure in the participants?\n20. 这项新研究对参与者测量了什么？\n\n21. What does Patricia Pendry think traditional stress management programs may do?\n21. 帕特丽夏·彭德里认为传统的压力管理课程怎么样？', 114, 1413.12, 1025.12, 1138.96, 1164.76, 1185.29, 1069.46, 1075.02, 'cet4-2023-12-1-g07', 'PASSAGE', '[17:05.12]Passage Two｜短文二\n[17:08.39]For college students under pressure, a dog may be the best stress fighter around, according to the finding of a recent study.｜根据最近的一项研究发现，对于承受压力的大学生来说，狗可能是最好的压力斗士。\n[17:16.85]“It''s a really powerful finding,” said Patricia Pendry, who led the study.｜这项研究的负责人帕特丽夏·彭德里说：“这是一个非常有用的发现。”\n[17:22.05]“Universities are doing a lot of great work trying to help students succeed academically, especially those who may be at risk due to a history of mental health issues or academic and learning issues.“｜大学正在做很多工作，试图帮助学生在学业上取得成功，尤其是那些可能因心理健康问题、学术和学习问题而面临风险的学生。\n[17:35.99]This study shows that traditional stress management approaches aren''t as effective for this population compared with programs that focus on providing opportunities to interact with therapy dogs.”｜这项研究表明，与专注于提供与治疗犬互动机会的项目相比，传统的压力管理方法对这一人群并不同样有效。”\n[17:49.46]The researchers measured executive functioning in the students involved in the study.｜研究人员测量了参与研究的学生的执行能力。\n[17:55.32]Executive function is a term for the skills one needs to plan, organize, motivate, concentrate, and memorize.｜执行能力是指一个人需要计划、组织、激励、集中注意力和记忆的技能。\n[18:04.01]These are skills students need to succeed in college.｜这些都是学生在大学取得成功所需要的技能。\n[18:08.12]The researchers found that students who were most at risk had the most improvements in executive functioning after interacting with therapy dogs.｜研究人员发现，风险最大的学生在与治疗犬互动后，执行功能改善最多。\n[18:18.69]These results remained when researchers followed up six weeks later.｜当研究人员在六周后进行随访时，这些结果仍然如此。\n[18:24.08]Many universities provide academic stress management programs and workshops.｜许多大学提供学业压力管理课程和研讨会。\n[18:30.16]These are traditionally very similar to college classes.｜这些课程与传统上的大学课程非常相似。\n[18:33.67]They often talk about ways to get more sleep, set goals, or manage stress or anxiety.｜他们经常谈论如何获得更多的睡眠、设定目标或管理压力或焦虑。\n[18:40.77]Pendry acknowledges that these are really important topics, and these workshops help typical students succeed, but they''re less helpful for struggling students.｜彭德里承认，这些都是非常重要的话题，这些研讨会帮助一般的学生取得成功，但对陷入困境的学生帮助不大。\n[18:51.71]Pendry believes those students may experience the programs as another lecture and feel even more stressed.｜彭德里认为，这些学生可能会将这些课程作为另一场讲座来体验，并感到压力更大。\n[18:58.96]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的短文回答第19至21题。\n[19:04.73]19. What does the recent study led by Patricia Pendry aim to examine?｜19. 由帕特丽夏·彭德里领导的最近这项研究旨在验证什么？\n[19:24.76]20. What did the new study measure in the participants?｜20. 这项新研究对参与者测量了什么？\n[19:45.59]21. What does Patricia Pendry think traditional stress management programs may do?｜21. 帕特丽夏·彭德里认为传统的压力管理课程怎么样？', NOW(), NOW()
