  (`question_id`, `audio_url`, `transcript`, `duration_seconds`, `audio_total_seconds`, `audio_start_seconds`, `audio_end_seconds`, `question_start_seconds`, `question_end_seconds`, `answer_start_seconds`, `answer_end_seconds`, `group_key`, `listening_type`, `lrc_content`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
SELECT
  @a_cet4_2023_12_2_21, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2023-12-2.mp3', 'Passage Two\n短文二\n\nNew research has studied the effects of a seven-week healthy cooking course.\n一项新的研究对为期7周的健康烹饪课程的效果进行了研究\n\nAcademics measured the program''s effect on cooking confidence and self-perceived mental health.\n学术界测量了该项目对烹饪自信和自我感知心理健康的影响。\n\nResearchers also measured participants'' overall satisfaction around cooking and diet-related behaviors.\n研究人员还测量了参与者对烹饪和饮食相关行为的总体满意度。\n\nWhat were the findings? Course participants saw significant improvements in general health.\n结果是什么？课程参与者在总体健康状况上显著改善。\n\nThey also reported improved mental health and subjective vitality immediately after the program.\n他们还报告说，项目结束后心理健康和主观活力立即得到改善。\n\nThese benefits remained six months after the completion of the course.\n这些益处在课程结束6个月后仍然存在。\n\nWhat caused these improvements?\n是什么导致了这些改进？\n\nResearchers have previously found the link between eating more fruits and vegetables, and improved longer term mental health.\n研究人员此前发现，多吃水果和蔬菜与改善长期心理健康之间存在联系。\n\nThis would imply that the participants in the current study may have felt better due to improved diet.\n这意味着当前研究的参与者可能因为饮食的改善而感觉更好。\n\nHowever, the study showed participants'' mental health improved even if their reported diets did not change after completing the program.\n然而，研究表明，即使参与者报告的饮食在完成该项目后没有改变，他们的心理健康状况也有所改善。\n\nAlso, the mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or obese, and those in a healthy weight range.\n此外，超重或肥胖的参与者与健康体重范围内的参与者的心理健康益处是相等的。\n\nThis suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around cooking, and mental health benefits.\n这表明烹饪自信、烹饪满意度和心理健康益处之间存在联系。\n\nWho benefits most from learning to cook? Gender plays a part.\n谁从学习烹饪中受益最大？性别起了一定作用。\n\nAt the start of the program, 77% of female participants were confident about cooking, but just 23% of males were confident.\n在该项目开始时，77%的女性参与者对烹饪有自信，但只有23%的男性有自信。\n\nAt the end of the program, cooking confidence and skills were equal across both counterparts.\n在项目结束时，他们的烹饪自信和技能相当。\n\nThis change in confidence could lead to a gender balance in home cooking.\n这种自信的改变可能会导致家庭烹饪中的性别平衡。\n\nThis, in turn, could reduce consumption of unhealthy, high calorie processed meals.\n这反过来可以减少不健康、高热量加工食品的消费。\n\nQuestions 19 to 21 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的短文回答第19至21题。\n\n19. What do we learn about the benefits the participants gained from the healthy cooking course?\n19. 关于参与者从健康烹饪课程中获得的益处，我们知道什么？\n\n20. What do the new research findings suggest about mental health benefits?\n20. 关于心理健康的益处，新的研究结果表明什么？\n\n21. What plays a role in determining who benefits most from learning to cook?\n21. 是什么决定了谁从学习烹饪中受益最大？', 119, 1415.93, 1013.30, 1132.50, 1179.57, 1200.79, NULL, NULL, 'cet4-2023-12-2-g07', 'PASSAGE', '[16:53.30]Passage Two｜短文二\n[16:56.65]New research has studied the effects of a seven-week healthy cooking course.｜一项新的研究对为期7周的健康烹饪课程的效果进行了研究\n[17:02.16]Academics measured the program''s effect on cooking confidence and self-perceived mental health.｜学术界测量了该项目对烹饪自信和自我感知心理健康的影响。\n[17:08.59]Researchers also measured participants'' overall satisfaction around cooking and diet-related behaviors.｜研究人员还测量了参与者对烹饪和饮食相关行为的总体满意度。\n[17:15.98]What were the findings? Course participants saw significant improvements in general health.｜结果是什么？课程参与者在总体健康状况上显著改善。\n[17:22.52]They also reported improved mental health and subjective vitality immediately after the program.｜他们还报告说，项目结束后心理健康和主观活力立即得到改善。\n[17:29.23]These benefits remained six months after the completion of the course.｜这些益处在课程结束6个月后仍然存在。\n[17:34.03]What caused these improvements?｜是什么导致了这些改进？\n[17:36.59]Researchers have previously found the link between eating more fruits and vegetables, and improved longer term mental health.｜研究人员此前发现，多吃水果和蔬菜与改善长期心理健康之间存在联系。\n[17:44.28]This would imply that the participants in the current study may have felt better due to improved diet.｜这意味着当前研究的参与者可能因为饮食的改善而感觉更好。\n[17:51.19]However, the study showed participants'' mental health improved even if their reported diets did not change after completing the program.｜然而，研究表明，即使参与者报告的饮食在完成该项目后没有改变，他们的心理健康状况也有所改善。\n[18:00.35]Also, the mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or obese, and those in a healthy weight range.｜此外，超重或肥胖的参与者与健康体重范围内的参与者的心理健康益处是相等的。\n[18:09.68]This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around cooking, and mental health benefits.｜这表明烹饪自信、烹饪满意度和心理健康益处之间存在联系。\n[18:17.26]Who benefits most from learning to cook? Gender plays a part.｜谁从学习烹饪中受益最大？性别起了一定作用。\n[18:22.36]At the start of the program, 77% of female participants were confident about cooking, but just 23% of males were confident.｜在该项目开始时，77%的女性参与者对烹饪有自信，但只有23%的男性有自信。\n[18:33.12]At the end of the program, cooking confidence and skills were equal across both counterparts.｜在项目结束时，他们的烹饪自信和技能相当。\n[18:39.77]This change in confidence could lead to a gender balance in home cooking.｜这种自信的改变可能会导致家庭烹饪中的性别平衡。\n[18:45.76]This, in turn, could reduce consumption of unhealthy, high calorie processed meals.｜这反过来可以减少不健康、高热量加工食品的消费。\n[18:52.50]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的短文回答第19至21题。\n[18:57.44]19. What do we learn about the benefits the participants gained from the healthy cooking course?｜19. 关于参与者从健康烹饪课程中获得的益处，我们知道什么？\n[19:18.14]20. What do the new research findings suggest about mental health benefits?｜20. 关于心理健康的益处，新的研究结果表明什么？\n[19:39.57]21. What plays a role in determining who benefits most from learning to cook?｜21. 是什么决定了谁从学习烹饪中受益最大？', NOW(), NOW()
