  @a_cet4_2023_12_2_25, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2023-12-2.mp3', 'Passage Three\n短文三\n\nWhat is personal space? We often think of it as an invisible bubble of space surrounding us that others can''t enter without causing discomfort.\n什么是个人空间？我们经常认为它是我们周围一个无形的空间气泡，其他人无法进入，从而不会引起不适。\n\nResearch shows, however, that we actually have bubbles of different sizes.\n然而，研究表明，我们实际上有不同大小的气泡。\n\nEach of these bubbles applies to a different set of people.\n其中的每一个气泡都适用于不同的人群。\n\nThe smallest zone, called intimate space, extends outward from our bodies 18 inches in every direction.\n最小的区域，称为亲密空间，从我们的身体向各个方向向外延伸18英寸。\n\nOnly family, pets and one''s closest friends may enter it.\n只有家人、宠物和最亲密的朋友才能进入。\n\nA mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes us uncomfortable.\n一个泛泛之交进入我们的亲密空间就会让我们感到不舒服。\n\nNext is the bubble, called personal space, extending from 1.5 feet to 4 feet away.\n其次的气泡称为个人空间，从1.5英尺延伸到4英尺远。\n\nFriends and acquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone, especially during informal conversations. But strangers are forbidden.\n朋友和熟人可以舒适地占据这个区域，尤其是在非正式交谈中。但陌生人是被禁止的。\n\nExtending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is social space.\n从4英尺到12英尺的距离是社交空间。\n\nHere people feel comfortable conducting routine social interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers. Those are the average sizes of Americans'' personal bubbles, anyway.\n在这里，人们可以放心地与新认识的人或完全陌生的人进行日常社交互动。这是美国人空间气泡的平均大小。\n\nIt is important to keep in mind that personal space varies depending on culture and context.\n要记住，个人空间因文化和环境而异。\n\nFurthermore, there are significant individual differences.\n此外，还存在显著的个体差异。\n\nAs we all know, cultural or individual differences in personal bubble diameters are all too often the cause of discomfort.\n众所周知，个人气泡直径内的文化或个人差异往往是造成不适的原因。\n\nHow did these personal bubbles arise?\n这些个人气泡是如何产生的？\n\nAccording to research, we begin to develop our individual sense of personal space around age 3 or\n4. 根据研究，我们在三四岁左右开始发展个人空间感。\n\nThe sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens.\n我们的气泡大小是在青少年时期决定的。\n\nThese bubbles are constructed and monitored by the brain region involved in fear.\n这些气泡是由大脑中与恐惧有关的区域构建和监测的。\n\nQuestions 22 to 25 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的短文回答第22至25题。\n\n22. What does research show about our personal space?\n22. 关于我们的个人空间，研究显示什么？\n\n23. What happens if a mere acquaintance enters our intimate space?\n23. 如果一个泛泛之交进入我们的亲密空间，会发生什么？\n\n24. Where do people feel comfortable interacting with new acquaintances or strangers?\n24. 人们在与新认识的人或陌生人互动时，在哪个空间会感觉舒服？\n\n25. When are the sizes of our bubbles fixed?\n25. 我们的社交气泡大小是什么时候确定的？', 124, 1415.93, 1201.09, 1325.29, 1394.19, 1406.19, NULL, NULL, 'cet4-2023-12-2-g08', 'PASSAGE', '[20:01.09]Passage Three｜短文三\n[20:04.42]What is personal space? We often think of it as an invisible bubble of space surrounding us that others can''t enter without causing discomfort.｜什么是个人空间？我们经常认为它是我们周围一个无形的空间气泡，其他人无法进入，从而不会引起不适。\n[20:16.20]Research shows, however, that we actually have bubbles of different sizes.｜然而，研究表明，我们实际上有不同大小的气泡。\n[20:21.19]Each of these bubbles applies to a different set of people.｜其中的每一个气泡都适用于不同的人群。\n[20:25.15]The smallest zone, called intimate space, extends outward from our bodies 18 inches in every direction.｜最小的区域，称为亲密空间，从我们的身体向各个方向向外延伸18英寸。\n[20:34.51]Only family, pets and one''s closest friends may enter it.｜只有家人、宠物和最亲密的朋友才能进入。\n[20:40.10]A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes us uncomfortable.｜一个泛泛之交进入我们的亲密空间就会让我们感到不舒服。\n[20:45.78]Next is the bubble, called personal space, extending from 1.5 feet to 4 feet away.｜其次的气泡称为个人空间，从1.5英尺延伸到4英尺远。\n[20:53.64]Friends and acquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone, especially during informal conversations. But strangers are forbidden.｜朋友和熟人可以舒适地占据这个区域，尤其是在非正式交谈中。但陌生人是被禁止的。\n[21:04.80]Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is social space.｜从4英尺到12英尺的距离是社交空间。\n[21:09.72]Here people feel comfortable conducting routine social interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers. Those are the average sizes of Americans'' personal bubbles, anyway.｜在这里，人们可以放心地与新认识的人或完全陌生的人进行日常社交互动。这是美国人空间气泡的平均大小。\n[21:23.43]It is important to keep in mind that personal space varies depending on culture and context.｜要记住，个人空间因文化和环境而异。\n[21:30.53]Furthermore, there are significant individual differences.｜此外，还存在显著的个体差异。\n[21:34.62]As we all know, cultural or individual differences in personal bubble diameters are all too often the cause of discomfort.｜众所周知，个人气泡直径内的文化或个人差异往往是造成不适的原因。\n[21:43.90]How did these personal bubbles arise?｜这些个人气泡是如何产生的？\n[21:47.06]According to research, we begin to develop our individual sense of personal space around age 3 or｜4. 根据研究，我们在三四岁左右开始发展个人空间感。\n[21:54.90]The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens.｜我们的气泡大小是在青少年时期决定的。\n[21:58.86]These bubbles are constructed and monitored by the brain region involved in fear.｜这些气泡是由大脑中与恐惧有关的区域构建和监测的。\n[22:05.29]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的短文回答第22至25题。\n[22:10.97]22. What does research show about our personal space?｜22. 关于我们的个人空间，研究显示什么？\n[22:29.25]23. What happens if a mere acquaintance enters our intimate space?｜23. 如果一个泛泛之交进入我们的亲密空间，会发生什么？\n[22:50.48]24. Where do people feel comfortable interacting with new acquaintances or strangers?｜24. 人们在与新认识的人或陌生人互动时，在哪个空间会感觉舒服？\n[23:14.19]25. When are the sizes of our bubbles fixed?｜25. 我们的社交气泡大小是什么时候确定的？', NOW(), NOW()
