SELECT
  @a_cet4_2024_06_1_25, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2024-06-1.mp3', 'Passage Three\n短文三\n\nIn a new Merrill Lynch Age Wave survey, a full 70% of the early adults said they''ve received financial support from their parents in the past year, and 58% said they couldn''t afford their current lifestyles without it.\n在美林集团和年龄浪潮合作的一项新调查中，整整70%的年轻人表示，他们在过去一年中得到了父母的经济支持，58%的人表示，如果没有父母的经济支持，他们负担不起现在的生活方式。\n\nThe most common types of financial support include cellphone plans, food, school costs, and car expenses.\n最常见的经济支持包括手机套餐、食物花销、学费和汽车费用。\n\nParental financial support of early adults, said Ken Dychtwald, CEO of Age Wave, is “the new normal”.\n年龄浪潮的首席执行官肯·戴奇沃迪说，父母对年轻人的经济支持是“新常态”。\n\nBut 64% of the young adults surveyed said parents'' financial support to children aged 25 to 34 is a bad thing, because it makes those kids dependent.\n但64%的受访年轻人表示，父母给25岁至34岁的子女提供经济支持是一件坏事，因为这会让那些子女产生依赖性。\n\nBy contrast, only 29% thought supporting men and women aged 18 to 24 is bad.\n相比之下，只有29%的人认为给18岁至24岁的子女提供经济支持是不好的。\n\nThe remaining 71% thought that assistance helps the adult children get ahead.\n其余71%的人认为这种经济上的帮助有助于成年子女取得成功。\n\nDychtwald believes the young women and men surveyed were saying that by 25, young adults ought to be financially independent.\n戴奇沃迪表示，接受调查的年轻男女认为，到25岁时，年轻人应该在经济上独立。\n\nIn fact, the respondents said, financial independence defines adulthood.\n事实上，受访者表示，经济独立标志着成年。\n\n“Financial independence is something they were struggling with and challenged by. And it scared them a bit,” Dychtwald said.\n戴奇沃迪说道：“经济独立是他们一直在努力应对的挑战，这让他们有点害怕。”\n\nOne big reason they''re struggling is attributed to college loans, of which the average amounts to $37,000.\n他们苦苦挣扎的一个重要原因是大学贷款，平均贷款金额达到37,000美元。\n\nMany of the parents have taken on college loans for the kids, too, sometimes at the expense of their own finances.\n许多父母也为子女承担了大学贷款，这有时是以损害自身的财务状况为代价的。\n\nIn the survey, 60% of early adults define financial success as being debt-free.\n在这项调查中，60%的年轻人将经济成功定义为无债务。\n\nWhether that''s likely, or even possible, anytime soon is anyone''s guess.\n至于这是否能很快实现，甚至是否有可能实现，谁也说不准。\n\nQuestions 22 to 25 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的短文回答第22至25题。\n\n22. What do we learn from a new survey by Merrill Lynch Age Wave?\n22. 我们从美林集团和年龄浪潮合作的一项新调查中了解到了什么？\n\n23. Why did most young adults in the survey say financial support to children aged 25 to 34 is a bad thing?\n23. 为什么受访的大多数年轻人表示对25岁至34岁的子女提供经济支持是一件坏事？\n\n24. What did the respondents in the survey say regarding financial independence?\n24. 调查中的受访者对经济独立有何看法？\n\n25. What is one big reason young adults are struggling?\n25. 年轻人苦苦挣扎的一个重要原因是什么？', 119, 1409.83, 1191.82, 1310.95, 1385.06, 1397.06, 1280.87, 1289.60, 'cet4-2024-06-1-g08', 'PASSAGE', '[19:51.82]Passage Three｜短文三\n[19:54.26]In a new Merrill Lynch Age Wave survey, a full 70% of the early adults said they''ve received financial support from their parents in the past year, and 58% said they couldn''t afford their current lifestyles without it.｜在美林集团和年龄浪潮合作的一项新调查中，整整70%的年轻人表示，他们在过去一年中得到了父母的经济支持，58%的人表示，如果没有父母的经济支持，他们负担不起现在的生活方式。\n[20:10.38]The most common types of financial support include cellphone plans, food, school costs, and car expenses.｜最常见的经济支持包括手机套餐、食物花销、学费和汽车费用。\n[20:20.69]Parental financial support of early adults, said Ken Dychtwald, CEO of Age Wave, is “the new normal”.｜年龄浪潮的首席执行官肯·戴奇沃迪说，父母对年轻人的经济支持是“新常态”。\n[20:29.61]But 64% of the young adults surveyed said parents'' financial support to children aged 25 to 34 is a bad thing, because it makes those kids dependent.｜但64%的受访年轻人表示，父母给25岁至34岁的子女提供经济支持是一件坏事，因为这会让那些子女产生依赖性。\n[20:42.70]By contrast, only 29% thought supporting men and women aged 18 to 24 is bad.｜相比之下，只有29%的人认为给18岁至24岁的子女提供经济支持是不好的。\n[20:49.94]The remaining 71% thought that assistance helps the adult children get ahead.｜其余71%的人认为这种经济上的帮助有助于成年子女取得成功。\n[20:56.26]Dychtwald believes the young women and men surveyed were saying that by 25, young adults ought to be financially independent.｜戴奇沃迪表示，接受调查的年轻男女认为，到25岁时，年轻人应该在经济上独立。\n[21:06.99]In fact, the respondents said, financial independence defines adulthood.｜事实上，受访者表示，经济独立标志着成年。\n[21:12.58]“Financial independence is something they were struggling with and challenged by. And it scared them a bit,” Dychtwald said.｜戴奇沃迪说道：“经济独立是他们一直在努力应对的挑战，这让他们有点害怕。”\n[21:20.87]One big reason they''re struggling is attributed to college loans, of which the average amounts to $37,000.｜他们苦苦挣扎的一个重要原因是大学贷款，平均贷款金额达到37,000美元。\n[21:29.90]Many of the parents have taken on college loans for the kids, too, sometimes at the expense of their own finances.｜许多父母也为子女承担了大学贷款，这有时是以损害自身的财务状况为代价的。\n[21:37.81]In the survey, 60% of early adults define financial success as being debt-free.｜在这项调查中，60%的年轻人将经济成功定义为无债务。\n[21:45.46]Whether that''s likely, or even possible, anytime soon is anyone''s guess.｜至于这是否能很快实现，甚至是否有可能实现，谁也说不准。\n[21:50.95]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的短文回答第22至25题。\n[21:57.78]22. What do we learn from a new survey by Merrill Lynch Age Wave?｜22. 我们从美林集团和年龄浪潮合作的一项新调查中了解到了什么？\n[22:19.17]23. Why did most young adults in the survey say financial support to children aged 25 to 34 is a bad thing?｜23. 为什么受访的大多数年轻人表示对25岁至34岁的子女提供经济支持是一件坏事？\n[22:44.16]24. What did the respondents in the survey say regarding financial independence?｜24. 调查中的受访者对经济独立有何看法？\n[23:05.06]25. What is one big reason young adults are struggling?｜25. 年轻人苦苦挣扎的一个重要原因是什么？', NOW(), NOW()
WHERE @a_cet4_2024_06_1_25 IS NOT NULL
