nd placed in a supposedly secure area.｜经过六天的全力搜寻，这条毒蛇最终被找到，并被安置在一个据称安全的区域。\n[02:54.03]But it seems the animal is no fan of the zoo, because yet again he is out on the loose.｜但这条蛇似乎并不喜欢动物园，因为它又一次逃脱了。\n[03:01.56]The snake is a relative newcomer to the zoo but has already been frustrating its staff.｜这条蛇虽是动物园的新成员，但它已经让工作人员感到沮丧。\n[03:08.26]The staff believe he squeezed himself out of a gap located around new energy-saving bulbs installed inside the snake house.｜工作人员认为，它是从蛇屋里新安装的节能灯泡周围的缝隙中挤出来的。\n[03:18.10]He was only found the first time around because staff brought in a special machine to trace him inside a wall opening.｜它第一次被找回是因为工作人员带来了一台特殊的机器，在一个墙洞里追踪到它的位置。\n[03:27.20]This kind of snake is one of the most deadly and poisonous in the wild, and they can range from three to five metres in length.｜这种蛇是野外最致命、毒性最强的蛇之一，体长可达三至五米。\n[03:36.52]Questions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的新闻报道回答第3题和第4题。\n[03:42.14]3. What do we learn about the deadly snake from the news report?｜3. 从新闻报道中我们对这条致命的蛇有何了解？\n[04:00.73]4. How have the zoo staff been feeling about the snake?｜4. 动物园工作人员对这条蛇有什么样的感受？', NOW(), NOW()
