SELECT
  @a_cet4_2024_06_2_14, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2024-06-2.mp3', 'Conversation Two\n对话二\n\nW: Welcome to Books in Review.\n女：欢迎收看《书评》。\n\nOur guest today is John Banks, the author of the bestselling new book, Rewarding Success.\n我们今天的嘉宾是约翰·班克斯，他是畅销新书《奖励成功》的作者。\n\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\n男：很高兴来到这里，简。\n\nW: Your book has sold 2,000,000 copies.\n女: 你的书已经卖出了200万册。\n\nBut before we discuss why it''s getting so much attention, let''s talk about your background. You''re an economist and spent two decades teaching at universities?\n但在讨论它为什么受到如此多的关注之前，我们先来聊聊你的背景。你是经济学家，在大学任教20年？\n\nM: I spent 25 years as a professor actually, and then, for the last 10 years, I''ve worked as a political consultant, advising politicians at the national level about problems in our country.\n男：事实上，我当了25年的教授，在过去的10年里，我一直担任政治顾问，就我国的问题为国家层面的政治家提供建议。\n\nW: You discuss three of those problems in the book, improving public education, reducing our nation''s health care burden, and increasing personal savings.\n女：你在书中讨论了其中的三个问题：改善公共教育、减轻国家的医疗负担和增加个人储蓄。\n\nBut your ideas about education are the most controversial.\n但你对教育的看法是最具争议的。\n\nM: Absolutely. A lot of people think I''m trying to punish students who aren''t doing well, when actually my goal is to give all students more incentive to succeed.\n男：的确如此。很多人认为我是在惩罚那些表现不好的学生，而实际上我的目的是让所有学生有更多的动力去取得成功。\n\nW: I''m not sure I agree with you.\n女：我不太同意你的观点。\n\nYour proposal to pay cash rewards to students who get good grades is a particular problem.\n你提议向成绩好的学生给予现金奖励，这是一个特殊的问题。\n\nWhat about students who don''t get good grades?\n那些成绩不好的学生怎么办？\n\nIt seems like you''re blaming them for not succeeding, when poor performance in school isn''t a child''s fault.\n你似乎在责怪他们没有成功，但学习成绩不佳并不是孩子的错。\n\nM: But my proposal is not just to reward students with good grades, but also students who show improvements.\n男：但我的建议并不只是要奖励成绩好的学生，还要奖励有进步的学生。\n\nW: Okay. Another criticism of the plan is the cash rewards themselves.\n女: 好吧，对该计划的另一项批评是现金奖励本身。\n\nWhere will the money come from?\n钱从哪里来？\n\nM: If students do better, we''ll spend less on schooling.\n男：如果学生表现得更好，我们在学校教育上的花费就会减少。\n\nSo in the end, the rewards will pay for themselves.\n所以到最后，奖励本身就能收回成本。\n\nW: What about now? How will we fund the rewards in the meantime?\n女：那现在呢？在此期间，我们如何为奖励提供资金？\n\nM: Well, by increasing taxes or moving money from other areas of the budget into education.\n男：通过增加税收或将其他领域的预算资金转移到教育领域。\n\nQuestions 12 to 15 are based on the conversation you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的对话回答第12至15题。\n\n12. What do we learn about the man?\n12. 关于男士，我们知道什么？\n\n13. What does the woman say is the most controversial?\n13. 女士说最具争议的是什么？\n\n14. What does the man say is his real goal?\n14. 男士说他真正的目的是什么？\n\n15. What will be one option for funding the proposed rewards according to the man?\n15. 根据该男士的说法，为提议的奖励提供资金的一种选择是什么？', 118, 1398.46, 632.72, 750.24, 794.34, 814.00, NULL, NULL, 'cet4-2024-06-2-g05', 'LONG_DIALOGUE', '[10:32.72]Conversation Two｜对话二\n[10:36.30]W: Welcome to Books in Review.｜女：欢迎收看《书评》。\n[10:39.21]Our guest today is John Banks, the author of the bestselling new book, Rewarding Success.｜我们今天的嘉宾是约翰·班克斯，他是畅销新书《奖励成功》的作者。\n[10:45.63]M: Glad to be here, Jane.｜男：很高兴来到这里，简。\n[10:46.83]W: Your book has sold 2,000,000 copies.｜女: 你的书已经卖出了200万册。\n[10:49.94]But before we discuss why it''s getting so much attention, let''s talk about your background. You''re an economist and spent two decades teaching at universities?｜但在讨论它为什么受到如此多的关注之前，我们先来聊聊你的背景。你是经济学家，在大学任教20年？\n[11:00.24]M: I spent 25 years as a professor actually, and then, for the last 10 years, I''ve worked as a political consultant, advising politicians at the national level about problems in our country.｜男：事实上，我当了25年的教授，在过去的10年里，我一直担任政治顾问，就我国的问题为国家层面的政治家提供建议。\n[11:13.17]W: You discuss three of those problems in the book, improving public education, reducing our nation''s health care burden, and increasing personal savings.｜女：你在书中讨论了其中的三个问题：改善公共教育、减轻国家的医疗负担和增加个人储蓄。\n[11:23.43]But your ideas about education are the most controversial.｜但你对教育的看法是最具争议的。\n[11:27.53]M: Absolutely. A lot of people think I''m trying to punish students who aren''t doing well, when actually my goal is to give all students more incentive to succeed.｜男：的确如此。很多人认为我是在惩罚那些表现不好的学生，而实际上我的目的是让所有学生有更多的动力去取得成功。\n[11:38.15]W: I''m not sure I agree with you.｜女：我不太同意你的观点。\n[11:40.27]Your proposal to pay cash rewards to students who get good grades is a particular problem.｜你提议向成绩好的学生给予现金奖励，这是一个特殊的问题。\n[11:46.44]What about students who don''t get good grades?｜那些成绩不好的学生怎么办？\n[11:49.52]It seems like you''re blaming them for not succeeding, when poor performance in school isn''t a child''s fault.｜你似乎在责怪他们没有成功，但学习成绩不佳并不是孩子的错。\n[11:56.68]M: But my proposal is not just to reward students with good grades, but also students who show improvements.｜男：但我的建议并不只是要奖励成绩好的学生，还要奖励有进步的学生。\n[12:04.56]W: Okay. Another criticism of the plan is the cash rewards themselves.｜女: 好吧，对该计划的另一项批评是现金奖励本身。\n[12:09.75]Where will the money come from?｜钱从哪里来？\n[12:11.56]M: If students do better, we''ll spend less on schooling.｜男：如果学生表现得更好，我们在学校教育上的花费就会减少。\n[12:15.63]So in the end, the rewards will pay for themselves.｜所以到最后，奖励本身就能收回成本。\n[12:18.97]W: What about now? How will we fund the rewards in the meantime?｜女：那现在呢？在此期间，我们如何为奖励提供资金？\n[12:23.06]M: Well, by increasing taxes or moving money from other areas of the budget into education.｜男：通过增加税收或将其他领域的预算资金转移到教育领域。\n[12:30.24]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the conversation you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的对话回答第12至15题。\n[12:36.53]12. What do we learn about the man?｜12. 关于男士，我们知道什么？\n[12:54.25]13. What does the woman say is the most controversial?｜13. 女士说最具争议的是什么？\n[13:14.34]14. What does the man say is his real goal?｜14. 男士说他真正的目的是什么？\n[13:34.30]15. What will be one option for funding the proposed rewards according to the man?｜15. 根据该男士的说法，为提议的奖励提供资金的一种选择是什么？', NOW(), NOW()
