SELECT
  @a_cet4_2025_06_1_08, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2025-06-1.mp3', 'Conversation One\n对话一\n\nM: Hey, Mary. You seem to be very much annoyed.\n男：嘿，玛丽，你看起来很生气。\n\nWhat happened?\n发生什么事了？\n\nW: Rush hour in this city is killing me.\n女：这座城市的交通高峰期简直要了我的命。\n\nM: Ah, yes. Rush hour is terrible, especially in the morning between 8:00 and 9:\n00. 男：啊，是啊。交通高峰期确实很糟糕，尤其是在早上8点到9点之间。\n\nBut what else can you expect in a city this big?\n但在这个大城市里，你还能期待什么呢？\n\nW: Well, I think the local government could help improve things.\n女：嗯，我认为当地政府可以帮忙改善状况。\n\nI mean, getting rid of rush hour may be impossible, but it could be made more tolerable.\n我的意思是，虽然不太可能摆脱交通高峰期，但可以让它变得更容易忍受一些。\n\nDon’t you think?\n你不觉得吗？\n\nM: Hmm, but I’m not sure how.\n男：嗯，但我不确定该怎么做。\n\nW: Well, for example, the subway system could have air conditioning.\n比如，地铁系统可以配备空调系统。\n\nI know many cities in the world have air conditioning in their subway.\n我知道世界上很多城市的地铁都有空调系统。\n\nSo why can’t we?\n那我们为什么不能呢？\n\nIt gets so hot in the summer.\n夏天地铁里热得要命。\n\nI can hardly breathe down there, and add to that the rush-hour crowds with strangers packed close together in the subway carriages.\n我在里面几乎都喘不过气来，再加上高峰期时地铁车厢里挤满了陌生人。\n\nThe whole thing is just horrible.\n整个情况简直太糟糕了。\n\nM: Ah, yes. You are completely right.\n男：啊，是啊。你说得太对了。\n\nThe trains here are too old.\n这里的地铁太旧了。\n\nThe government should definitely invest in new ones with air conditioning.\n政府确实应该投资购买一些安装了空调系统的新地铁。\n\nI guess I’m fortunate I take the bus instead.\n我想我坐公交车还算幸运。\n\nW: Oh, that’s much better.\n女：哦，那好多了。\n\nM: Yeah, it’s more convenient.\n男：是啊，坐公交车更方便。\n\nBus No. 36 goes straight from my house to the office. 36\n路公交车可以从我家直达办公室。\n\nIt’s a 30-minute ride, and I don’t have to make any changes.\n总共是30分钟的车程，不需要任何换乘。\n\nW: That sounds nice.\n女：听起来不错。\n\nI tell you my current commute is killing me.\n我跟你说，我现在的通勤快把我累死了。\n\nMaybe I should move closer to the office.\n也许我应该搬到离办公室更近的地方。\n\nM: Well, I know a great housing agent.\n男：嗯，我认识一位很棒的房产中介。\n\nI found the flat I am living in now through him, and I love it.\n我现在住的公寓就是通过他找到的，我很喜欢那里。\n\nW: Hmm, could you send me his number, please?\n女：嗯，你能把他的电话号码发给我吗？\n\nM: Sure thing. Just tell him exactly what you are looking for.\n男：当然可以。你只要告诉他具体想要什么样的房子就行了。\n\nAnd I’m sure he will find something good.\n我相信他会找到适合你的房子。\n\nQuestions 8 to 11 are based on the conversation you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的对话回答第8至11题。\n\n8. Why is the woman complaining?\n8. 女士为什么抱怨？\n\n9. What does the woman suggest doing?\n9. 女士建议做什么？\n\n10. What do we learn about the man from the conversation?\n10. 关于男士，我们可以从对话中了解到什么？\n\n11. What does the woman ask the man to do at the end of the conversation?\n11. 对话结束时，女士要求男士做什么？', 107, 1415.84, 462.62, 569.49, 574.76, 592.14, 469.16, 471.71, 'cet4-2025-06-1-g04', 'LONG_DIALOGUE', '[07:42.62]Conversation One｜对话一\n[07:44.69]M: Hey, Mary. You seem to be very much annoyed.｜男：嘿，玛丽，你看起来很生气。\n[07:47.96]What happened?｜发生什么事了？\n[07:49.16]W: Rush hour in this city is killing me.｜女：这座城市的交通高峰期简直要了我的命。\n[07:52.01]M: Ah, yes. Rush hour is terrible, especially in the morning between 8:00 and 9:｜00. 男：啊，是啊。交通高峰期确实很糟糕，尤其是在早上8点到9点之间。\n[07:57.91]But what else can you expect in a city this big?｜但在这个大城市里，你还能期待什么呢？\n[08:01.09]W: Well, I think the local government could help improve things.｜女：嗯，我认为当地政府可以帮忙改善状况。\n[08:04.88]I mean, getting rid of rush hour may be impossible, but it could be made more tolerable.｜我的意思是，虽然不太可能摆脱交通高峰期，但可以让它变得更容易忍受一些。\n[08:11.36]Don’t you think?｜你不觉得吗？\n[08:12.52]M: Hmm, but I’m not sure how.｜男：嗯，但我不确定该怎么做。\n[08:16.25]W: Well, for example, the subway system could have air conditioning.｜比如，地铁系统可以配备空调系统。\n[08:21.19]I know many cities in the world have air conditioning in their subway.｜我知道世界上很多城市的地铁都有空调系统。\n[08:25.63]So why can’t we?｜那我们为什么不能呢？\n[08:27.60]It gets so hot in the summer.｜夏天地铁里热得要命。\n[08:29.98]I can hardly breathe down there, and add to that the rush-hour crowds with strangers packed close together in the subway carriages.｜我在里面几乎都喘不过气来，再加上高峰期时地铁车厢里挤满了陌生人。\n[08:39.47]The whole thing is just horrible.｜整个情况简直太糟糕了。\n[08:42.15]M: Ah, yes. You are completely right.｜男：啊，是啊。你说得太对了。\n[08:45.13]The trains here are too old.｜这里的地铁太旧了。\n[08:47.17]The government should definitely invest in new ones with air conditioning.｜政府确实应该投资购买一些安装了空调系统的新地铁。\n[08:51.43]I guess I’m fortunate I take the bus instead.｜我想我坐公交车还算幸运。\n[08:54.56]W: Oh, that’s much better.｜女：哦，那好多了。\n[08:56.23]M: Yeah, it’s more convenient.｜男：是啊，坐公交车更方便。\n[08:58.42]Bus No. 36 goes straight from my house to the office. 36｜路公交车可以从我家直达办公室。\n[09:02.41]It’s a 30-minute ride, and I don’t have to make any changes.｜总共是30分钟的车程，不需要任何换乘。\n[09:06.07]W: That sounds nice.｜女：听起来不错。\n[09:07.64]I tell you my current commute is killing me.｜我跟你说，我现在的通勤快把我累死了。\n[09:11.22]Maybe I should move closer to the office.｜也许我应该搬到离办公室更近的地方。\n[09:13.97]M: Well, I know a great housing agent.｜男：嗯，我认识一位很棒的房产中介。\n[09:16.78]I found the flat I am living in now through him, and I love it.｜我现在住的公寓就是通过他找到的，我很喜欢那里。\n[09:20.42]W: Hmm, could you send me his number, please?｜女：嗯，你能把他的电话号码发给我吗？\n[09:23.25]M: Sure thing. Just tell him exactly what you are looking for.｜男：当然可以。你只要告诉他具体想要什么样的房子就行了。\n[09:26.94]And I’m sure he will find something good.｜我相信他会找到适合你的房子。\n[09:29.49]Questions 8 to 11 are based on the conversation you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的对话回答第8至11题。\n[09:34.76]8. Why is the woman complaining?｜8. 女士为什么抱怨？\n[09:52.44]9. What does the woman suggest doing?｜9. 女士建议做什么？\n[10:10.49]10. What do we learn about the man from the conversation?｜10. 关于男士，我们可以从对话中了解到什么？\n[10:29.62]11. What does the woman ask the man to do at the end of the conversation?｜11. 对话结束时，女士要求男士做什么？', NOW(), NOW()
