SELECT
  @a_cet4_2025_06_1_25, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2025-06-1.mp3', 'Passage Three\n短文三\n\nHuman fascination with animals goes back as far as humans do.\n人类对动物的着迷可以追溯到人类存在的远古时期。\n\nOf the oldest cave paintings discovered, some are up to 40,000 years old, and there are more images of animals than humans.\n在已发现的最古老的洞穴壁画中，有些已有4万年之久，而且其中动物的图像比人类的图像还多。\n\nAt some point, humans began to capture and hold animals.\n在某个时期，人类开始捕捉并持有动物。\n\nThe first known collections were held by royalty and were not open to the public.\n已知最早的动物收藏由王室成员持有，且不对公众开放。\n\nIn Egypt, researchers have found buildings from around 3500 B.C. containing the remains of animals, including elephants, which were not native to Egypt, but life wasn’t easy for these animals.\n在埃及，研究人员发现了公元前3500年左右的建筑遗迹，其中存有动物的遗骸，包括并非埃及本土物种的大象。然而，这些动物的生活并不容易。\n\nThey probably had short lives, and the remains show evidence of severe injuries.\n它们可能寿命短暂，遗骸上还显示出严重受伤的痕迹。\n\nThe first public exhibit of animals may have been created by Egypt’s Queen Hatshepsut around 1480 B.C.\n最早的动物公开展览可能是在公元前1480年左右由埃及女王哈特谢普苏特创办的。\n\nResearchers think the zoo was started with animals brought home from a far-off land known as Punt.\n研究人员认为，这个动物园最初是从一个名为蓬特的遥远国度带回动物后开始建立的。\n\nIt’s unclear why the Queen built the zoo, but it might have been to show off her wealth and power.\n目前尚不清楚埃及女王为什么要建造动物园，但这可能是为了炫耀她的财富和权力。\n\nEarly zoos are found all over the world.\n世界各地都有早期动物园的踪迹。\n\nIn China, Emperor Wen-Wang is said to have built a Garden of Intelligence around 1060 B.C.\n在中国，据说周文王在公元前1060年左右建造了一个名为“灵囿”的园林。\n\nIt included deer, birds and many fish.\n其中饲养着鹿、鸟和许多鱼类。\n\nIn England, King Henry Ⅰ set up a collection of animals in about 1110 as a part of the royal estate.\n在英格兰，国王亨利一世于1110年左右建了一个动物园，作为王室财产的一部分。\n\nHis collection included tigers and lions.\n里面饲养了老虎和狮子。\n\nThis collection eventually moved to the Tower of London in 1235.\n这个动物园最终在1235年被移至伦敦塔。\n\nThe collection stayed at that location for 600 years.\n并在那里留存了六百年之久。\n\nQuestions 22 to 25 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的短文回答第22至25题。\n\n22. What can we see in the oldest cave paintings?\n22. 在最古老的洞穴壁画中，我们能看到什么?\n\n23. What do we learn about the first known collections of animals?\n23. 关于已知最早的动物收藏，我们了解到了什么?\n\n24. What might be the reason the Egyptian Queen created the zoo?\n24. 埃及女王创建动物园的原因可能是什么?\n\n25. What do we learn about King Henry Ⅰ’s collection of animals in England?\n25. 关于英格兰国王亨利一世的动物园，我们了解到了什么?', 107, 1415.84, 1215.67, 1323.16, 1390.90, 1402.90, 1301.68, 1309.39, 'cet4-2025-06-1-g08', 'PASSAGE', '[20:15.67]Passage Three｜短文三\n[20:17.37]Human fascination with animals goes back as far as humans do.｜人类对动物的着迷可以追溯到人类存在的远古时期。\n[20:22.44]Of the oldest cave paintings discovered, some are up to 40,000 years old, and there are more images of animals than humans.｜在已发现的最古老的洞穴壁画中，有些已有4万年之久，而且其中动物的图像比人类的图像还多。\n[20:32.42]At some point, humans began to capture and hold animals.｜在某个时期，人类开始捕捉并持有动物。\n[20:37.47]The first known collections were held by royalty and were not open to the public.｜已知最早的动物收藏由王室成员持有，且不对公众开放。\n[20:43.45]In Egypt, researchers have found buildings from around 3500 B.C. containing the remains of animals, including elephants, which were not native to Egypt, but life wasn’t easy for these animals.｜在埃及，研究人员发现了公元前3500年左右的建筑遗迹，其中存有动物的遗骸，包括并非埃及本土物种的大象。然而，这些动物的生活并不容易。\n[20:57.79]They probably had short lives, and the remains show evidence of severe injuries.｜它们可能寿命短暂，遗骸上还显示出严重受伤的痕迹。\n[21:03.82]The first public exhibit of animals may have been created by Egypt’s Queen Hatshepsut around 1480 B.C.｜最早的动物公开展览可能是在公元前1480年左右由埃及女王哈特谢普苏特创办的。\n[21:13.24]Researchers think the zoo was started with animals brought home from a far-off land known as Punt.｜研究人员认为，这个动物园最初是从一个名为蓬特的遥远国度带回动物后开始建立的。\n[21:19.50]It’s unclear why the Queen built the zoo, but it might have been to show off her wealth and power.｜目前尚不清楚埃及女王为什么要建造动物园，但这可能是为了炫耀她的财富和权力。\n[21:26.22]Early zoos are found all over the world.｜世界各地都有早期动物园的踪迹。\n[21:29.70]In China, Emperor Wen-Wang is said to have