ialised more with each other during public feeding times.｜他们发现，在公开投喂时间，大象之间的社交互动更多。\n[02:54.46]After public feeding times, they were more likely to search for food and less likely to be inactive.｜公开投喂时间过后，它们更可能去寻找食物，而不太会处于不活跃的状态。\n[03:01.60]In the presence of many visitors, elephants were less likely to use repetitive behaviours, which often indicate boredom.｜大量游客在场时，大象较少出现重复性行为，这类行为通常表明动物感到无聊。\n[03:09.38]Visitors had a similar effect on some other species too, including polar bears, certain jungle cats and black-tailed plain dogs.｜游客对一些其他物种也有类似的影响，包括北极熊、某些丛林猫科动物以及黑尾草原犬鼠。\n[03:20.12]Some animal species have been born and raised in zoos and so have likely become used to the presence of humans.｜有些动物物种在动物园里出生并长大，因此很可能已经习惯了人类的存在。\n[03:27.60]Zoo visitors are often aspects of zoo animals'' environment that animals cannot control and as such can be stressful, although some species appear to show good adaptability to the changing conditions of visitors.｜动物园游客往往是动物无法控制的环境因素，因此可能会给动物带来压力，不过有些物种似乎对游客带来的变化表现出了良好的适应能力。\n[03:43.32]Questions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的新闻报道回答第3题和第4题。\n[03:48.40]3. What did animal behaviour experts do, according to the news report?｜3. 根据新闻报道，动物行为专家做了什么？\n[04:09.92]4. Why have some animal species likely become used to the presence of humans?｜4. 为什么有些动物物种很可能已经习惯了人类的存在？', NOW(), NOW()
