INSERT INTO `listening_audio`
  (`question_id`, `audio_url`, `transcript`, `duration_seconds`, `audio_total_seconds`, `audio_start_seconds`, `audio_end_seconds`, `question_start_seconds`, `question_end_seconds`, `answer_start_seconds`, `answer_end_seconds`, `group_key`, `listening_type`, `lrc_content`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
SELECT
  @a_cet4_2026_06_2_16, '/audio/exams/listening-cet4-2026-06-2.mp3', 'Passage One\n短文一\n\nIf U.S. workers want to switch jobs, they''d better do it now.\n如果美国打工人想跳槽，最好现在就行动。\n\nLast year, job switchers earned far higher wage increases than those staying in their jobs, but the gap is now closing.\n去年，跳槽的人获得的薪资涨幅远高于留在原岗位的人，但如今这一差距正在缩小。\n\nA three-month moving average of annual wage increases from the Atlanta Federal Reserve Bank''s wage tracker shows that U.S. job switchers got the highest wage increase last July, when the measure touched 8.5%.\n在亚特兰大联邦储备银行的薪资增长追踪指标中，年度薪资涨幅的三个月移动平均值显示，去年7月美国跳槽的人薪资涨幅最高，当时该指标达到了8.5%。\n\nSince then, it has gone up and down and has been going steadily down since November.\n自那以后，这个数字就一直上下波动，并从11月起持续稳步下降。\n\nThe gap in wage gains between people who switched jobs and those who stayed in their jobs is now closing.\n跳槽的人与留在原岗位的人之间的薪资涨幅差距现在正在缩小。\n\nOn average, people who changed jobs have almost always gotten better pay raises than those who stayed.\n平均来看，跳槽的人几乎总能比留在原岗位的人获得更高的加薪。\n\nBut in the past, the difference was usually around 1 percentage point.\n不过在以前，两者差距通常约为1个百分点。\n\nIn July, though, the gap widened to 2.6 percentage points.\n然而在7月，这一差距扩大到了2.6个百分点。\n\nBut now wage growth is slowing down for everyone.\n但如今所有人的薪资增长都在放缓。\n\nAnd job switchers are getting smaller wage bumps in their new jobs.\n跳槽的人在新工作中获得的加薪幅度也在变小。\n\nAt the moment, those who change jobs get an average 7.3% raise every year, while those who stay get a 5.4% raise.\n目前跳槽的人平均每年加薪7.3%，而留在原岗位的人加薪5.4%。\n\nThat suggests the demand for workers has reached, or is at least nearing, its peak.\n这表明对劳动力的需求已经到达峰值，或至少接近峰值。\n\nThe labor market is likely cooling, because even a shortage of workers isn''t overcoming the historic imbalance between employers and employees in the U.S.\n劳动力市场可能正在降温，因为即便劳动力短缺也无法扭转美国雇主与雇员之间长期存在的不平衡。\n\nThe volume of income that workers take home as a proportion of what the U.S. economy produces is still falling.\n雇员实际到手收入在美国经济总产出中所占的比例仍在下滑。\n\nQuestions 16 to 18 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚听到的短文回答第16至18题。\n\n16. What does the passage say about the gap in wage gains between job switchers and those who stay in their jobs?\n16. 关于跳槽的人与留在原岗位的人之间的薪资涨幅差距，短文说了什么？\n\n17. What do we learn about wage growth now?\n17. 关于如今薪资增长的情况，我们了解到什么？\n\n18. What is the labor market likely doing, according to the passage?\n18. 根据短文，劳动力市场可能正在发生什么？', 115, 1387.37, 821.22, 935.90, 941.30, 961.30, NULL, NULL, 'cet4-2026-06-2-g06', 'PASSAGE', '[13:41.22]Passage One｜短文一\n[13:43.54]If U.S. workers want to switch jobs, they''d better do it now.｜如果美国打工人想跳槽，最好现在就行动。\n[13:48.06]Last year, job switchers earned far higher wage increases than those staying in their jobs, but the gap is now closing.｜去年，跳槽的人获得的薪资涨幅远高于留在原岗位的人，但如今这一差距正在缩小。\n[13:57.38]A three-month moving average of annual wage increases from the Atlanta Federal Reserve Bank''s wage tracker shows that U.S. job switchers got the highest wage increase last July, when the measure touched 8.5%.｜在亚特兰大联邦储备银行的薪资增长追踪指标中，年度薪资涨幅的三个月移动平均值显示，去年7月美国跳槽的人薪资涨幅最高，当时该指标达到了8.5%。\n[14:14.64]Since then, it has gone up and down and has been going steadily down since November.｜自那以后，这个数字就一直上下波动，并从11月起持续稳步下降。\n[14:21.80]The gap in wage gains between people who switched jobs and those who stayed in their jobs is now closing.｜跳槽的人与留在原岗位的人之间的薪资涨幅差距现在正在缩小。\n[14:29.16]On average, people who changed jobs have almost always gotten better pay raises than those who stayed.｜平均来看，跳槽的人几乎总能比留在原岗位的人获得更高的加薪。\n[14:37.08]But in the past, the difference was usually around 1 percentage point.｜不过在以前，两者差距通常约为1个百分点。\n[14:42.48]In July, though, the gap widened to 2.6 percentage points.｜然而在7月，这一差距扩大到了2.6个百分点。\n[14:48.28]But now wage growth is slowing down for everyone.｜但如今所有人的薪资增长都在放缓。\n[14:52.04]And job switchers are getting smaller wage bumps in their new jobs.｜跳槽的人在新工作中获得的加薪幅度也在变小。\n[14:56.88]At the moment, those who change jobs get an average 7.3% raise every year, while those who stay get a 5.4% raise.｜目前跳槽的人平均每年加薪7.3%，而留在原岗位的人加薪5.4%。\n[15:08.32]That suggests the demand for workers has reached, or is at least nearing, its peak.｜这表明对劳动力的需求已经到达峰值，或至少接近峰值。\n[15:15.38]The labor market is likely cooling, because even a shortage of workers isn''t overcoming the historic imbalance between employers and employees in the U.S.｜劳动力市场可能正在降温，因为即便劳动力短缺也无法扭转美国雇主与雇员之间长期存在的不平衡。\n[15:27.02]The volume of income that workers take home as a proportion of what the U.S. economy produces is still falling.｜雇员实际到手收入在美国经济总产出中所占的比例仍在下滑。\n[15:35.90]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚听到的短文回答第16至18题。\n[15:41.30]16. What does the passage say about the gap in wage gains between job switchers and those who stay in their jobs?｜16. 关于跳槽的人与留在原岗位的人之间的薪资涨幅差距，短文说了什么？\n[16:05.30]17. What do we learn about wage growth now?｜17. 关于如今薪资增长的情况，我们了解到什么？\n[16:25.50]18. What is the labor market likely doing, according to the passage?｜18. 根据短文，劳动力市场可能正在发生什么？', NOW(), NOW()
