SELECT
  @a_cet6_2022_06_1_13, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2022-06-1.mp3', 'Passage Two\n短文二\n\nA recent study overturned what we think we know about lying.\n最近的一项研究推翻了我们对撒谎的认识。\n\nMost of us have a theory about how to tell if someone is telling a lie.\n我们大多数人都有关于如何判断某人是否在说谎的理论。\n\nWe may develop that theory from observations of those people we know well and see regularly, but we tend to generalize what we gather from that unscientific daily research and make it a universal theory.\n我们可能会通过观察那些我们熟悉并经常看到的人来逐渐形成这一理论，但我们倾向于对那些由不怎么科学的日常研究中所收集到的东西进行概括，使之成为一个普遍的理论。\n\nSo we might imagine that liars have evasive eyes, or the opposite—they simply stare at you.\n所以，我们可能会想象说谎者的眼神是闪躲的，或者相反——他们只是盯着你看。\n\nOr perhaps it is more generally nervous behavior we associate with lies.\n或者，我们会将谎言与那些更为普遍的紧张行为联系在一起。\n\nWhatever the particular theory, it''s usually based on close observation of people we know.\n不管具体的理论是什么，它通常是以对我们认识的人的密切观察为基础的。\n\nAnd we get lots of practice.\n我们得到了大量的练习。\n\nOn average, we''re lied to some 200 times per day.\n平均来说，我们每天要面对200次左右的谎言。\n\nThese are mostly harmless lies, but lies nonetheless.\n这些谎言大多是无害的，但它们终归是谎言。\n\nBut there''s a problem with our theories, even though they''re based on all these observations.\n不过，尽管我们的理论是以所有这些观察为基础的，但它们还是有一个问题。\n\nThe average person—you and me—tested rigorously on how well we detect lies fails to do better than chance.\n如果对像你和我这样的普通人进行严格的辨别谎言的测试，我们做得不比凑巧得出的结果好多少。\n\nThat''s well established over many studies and lots of attempts by researchers to work out reliable ways to detect lies.\n许多为找出可靠的谎言检测方法所做的研究和为此进行了大量尝试的研究人员都可以充分证实这一结果。\n\nIt''s even relatively easy to fool lie detectors, the gold standard of lie detection, by training yourself in breathing techniques and symptom suppression.\n通过在呼吸技巧和症状抑制方面的训练，欺骗堪称测谎黄金标准的测谎仪相对也挺容易的。\n\nIs there any way to get better at detecting lies?\n有什么方法可以更好地发现谎言吗？\n\nThe new research offers some surprising advice. Stop looking and listen instead.\n一项新研究提供了一些令人惊讶的建议。不要使用视觉，而应该使用听觉。\n\nIt turns out that if we''re unable to see the face, but rather focus on the voice of the person in question, our accuracy rate improves considerably.\n事实证明，如果我们看不到人的面部，而是专注于去倾听那个人的声音，我们的准确率会大大提高。\n\nQuestions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的文章回答问题12到15。\n\n12. According to the passage, how do most people detect lying?\n12. 根据这篇短文，大多数人是如何辨别谎言的？\n\n13. What does the passage say about most lies?\n13. 关于大多数谎言，这篇短文说了什么？\n\n14. What have many studies uncovered about the average person''s lie detection?\n14. 在很多研究中，关于普通人对谎言的辨别都有什么发现？\n\n15. What advice does the new research offer regarding lie detection?\n15. 关于谎言辨别，新研究给出了什么建议？\n\nSection C\nC节', 122, 1708.83, 711.10, 833.56, 859.26, 878.81, NULL, NULL, 'cet6-2022-06-1-g04', 'PASSAGE', '[11:51.10]Passage Two｜短文二\n[11:53.15]A recent study overturned what we think we know about lying.｜最近的一项研究推翻了我们对撒谎的认识。\n[11:58.79]Most of us have a theory about how to tell if someone is telling a lie.｜我们大多数人都有关于如何判断某人是否在说谎的理论。\n[12:04.10]We may develop that theory from observations of those people we know well and see regularly, but we tend to generalize what we gather from that unscientific daily research and make it a universal theory.｜我们可能会通过观察那些我们熟悉并经常看到的人来逐渐形成这一理论，但我们倾向于对那些由不怎么科学的日常研究中所收集到的东西进行概括，使之成为一个普遍的理论。\n[12:20.27]So we might imagine that liars have evasive eyes, or the opposite—they simply stare at you.｜所以，我们可能会想象说谎者的眼神是闪躲的，或者相反——他们只是盯着你看。\n[12:28.12]Or perhaps it is more generally nervous behavior we associate with lies.｜或者，我们会将谎言与那些更为普遍的紧张行为联系在一起。\n[12:33.75]Whatever the particular theory, it''s usually based on close observation of people we know.｜不管具体的理论是什么，它通常是以对我们认识的人的密切观察为基础的。\n[12:40.34]And we get lots of practice.｜我们得到了大量的练习。\n[12:43.23]On average, we''re lied to some 200 times per day.｜平均来说，我们每天要面对200次左右的谎言。\n[12:47.78]These are mostly harmless lies, but lies nonetheless.｜这些谎言大多是无害的，但它们终归是谎言。\n[12:52.47]But there''s a problem with our theories, even though they''re based on all these observations.｜不过，尽管我们的理论是以所有这些观察为基础的，但它们还是有一个问题。\n[12:58.08]The average person—you and me—tested rigorously on how well we detect lies fails to do better than chance.｜如果对像你和我这样的普通人进行严格的辨别谎言的测试，我们做得不比凑巧得出的结果好多少。\n[13:07.93]That''s well established over many studies and lots of attempts by researchers to work out reliable ways to detect lies.｜许多为找出可靠的谎言检测方法所做的研究和为此进行了大量尝试的研究人员都可以充分证实这一结果。\n[13:18.00]It''s even relatively easy to fool lie detectors, the gold standard of lie detection, by training yourself in breathing techniques and symptom suppression.｜通过在呼吸技巧和症状抑制方面的训练，欺骗堪称测谎黄金标准的测谎仪相对也挺容易的。\n[13:30.30]Is there any way to get better at detecting lies?｜有什么方法可以更好地发现谎言吗？\n[13:34.86]The new research offers some surprising advice. Stop looking and listen instead.｜一项新研究提供了一些令人惊讶的建议。不要使用视觉，而应该使用听觉。\n[13:41.57]It turns out that if we''re unable to see the face, but rather focus on the voice of the person in question, our accuracy rate improves considerably.｜事实证明，如果我们看不到人的面部，而是专注于去倾听那个人的声音，我们的准确率会大大提高。\n[13:53.56]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的文章回答问题12到15。\n[14:00.22]12. According to the passage, how do most people detect lying?｜12. 根据这篇短文，大多数人是如何辨别谎言的？\n[14:19.26]13. What does the passage say about most lies?｜13. 关于大多数谎言，这篇短文说了什么？\n[14:39.11]14. What have many studies uncovered about the average person''s lie detection?｜14. 在很多研究中，关于普通人对谎言的辨别都有什么发现？\n[15:00.30]15. What advice does the new research offer regarding lie detection?｜15. 关于谎言辨别，新研究给出了什么建议？\n[15:19.50]Section C｜C节', NOW(), NOW()
