hat if we''re unable to see the face, but rather focus on the voice of the person in question, our accuracy rate improves considerably.\n事实证明，如果我们看不到人的面部，而是专注于去倾听那个人的声音，我们的准确率会大大提高。\n\nQuestions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的文章回答问题12到15。\n\n12. According to the passage, how do most people detect lying?\n12. 根据这篇短文，大多数人是如何辨别谎言的？\n\n13. What does the passage say about most lies?\n13. 关于大多数谎言，这篇短文说了什么？\n\n14. What have many studies uncovered about the average person''s lie detection?\n14. 在很多研究中，关于普通人对谎言的辨别都有什么发现？\n\n15. What advice does the new research offer regarding lie detection?\n15. 关于谎言辨别，新研究给出了什么建议？\n\nSection C\nC节', 122, 1708.83, 711.10, 833.56, 879.11, 900.00, NULL, NULL, 'cet6-2022-06-1-g04', 'PASSAGE', '[11:51.10]Passage Two｜短文二\n[11:53.15]A recent study overturned what we think we know about lying.｜最近的一项研究推翻了我们对撒谎的认识。\n[11:58.79]Most of us have a theory about how to tell if someone is telling a lie.｜我们大多数人都有关于如何判断某人是否在说谎的理论。\n[12:04.10]We may develop that theory from observations of those people we know well and see regularly, but we tend to generalize what we gather from that unscientific daily research and make it a universal theory.｜我们可能会通过观察那些我们熟悉并经常看到的人来逐渐形成这一理论，但我们倾向于对那些由不怎么科学的日常研究中所收集到的东西进行概括，使之成为一个普遍的理论。\n[12:20.27]So we might imagine that liars have evasive eyes, or the opposite—they simply stare at you.｜所以，我们可能会想象说谎者的眼神是闪躲的，或者相反——他们只是盯着你看。\n[12:28.12]Or perhaps it is more generally nervous behavior we associate with lies.｜或者，我们会将谎言与那些更为普遍的紧张行为联系在一起。\n[12:33.75]Whatever the particular theory, it''s usually based on close observation of people we know.｜不管具体的理论是什么，它通常是以对我们认识的人的密切观察为基础的。\n[12:40.34]And we get lots of practice.｜我们得到了大量的练习。\n[12:43.23]On average, we''re lied to some 200 times per day.｜平均来说，我们每天要面对200次左右的谎言。\n[12:47.78]These are mostly harmless lies, but lies nonetheless.｜这些谎言大多是无害的，但它们终归是谎言。\n[12:52.47]But there''s a problem with our theories, even though they''re based on all these observations.｜不过