SELECT
  @a_cet6_2022_06_1_16, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2022-06-1.mp3', 'Recording One\n录音一\n\nAppear to be submissive, humble, grateful and undemanding; show great pleasure when a doctor comes into your room, even if the visit is brief and useless.\n当医生走进你的房间，即使逗留时间短暂且毫无用处，也要表现出顺从、谦逊、感激和随和，表现出极大的喜悦。\n\nDon''t challenge anyone with authority unless you are famous or very rich.\n除非你是名人或非常富有，否则，不要去挑战任何有权威的人。\n\nThose are a few strategies for dealing with today''s American medical establishment.\n这些是应对当今美国医疗机构的一些策略。\n\nWhat patients want is to be treated with respect and consideration.\n患者希望得到尊重和体谅。\n\nBut in my experience, too few hospitals and doctors are ready to do that.\n但根据我的经验，很少有医院或医生愿意这样做。\n\nIn his book, A Whole New Life, novelist Reynolds Price recalls that his doctors chose a crowded hallway as the place to tell him he might have a tumor on his spinal cord.\n《全新生活》这本书的作者，小说家雷诺兹·普莱斯在书中回忆道，他的医生们选择在一个拥挤的走廊告诉他在他的脊髓处可能长了一个肿瘤。\n\nIt did not occur to the two physicians that a hallway was not the most appropriate place for that particular piece of news.\n这两位医生并没有想到走廊并不是最适合给出这种信息的地方。\n\nMy surgeon, who is in his mid-thirties, looks tired.\n我的外科医生，三十多岁，看起来很疲惫。\n\nHe has been overwhelmed with patients who have fallen on the winter ice.\n那些冬天在冰上摔倒的病人让他应接不暇。\n\nHe is a witty man, but sometimes his wit is unwelcome.\n他是个俏皮的人，但有时他的俏皮不怎么受欢迎。\n\n“The health insurance company Blue Cross wants me to put you out in the snow tomorrow afternoon,” he tells me after I have been in the hospital for more than a week.\n在我住院一个多星期后，他告诉我：“蓝十字健康保险公司希望我明天下午就把你扔到雪地里。”\n\nI''m terrified because I have no idea where to go.\n我吓坏了，因为我不知道该去哪里。\n\nI cannot walk or even lift my leg a few inches.\n我走不了路，甚至连把腿抬起几英寸都做不到。\n\nThe hospital social worker strikes me as an idiot.\n我感觉医院的社工是个白痴。\n\nBut my complaints about her only annoy my surgeon.\n但我对她的抱怨只会惹恼我的外科医生。\n\n“I have to work with these people,” he tells my friend, Dr. Karen Brudney, when she mercifully intervenes on my behalf and arranges for me to be transferred to another hospital.\n“我得和这些人共事。”当我的朋友凯伦·布鲁特妮医生十分好心地插手我的事，安排将我转移到另一家医院时，他是这样对她说的。\n\n“If you say one negative thing, they get defensive,” she tells me later.\n“如果你说了什么不好听的话，他们就会开始自卫，”她后来告诉我说。\n\n“They have this kind of institutional loyalty.\n“他们有这种机构忠诚感。\n\nAlways bring an advocate, that is, any other person with you to the hospital, and write down every single question and the answer, the name of every doctor and nurse.\n随时找一位支持者陪同，也就是说，让这个人和你一起去医院，记录下每个问题和答案，写下每位医生和护士的名字。\n\nWhen people know you have their names, they behave better. And,” Brudney adds,\n当人们知道你有他们的名字时，他们会表现得更好。而且，”布鲁特妮补充道，\n\n“if you, as a patient, suggest that you might like to control even part of the situation or be consulted or informed, then you are considered difficult.\n“如果作为一名患者，你暗示想要控制，哪怕是只控制个别情况，或者希望有人征询你的意见或对你有所告知，那么你就会被认为是很难搞的人。\n\nThey want you to be totally passive.\n他们希望你是完全被动的。\n\nThe entire health care system, particularly hospitals and nursing homes, exists for reasons that have nothing to do with taking care of patients. Patients are incidental.”\n整个医疗体系，尤其是医院和养老院，他们存在的原因与照顾患者无关。患者是次要的。”\n\nQuestions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答第16到第18题。\n\n16. What does the speaker say about most American hospitals?\n16. 关于美国的大多数医院，讲话者说了什么？\n\n17. What does Karen Brudney suggest patients do?\n17. 凯伦·布鲁特妮建议患者做什么？\n\n18. What do American doctors expect their patients to be, according to Karen Brudney?\n18. 根据凯伦·布鲁特妮的说法，美国医生期望他们的患者是怎样的？', 180, 1708.83, 949.65, 1129.78, 1136.32, 1156.06, 978.36, 983.45, 'cet6-2022-06-1-g05', 'LECTURE', '[15:49.65]Recording One｜录音一\n[15:53.00]Appear to be submissive, humble, grateful and undemanding; show great pleasure when a doctor comes into your room, even if the visit is brief and useless.｜当医生走进你的房间，即使逗留时间短暂且毫无用处，也要表现出顺从、谦逊、感激和随和，表现出极大的喜悦。\n[16:06.10]Don''t challenge anyone with authority unless you are famous or very rich.｜除非你是名人或非常富有，否则，不要去挑战任何有权威的人。\n[16:11.82]Those are a few strategies for dealing with today''s American medical establishment.｜这些是应对当今美国医疗机构的一些策略。\n[16:18.36]What patients want is to be treated with respect and consideration.｜患者希望得到尊重和体谅。\n[16:23.75]But in my experience, too few hospitals and doctors are ready to do that.｜但根据我的经验，很少有医院或医生愿意这样做。\n[16:29.79]In his book, A Whole New Life, novelist Reynolds Price recalls that his doctors chose a crowded hallway as the place to tell him he might have a tumor on his spinal cord.｜《全新生活》这本书的作者，小说家雷诺兹·普莱斯在书中回忆道，他的医生们选择在一个拥挤的走廊告诉他在他的脊髓处可能长了一个肿瘤。\n[16:44.06]It did not occur to the two physicians that a hallway was not the most appropriate place for that particular piece of news.｜这两位医生并没有想到走廊并不是最适合给出这种信息的地方。\n[16:53.81]My surgeon, who is in his mid-thirties, looks tired.｜我的外科医生，三十多岁，看起来很疲惫。\n[16:58.56]He has been overwhelmed with patients who have fallen on the winter ice.｜那些冬天在冰上摔倒的病人让他应接不暇。\n[17:03.90]He is a witty man, but sometimes his wit is unwelcome.｜他是个俏皮的人，但有时他的俏皮不怎么受欢迎。\n[17:09.00]“The health insurance company Blue Cross wants me to put you out in the snow tomorrow afternoon,” he tells me after I have been in the hospital for more than a week.｜在我住院一个多星期后，他告诉我：“蓝十字健康保险公司希望我明天下午就把你扔到雪地里。”\n[17:20.87]I''m terrified because I have no idea where to go.｜我吓坏了，因为我不知道该去哪里。\n[17:25.38]I cannot walk or even lift my leg a few inches.｜我走不了路，甚至连把腿抬起几英寸都做不到。\n[17:29.79]The hospital social worker strikes me as an idiot.｜我感觉医院的社工是个白痴。\n[17:34.39]But my complaints about her only annoy my surgeon.｜但我对她的抱怨只会惹恼我的外科医生。\n[17:38.22]“I have to work with these people,” he tells my friend, Dr. Karen Brudney, when she mercifully intervenes on my behalf and arranges for me to be transferred to another hospital.｜“我得和这些人共事。”当我的朋友凯伦·布鲁特妮医生十分好心地插手我的事，安排将我转移到另一家医院时，他是这样对她说的。\n[17:51.44]“If you say one negative thing, they get defensive,” she tells me later.｜“如果你说了什么不好听的话，他们就会开始自卫，”她后来告诉我说。\n[17:57.07]“They have this kind of institutional loyalty.｜“他们有这种机构忠诚感。\n[18:00.71]Always bring an advocate, that is, any other person with you to the hospital, and write down every single question and the answer, the name of every doctor and nurse.｜随时找一位支持者陪同，也就是说，让这个人和你一起去医院，记录下每个问题和答案，写下每位医生和护士的名字。\n[18:14.78]When people know you have their names, they behave better. And,” Brudney adds,｜当人们知道你有他们的名字时，他们会表现得更好。而且，”布鲁特妮补充道，\n[18:21.24]“if you, as a patient, suggest that you might like to control even part of the situation or be consulted or informed, then you are considered difficult.｜“如果作为一名患者，你暗示想要控制，哪怕是只控制个别情况，或者希望有人征询你的意见或对你有所告知，那么你就会被认为是很难搞的人。\n[18:33.66]They want you to be totally passive.｜他们希望你是完全被动的。\n[18:36.83]The entire health care system, particularly hospitals and nursing homes, exists for reasons that have nothing to do with taking care of patients. Patients are incidental.”｜整个医疗体系，尤其是医院和养老院，他们存在的原因与照顾患者无关。患者是次要的。”\n[18:49.78]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答第16到第18题。\n[18:56.32]16. What does the speaker say about most American hospitals?｜16. 关于美国的大多数医院，讲话者说了什么？\n[19:16.36]17. What does Karen Brudney suggest patients do?｜17. 凯伦·布鲁特妮建议患者做什么？\n[19:34.50]18. What do American doctors expect their patients to be, according to Karen Brudney?｜18. 根据凯伦·布鲁特妮的说法，美国医生期望他们的患者是怎样的？', NOW(), NOW()
