SELECT
  @a_cet6_2022_09_1_01, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2022-09-1.mp3', 'Conversation One\n对话一\n\nM: Welcome to Money Matters, a weekly program that helps you manage your money.\n男：欢迎来到《金钱很重要》，本节目每周一期，帮您管好您的钱财。\n\nTonight, I''ll be talking to Mary Johnson about budgeting.\n今晚，我将和玛丽·约翰逊谈谈预算问题。\n\nW: Hello, everyone.\n女：大家好。\n\nM: There''s a magic about money.\n男：金钱有一种魔力。\n\nWhen it''s not planned for, tracked and kept a record of, it literally disappears.\n不做计划、不进行追踪和记录，钱就真的消失不见了。\n\nWhat are some of the steps we can take to prevent this from happening?\n我们可以采取哪些措施来防止这种情况的出现呢？\n\nW: Well, it''s all about keeping track of your money.\n女：其实，这都跟对金钱进行追踪有关。\n\nIf you don''t do that, you''ll never be able to set any goals for your budget or have the discipline to stick to them.\n如果你不这样做，你将永远无法为你的预算设定任何目标，也不会有坚持下去的自制力。\n\nM: That''s easier said than done.\n男：说起来容易做起来难。\n\nI read recently that only 41% of Americans adhere to a budget.\n我最近看到，只有41%的美国人能够坚定地执行预算。\n\nW: Yes, but knowing what you earn and what you spend can give you reassurance that you won''t get into debt in the first place.\n女：是的，但是了解自己的收入和支出首先就可以确保你不会负债。\n\nYou can do this by adding up all of your sources of income you have and writing them all down on a piece of paper.\n你可以把你所有的收入来源都加在一起，写在一张纸上。\n\nOn the same page, write down all of your monthly expenses.\n在同一张纸上，也写下你每月所有的开支。\n\nM: I''m always amazed at how much my expenses add up.\n男：我的花费加在一起有那么多，这总是让我很吃惊。\n\nBut designating each item as an income or an expense really helps me have a much better sense of all my spending.\n但将每一项费用都标明是收入还是支出确实有助于我更好地了解自己的所有支出。\n\nW: Right, most people have no idea how much they spend each day, let alone each week or month, no matter how careful they are.\n女：是的，不管人们有多细心，大多数人并不知道他们每天都花了多少钱，更不用说每周或每月了。\n\nNext, subtract your monthly expenses from income.\n下一步，从你的收入中减去每月的支出。\n\nIf the result is positive, you are living within your means.\n如果得出的是正数，那么你就是量入为出。\n\nIf the result is a negative number, you''re going to have to cut back on your spending.\n如果结果是负数，你将不得不削减开支。\n\nM: I''m usually a negative number.\n男：我通常是负数。\n\nI just can''t resist the allure of all those prestigious goods.\n我无法抗拒那些名贵商品的诱惑。\n\nW: Well, it''s not a catastrophe.\n女：好吧，这算不上是灾难性的。\n\nBut you do have to make some changes: try cutting back on those non-essential items, sell some stuff in your attic or shop online to avoid unnecessary temptations like chocolate.\n但你必须做出一些改变：试着减少那些不必要的东西，把存放在阁楼上的东西卖出去一部分，或者在网上购物，以避免受到像巧克力那样的不必要的诱惑。\n\nFailing that you can always find yourself a part-time job.\n就算你做不到，那你总能找到一份兼职的工作吧。\n\nQuestions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的对话回答问题1到4。\n\n1. What does the man say about the weekly program?\n1. 关于每周一期的这个节目，这位男士说了什么？\n\n2. What did the man read recently?\n2. 男士最近看到了什么（信息）？\n\n3. What does the woman suggest the man do first to avoid getting into debt?\n3. 为了避免负债，女士建议男士第一步应该做什么？\n\n4. What does the woman say about online shopping?\n4. 关于网上购物，这位女士说了什么？', 126, 1642.1, 37.09, 163.17, 168.43, 185.12, 39.68, 50.17, 'cet6-2022-09-1-g01', 'LONG_DIALOGUE', '[00:37.09]Conversation One｜对话一\n[00:39.68]M: Welcome to Money Matters, a weekly program that helps you manage your money.｜男：欢迎来到《金钱很重要》，本节目每周一期，帮您管好您的钱财。\n[00:45.54]Tonight, I''ll be talking to Mary Johnson about budgeting.｜今晚，我将和玛丽·约翰逊谈谈预算问题。\n[00:50.47]W: Hello, everyone.｜女：大家好。\n[00:51.65]M: There''s a magic about money.｜男：金钱有一种魔力。\n[00:53.91]When it''s not planned for, tracked and kept a record of, it literally disappears.｜不做计划、不进行追踪和记录，钱就真的消失不见了。\n[01:00.36]What are some of the steps we can take to prevent this from happening?｜我们可以采取哪些措施来防止这种情况的出现呢？\n[01:03.92]W: Well, it''s all about keeping track of your money.｜女：其实，这都跟对金钱进行追踪有关。\n[01:07.47]If you don''t do that, you''ll never be able to set any goals for your budget or have the discipline to stick to them.｜如果你不这样做，你将永远无法为你的预算设定任何目标，也不会有坚持下去的自制力。\n[01:14.41]M: That''s easier said than done.｜男：说起来容易做起来难。\n[01:16.78]I read recently that only 41% of Americans adhere to a budget.｜我最近看到，只有41%的美国人能够坚定地执行预算。\n[01:22.70]W: Yes, but knowing what you earn and what you spend can give you reassurance that you won''t get into debt in the first place.｜女：是的，但是了解自己的收入和支出首先就可以确保你不会负债。\n[01:30.62]You can do this by adding up all of your sources of income you have and writing them all down on a piece of paper.｜你可以把你所有的收入来源都加在一起，写在一张纸上。\n[01:37.36]On the same page, write down all of your monthly expenses.｜在同一张纸上，也写下你每月所有的开支。\n[01:41.69]M: I''m always amazed at how much my expenses add up.｜男：我的花费加在一起有那么多，这总是让我很吃惊。\n[01:45.53]But designating each item as an income or an expense really helps me have a much better sense of all my spending.｜但将每一项费用都标明是收入还是支出确实有助于我更好地了解自己的所有支出。\n[01:54.50]W: Right, most people have no idea how much they spend each day, let alone each week or month, no matter how careful they are.｜女：是的，不管人们有多细心，大多数人并不知道他们每天都花了多少钱，更不用说每周或每月了。\n[02:03.12]Next, subtract your monthly expenses from income.｜下一步，从你的收入中减去每月的支出。\n[02:07.24]If the result is positive, you are living within your means.｜如果得出的是正数，那么你就是量入为出。\n[02:10.90]If the result is a negative number, you''re going to have to cut back on your spending.｜如果结果是负数，你将不得不削减开支。\n[02:16.24]M: I''m usually a negative number.｜男：我通常是负数。\n[02:18.63]I just can''t resist the allure of all those prestigious goods.｜我无法抗拒那些名贵商品的诱惑。\n[02:23.39]W: Well, it''s not a catastrophe.｜女：好吧，这算不上是灾难性的。\n[02:25.82]But you do have to make some changes: try cutting back on those non-essential items, sell some stuff in your attic or shop online to avoid unnecessary temptations like chocolate.｜但你必须做出一些改变：试着减少那些不必要的东西，把存放在阁楼上的东西卖出去一部分，或者在网上购物，以避免受到像巧克力那样的不必要的诱惑。\n[02:38.54]Failing that you can always find yourself a part-time job.｜就算你做不到，那你总能找到一份兼职的工作吧。\n[02:43.17]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的对话回答问题1到4。\n[02:48.43]1. What does the man say about the weekly program?｜1. 关于每周一期的这个节目，这位男士说了什么？\n[03:05.42]2. What did the man read recently?｜2. 男士最近看到了什么（信息）？\n[03:22.46]3. What does the woman suggest the man do first to avoid getting into debt?｜3. 为了避免负债，女士建议男士第一步应该做什么？\n[03:42.11]4. What does the woman say about online shopping?｜4. 关于网上购物，这位女士说了什么？', NOW(), NOW()
