  @a_cet6_2023_06_1_06, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2023-06-1.mp3', 'Conversation Two\n对话二\n\nW: Welcome to our program Book Talk.\n女:欢迎来到我们的《书论》节目。\n\nOur guest today is Frank Jones, a critic of our education system, and the author of a new book, How to Reform Our Universities.\n今天我们的嘉宾是弗兰克·琼斯，他是我们教育体系的批评家，也是新书《如何改革我们的大学》的作者。\n\nM: Hello, Susan.\n男:你好，苏珊。\n\nW: Frank, you support radically changing universities in America?\n女:弗兰克，你支持彻底改变美国的大学?\n\nM: Yes, I believe that the purpose of higher education is to prepare young people to enter the workforce, and that our current system fails to do this.\n男:是的，我认为高等教育的目的是让年轻人做好参加工作的准备，而我们当前的体系没有做到这一点。\n\nWe''re allocating too many resources to disciplines that don''t match the needs of employers.\n我们把太多的资源分配给了不符合雇主需求的学科。\n\nW: I think your attitude to education is a bit cynical, Frank.\n女:我认为你对教育的态度有点愤世嫉俗，弗兰克。\n\nSurely the purpose of university is to prepare young people to participate fully in civic life, rather than just to find well-paid jobs.\n大学的目的无疑是让年轻人做好充分参与公民生活的准备，而不仅仅是为了找到高薪的工作。\n\nM: Susan, many young graduates struggle to find any job, let alone a good one.\n男:苏珊，许多年轻的毕业生都很难找到工作，更不用说找一份好的工作了。\n\nThe job market is grim, particularly for students who study the arts.\n就业市场形势严峻，尤其是对艺术专业的学生而言。\n\nW: I agree that it isn''t easy for young people to find work, but you propose closing down all departments that aren''t directly related to science and technology.\n女:我同意年轻人找工作不容易，但是你提议关闭所有与科技没有直接关系的学院。\n\nIs that really the solution?\n这真的是解决办法吗?\n\nM: You''re overstating my point.\n男:你夸大了我的观点。\n\nMy argument is that we need to use more of our budget on areas like science and engineering.\n我的观点是，我们需要把更多的预算用于科学和工程等科目。\n\nTo do that, we need to take money from subjects like literature and music.\n要做到这一点，我们需要从文学和音乐等科目上把钱拿出来。\n\nW: But the arts have value.\n女:但是艺术是有价值的。\n\nThey''re an important part of our culture.\n它们是我们文化的重要组成部分。\n\nStudying literature or music or sculpture might not result in a job in that area, but it helps young people to think about the world in a deeper way, which makes them better citizens and makes for a better society.\n学习文学、音乐或雕塑可能不会给年轻人一份该领域的工作，但它可以帮助年轻人以更深刻的方式来思考世界，这会让他们成为更好的公民，构建一个更好的社会。\n\nM: I agree that the arts are valuable to society, but it''s naive to think that not only the most talented, but all students should study them at university level.\n男:我同意艺术对社会是有价值的，但是认为不仅是那些最有天赋的学生，而且所有的学生都应该在大学阶段学习艺术就太天真了。\n\nThe arts are very competitive, and most graduates will end up with a great deal of debt, obtaining a degree that has little value on the job market.\n艺术专业竞争非常激烈，大多数毕业生最终都会背负大量债务，获得一个在就业市场上几乎没有价值的学位。\n\nQuestions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的对话回答问题5至8。\n\n5. What do we learn from the conversation about the man?\n5. 从对话中我们可以了解到关于男士的什么信息?\n\n6.What does the man believe is the problem with the current American system of higher education?\n6. 男士认为当前美国高等教育体系的问题是什么?\n\n7.How should the education budget be allocated according to the man?\n7. 根据男士的说法，教育预算应该如何分配?\n\n8.What does the woman say the arts can do?\n8. 女士说艺术可以做什么?\n\nSection B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages.\nB节 说明：在本节中，你将会听到两篇短文。', 133, 1652.72, 233.39, 366.58, 388.56, 408.79, 293.07, 308.32, 'cet6-2023-06-1-g02', 'LONG_DIALOGUE', '[03:53.39]Conversation Two｜对话二\n[03:55.59]W: Welcome to our program Book Talk.｜女:欢迎来到我们的《书论》节目。\n[03:58.86]Our guest today is Frank Jones, a critic of our education system, and the author of a new book, How to Reform Our Universities.｜今天我们的嘉宾是弗兰克·琼斯，他是我们教育体系的批评家，也是新书《如何改革我们的大学》的作者。\n[04:09.12]M: Hello, Susan.｜男:你好，苏珊。\n[04:10.58]W: Frank, you support radically changing universities in America?｜女:弗兰克，你支持彻底改变美国的大学?\n[04:15.60]M: Yes, I believe that the purpose of higher education is to prepare young people to enter the workforce, and that our current system fails to do this.｜男:是的，我认为高等教育的目的是让年轻人做好参加工作的准备，而我们当前的体系没有做到这一点。\n[04:26.58]We''re allocating too many resources to disciplines that don''t match the needs of employers.｜我们把太多的资源分配给了不符合雇主需求的学科。\n[04:32.73]W: I think your attitude to education is a bit cynical, Frank.｜女:我认为你对教育的态度有点愤世嫉俗，弗兰克。\n[04:36.63]Surely the purpose of university is to prepare young people to participate fully in civic life, rather than just to find well-paid jobs.｜大学的目的无疑是让年轻人做好充分参与公民生活的准备，而不仅仅是为了找到高薪的工作。\n[04:47.02]M: Susan, many young graduates struggle to find any job, let alone a good one.｜男:苏珊，许多年轻的毕业生都很难找到工作，更不用说找一份好的工作了。\n[04:53.07]The job market is grim, particularly for students who study the arts.｜就业市场形势严峻，尤其是对艺术专业的学生而言。\n[04:57.63]W: I agree that it isn''t easy for young people to find work, but you propose closing down all departments that aren''t directly related to science and technology.｜女:我同意年轻人找工作不容易，但是你提议关闭所有与科技没有直接关系的学院。\n[05:08.62]Is that really the solution?｜这真的是解决办法吗?\n[05:10.98]M: You''re overstating my point.｜男:你夸大了我的观点。\n[05:13.04]My argument is that we need to use more of our budget on areas like science and engineering.｜我的观点是，我们需要把更多的预算用于科学和工程等科目。\n[05:19.59]To do that, we need to take money from subjects like literature and music.｜要做到这一点，我们需要从文学和音乐等科目上把钱拿出来。\n[05:25.00]W: But the arts have value.｜女:但是艺术是有价值的。\n[05:27.23]They''re an important part of our culture.｜它们是我们文化的重要组成部分。\n[05:29.24]Studying literature or music or sculpture might not result in a job in that area, but it helps young people to think about the world in a deeper way, which makes them better citizens and makes for a better society.｜学习文学、音乐或雕塑可能不会给年轻人一份该领域的工作，但它可以帮助年轻人以更深刻的方式来思考世界，这会让他们成为更好的公民，构建一个更好的社会。\n[05:43.42]M: I agree that the arts are valuable to society, but it''s naive to think that not only the most talented, but all students should study them at university level.｜男:我同意艺术对社会是有价值的，但是认为不仅是那些最有天赋的学生，而且所有的学生都应该在大学阶段学习艺术就太天真了。\n[05:55.61]The arts are very competitive, and most graduates will end up with a great deal of debt, obtaining a degree that has little value on the job market.｜艺术专业竞争非常激烈，大多数毕业生最终都会背负大量债务，获得一个在就业市场上几乎没有价值的学位。\n[06:06.58]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的对话回答问题5至8。\n[06:11.73]5. What do we learn from the conversation about the man?｜5. 从对话中我们可以了解到关于男士的什么信息?\n[06:28.56]6.What does the man believe is the problem with the current American system of higher education?｜6. 男士认为当前美国高等教育体系的问题是什么?\n[06:49.09]7.How should the education budget be allocated according to the man?｜7. 根据男士的说法，教育预算应该如何分配?\n[07:08.20]8.What does the woman say the arts can do?｜8. 女士说艺术可以做什么?\n[07:24.96]Section B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages.｜B节 说明：在本节中，你将会听到两篇短文。', NOW(), NOW()
