  @a_cet6_2023_12_1_16, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2023-12-1.mp3', 'Recording One\n讲座一\n\nWhat makes humans different from other species?\n是什么让人类与其他物种有所不同？\n\nSome philosophers argue it''s morals or ethics, while some scientists assert it''s our greater cognitive development.\n有些哲学家认为是道德或伦理，而有些科学家则坚称是我们较强大的认知发展。\n\nBut I argue that the main difference is our desire to combat routine.\n但我认为，主要的区别在于我们对抗常规的欲望。\n\nThis makes being creative a biological mandate, as what we seek in art and technology is surprise, not simply a fulfillment of expectations.\n这使得创造性成了某种生物学指令，我们在艺术和技术中寻求的是惊喜，而不仅仅是达成期待后的满足感。\n\nAs a result, a wild imagination has characterized the history of our species:\n因此，狂野的想象力成了我们这个物种的历史的特征：\n\nwe build intricate habitats, devise complex recipes for our food, wear clothes that reflect constant changes in fashion, communicate with elaborate signs, symbols and sounds, and travel between habitats on wings and wheels of our own design.\n我们建造复杂的栖息地，设计复杂的食谱，穿着能反映不断变化的时尚的衣服，用精心制作的标志、符号和声音交流，用自己设计的翅膀和轮子在栖息地之间穿行。\n\nTo satisfy our appetite for novelty, innovation is key.\n为了满足我们对新奇事物的欲望，创新是关键。\n\nBut who innovates?\n但是谁在创新呢？\n\nNow, many people, both laymen and experts, believe that only geniuses innovate.\n现在，许多人，包括外行和专家，都认为只有天才才能创新。\n\nBut I believe that innovation is not something that only a few people do.\n但我相信，创新并不是只有少数人才能做到的。\n\nThe innovative drive lives in every human brain, and the resulting war against the repetitive is what powers the massive changes that distinguish one generation from the next.\n创新的动力存在于每个人的大脑中，由此产生的对抗重复的战争是区分一代人和下一代人的巨大变化的驱动力。\n\nThe drive to create the new is a trait of being human.\n创造新事物的欲望是人类的一个特点。\n\nWe build cultures by the hundreds and tell new stories by the millions.\n我们建立了成百上千种文化，讲述了数以千万计的新故事。\n\nWe create and surround ourselves with things that have never existed before, while animals do not.\n我们创造了前所未有的事物，并将自己置身其中，而动物却做不到。\n\nBut where do our new ideas come from?\n但我们的新想法是从哪里来的呢？\n\nAccording to many, new ideas come from seemingly nowhere to great minds.\n很多人认为，新的想法不知出处，似乎突然出现在伟人的头脑中。\n\nFrom this perspective, new ideas are almost like magic.\n从这个角度来看，新想法几乎就像魔术一样。\n\nThey come in a flash of inspiration to a select few.\n它们是少数人的灵光一现。\n\nHowever, the reality is that across the spectrum of human activities, prior work propels the creative process.\n然而，事实是，在人类活动的各个领域，先前的工作推动了创造的过程。\n\nWe may think of innovation as being the result of inspiration or genius, but it''s really the result of developing the ideas of others further.\n我们可能认为创新是灵感或天赋的结果，但它实际上是进一步发展他人想法的成果。\n\nThis happens in technology, where one invention enables or inspires further inventions.\n这种情况发生在技术领域，在这个领域中，一项发明可以导致或激发进一步的发明。\n\nAnd it happens in the arts, as writers, composers and painters use the work of previous artists in their own work.\n这种情况也发生在艺术领域中，作家、作曲家和画家会在自己的作品中运用以前的艺术家的成果。\n\nThe human brain works from precedent.\n人类大脑在先例的基础上运作。\n\nWe take the ideas we''ve inherited and put them together into some new shape.\n我们把继承而来的想法加以组合，构成新的想法。\n\nWhat is a true creator?\n什么是真正的创造者？\n\nIs a creator a genius who makes something out of nothing?\n创造者是无中生有的天才吗？\n\nNo. Creators are simply humans who use what they inherit, who absorb the past and manipulate it to create possible futures.\n不是的。创造者只不过是利用了他们所继承的东西的人，是吸收过去并加以操纵来创造可能的未来的人。\n\nThus, humans are creators as a rule, rather than as an exception.\n因此，人类都是创造者，这是常规，而非例外。\n\nQuestions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n\n16. What question does the speaker address in this talk?\n16. 讲话者在这次演讲中提出了什么问题？\n\n17. What does the speaker believe about innovation?\n17. 讲话者对创新有何看法？\n\n18. How does the human brain work according to the speaker?\n18. 按照讲话者的说法，人类的大脑是如何运作的？', 179, 1566.81, 853.02, 1032.46, 1037.65, 1054.08, NULL, NULL, 'cet6-2023-12-1-g05', 'LECTURE', '[14:13.02]Recording One｜讲座一\n[14:14.92]What makes humans different from other species?｜是什么让人类与其他物种有所不同？\n[14:18.79]Some philosophers argue it''s morals or ethics, while some scientists assert it''s our greater cognitive development.｜有些哲学家认为是道德或伦理，而有些科学家则坚称是我们较强大的认知发展。\n[14:26.77]But I argue that the main difference is our desire to combat routine.｜但我认为，主要的区别在于我们对抗常规的欲望。\n[14:31.89]This makes being creative a biological mandate, as what we seek in art and technology is surprise, not simply a fulfillment of expectations.｜这使得创造性成了某种生物学指令，我们在艺术和技术中寻求的是惊喜，而不仅仅是达成期待后的满足感。\n[14:42.51]As a result, a wild imagination has characterized the history of our species:｜因此，狂野的想象力成了我们这个物种的历史的特征：\n[14:48.35]we build intricate habitats, devise complex recipes for our food, wear clothes that reflect constant changes in fashion, communicate with elaborate signs, symbols and sounds, and travel between habitats on wings and wheels of our own design.｜我们建造复杂的栖息地，设计复杂的食谱，穿着能反映不断变化的时尚的衣服，用精心制作的标志、符号和声音交流，用自己设计的翅膀和轮子在栖息地之间穿行。\n[15:06.99]To satisfy our appetite for novelty, innovation is key.｜为了满足我们对新奇事物的欲望，创新是关键。\n[15:11.54]But who innovates?｜但是谁在创新呢？\n[15:13.58]Now, many people, both laymen and experts, believe that only geniuses innovate.｜现在，许多人，包括外行和专家，都认为只有天才才能创新。\n[15:20.14]But I believe that innovation is not something that only a few people do.｜但我相信，创新并不是只有少数人才能做到的。\n[15:24.95]The innovative drive lives in every human brain, and the resulting war against the repetitive is what powers the massive changes that distinguish one generation from the next.｜创新的动力存在于每个人的大脑中，由此产生的对抗重复的战争是区分一代人和下一代人的巨大变化的驱动力。\n[15:36.30]The drive to create the new is a trait of being human.｜创造新事物的欲望是人类的一个特点。\n[15:40.59]We build cultures by the hundreds and tell new stories by the millions.｜我们建立了成百上千种文化，讲述了数以千万计的新故事。\n[15:45.32]We create and surround ourselves with things that have never existed before, while animals do not.｜我们创造了前所未有的事物，并将自己置身其中，而动物却做不到。\n[15:52.33]But where do our new ideas come from?｜但我们的新想法是从哪里来的呢？\n[15:55.39]According to many, new ideas come from seemingly nowhere to great minds.｜很多人认为，新的想法不知出处，似乎突然出现在伟人的头脑中。\n[16:01.24]From this perspective, new ideas are almost like magic.｜从这个角度来看，新想法几乎就像魔术一样。\n[16:05.83]They come in a flash of inspiration to a select few.｜它们是少数人的灵光一现。\n[16:09.57]However, the reality is that across the spectrum of human activities, prior work propels the creative process.｜然而，事实是，在人类活动的各个领域，先前的工作推动了创造的过程。\n[16:18.26]We may think of innovation as being the result of inspiration or genius, but it''s really the result of developing the ideas of others further.｜我们可能认为创新是灵感或天赋的结果，但它实际上是进一步发展他人想法的成果。\n[16:28.39]This happens in technology, where one invention enables or inspires further inventions.｜这种情况发生在技术领域，在这个领域中，一项发明可以导致或激发进一步的发明。\n[16:34.67]And it happens in the arts, as writers, composers and painters use the work of previous artists in their own work.｜这种情况也发生在艺术领域中，作家、作曲家和画家会在自己的作品中运用以前的艺术家的成果。\n[16:42.95]The human brain works from precedent.｜人类大脑在先例的基础上运作。\n[16:46.13]We take the ideas we''ve inherited and put them together into some new shape.｜我们把继承而来的想法加以组合，构成新的想法。\n[16:51.00]What is a true creator?｜什么是真正的创造者？\n[16:53.13]Is a creator a genius who makes something out of nothing?｜创造者是无中生有的天才吗？\n[16:56.84]No. Creators are simply humans who use what they inherit, who absorb the past and manipulate it to create possible futures.｜不是的。创造者只不过是利用了他们所继承的东西的人，是吸收过去并加以操纵来创造可能的未来的人。\n[17:06.41]Thus, humans are creators as a rule, rather than as an exception.｜因此，人类都是创造者，这是常规，而非例外。\n[17:12.46]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n[17:17.65]16. What question does the speaker address in this talk?｜16. 讲话者在这次演讲中提出了什么问题？\n[17:34.38]17. What does the speaker believe about innovation?｜17. 讲话者对创新有何看法？\n[17:53.70]18. How does the human brain work according to the speaker?｜18. 按照讲话者的说法，人类的大脑是如何运作的？', NOW(), NOW()
