SELECT
  @a_cet6_2024_06_1_12, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2024-06-1.mp3', 'Passage Two\n短文二\n\nBelieve it or not, human creativity benefits from constraints.\n信不信由你，人类的创造力受益于限制。\n\nAccording to psychologists, when you have less to work with, you actually begin to see the world differently.\n按照心理学家的说法，当你缺少资源时，你实际上开始以不同的方式看待世界。\n\nWith constraints, you dedicate your mental energy to acting more resourcefully.\n有了限制，你就会把精力投入到更机智地行动中。\n\nWhen challenged, you figure out new ways to be better.\n当遇到挑战时，你会找到新方法以变得更好。\n\nThe most successful creative people know that constraints give their minds the impetus to leap higher.\n最成功的富有创造力的人知道，限制会给他们的思想带来动力去跳得更高。\n\nPeople who invent new products are not limited by what they don''t have or can''t do.\n发明新产品的人不受他们没有或不能做的东西的限制。\n\nThey leverage their limitations to push themselves even further.\n他们利用自己的局限性来推动自己走得更远。\n\nMany products and services are created because the founders saw a limitation in what they use.\n许多产品和服务之所以被创造出来，是因为创始人看到了他们所使用的东西的局限性。\n\nThey created innovation based on what was not working for them at the moment.\n他们在当时不适合自己的基础上进行创新。\n\nInnovation is a creative person''s response to limitation.\n创新是有创造力的人对限制的反应。\n\nIn a 2015 study, which examined how thinking about scarcity or abundance influences how creatively people use their resources, Ravi Mehta at the University of Illinois and Meng Zhu at Johns Hopkins University found that people simply have no incentive to use what''s available to them in novel ways.\n2015年的一项研究调查了对稀缺或丰富的思考如何影响人们创造性地使用资源，伊利诺斯大学的拉维·梅塔和约翰·霍普金斯大学的朱萌在该研究中发现，人们根本没有动力以新颖的方式使用现有资源。\n\nWhen people face scarcity, they give themselves the freedom to use resources in less conventional ways because they have to.\n当人们面临资源短缺时，他们会给自己以不那么传统的方式使用资源的自由，因为他们必须这样做。\n\nObstacles can broaden your perception and open up your thinking processes.\n障碍可以拓宽你的视野，打开你的思维过程。\n\nConsistent constraints help you improve at connecting unrelated ideas and concepts.\n一贯的限制可以帮助你提高将不相关的想法和概念联系起来的能力。\n\nMarissa Mayer, former vice-president for search products and user experience at Google, once wrote in a publication on Bloomberg, "Constraints shape and focus problems and provide clear challenges to overcome.\n谷歌负责搜索产品和用户体验的前副总裁玛丽莎?梅耶尔曾在彭博社的一篇文章中写道："限制塑造和聚焦问题，并提供需要克服的明确挑战。\n\nCreativity thrives best when constrained."\n创造力在受到限制的情况下才能发挥得最好。"\n\nQuestions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题12到15。\n\n12. What do psychologists say people do when they are short of resources?\n12. 心理学家说，当人们缺乏资源时，他们会做什么？\n\n13.What does the passage say about innovation?\n13. 关于创新，文章说了什么？\n\n14.What did a 2015 study by Ravi Mehta and Meng Zhu find?\n14. 拉维·梅塔和朱萌在2015年做的一项研究发现了什么？\n\n15.What did Marissa Mayer once write concerning creativity?\n15. 关于创造力，玛丽莎·梅耶尔曾经写过什么?\n\nSection C Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions.\nC节 说明：在本节中，你将会听到三篇讲座或讲话，每篇讲座或讲话后面都有三个或四个问题。', 122, 1620.24, 653.70, 775.97, 781.52, 799.93, 660.47, 666.81, 'cet6-2024-06-1-g04', 'PASSAGE', '[10:53.70]Passage Two｜短文二\n[10:55.39]Believe it or not, human creativity benefits from constraints.｜信不信由你，人类的创造力受益于限制。\n[11:00.47]According to psychologists, when you have less to work with, you actually begin to see the world differently.｜按照心理学家的说法，当你缺少资源时，你实际上开始以不同的方式看待世界。\n[11:07.11]With constraints, you dedicate your mental energy to acting more resourcefully.｜有了限制，你就会把精力投入到更机智地行动中。\n[11:12.86]When challenged, you figure out new ways to be better.｜当遇到挑战时，你会找到新方法以变得更好。\n[11:16.75]The most successful creative people know that constraints give their minds the impetus to leap higher.｜最成功的富有创造力的人知道，限制会给他们的思想带来动力去跳得更高。\n[11:24.86]People who invent new products are not limited by what they don''t have or can''t do.｜发明新产品的人不受他们没有或不能做的东西的限制。\n[11:31.15]They leverage their limitations to push themselves even further.｜他们利用自己的局限性来推动自己走得更远。\n[11:35.97]Many products and services are created because the founders saw a limitation in what they use.｜许多产品和服务之所以被创造出来，是因为创始人看到了他们所使用的东西的局限性。\n[11:43.06]They created innovation based on what was not working for them at the moment.｜他们在当时不适合自己的基础上进行创新。\n[11:48.39]Innovation is a creative person''s response to limitation.｜创新是有创造力的人对限制的反应。\n[11:53.28]In a 2015 study, which examined how thinking about scarcity or abundance influences how creatively people use their resources, Ravi Mehta at the University of Illinois and Meng Zhu at Johns Hopkins University found that people simply have no incentive to use what''s available to them in novel ways.｜2015年的一项研究调查了对稀缺或丰富的思考如何影响人们创造性地使用资源，伊利诺斯大学的拉维·梅塔和约翰·霍普金斯大学的朱萌在该研究中发现，人们根本没有动力以新颖的方式使用现有资源。\n[12:15.72]When people face scarcity, they give themselves the freedom to use resources in less conventional ways because they have to.｜当人们面临资源短缺时，他们会给自己以不那么传统的方式使用资源的自由，因为他们必须这样做。\n[12:24.46]Obstacles can broaden your perception and open up your thinking processes.｜障碍可以拓宽你的视野，打开你的思维过程。\n[12:30.26]Consistent constraints help you improve at connecting unrelated ideas and concepts.｜一贯的限制可以帮助你提高将不相关的想法和概念联系起来的能力。\n[12:36.78]Marissa Mayer, former vice-president for search products and user experience at Google, once wrote in a publication on Bloomberg, "Constraints shape and focus problems and provide clear challenges to overcome.｜谷歌负责搜索产品和用户体验的前副总裁玛丽莎?梅耶尔曾在彭博社的一篇文章中写道："限制塑造和聚焦问题，并提供需要克服的明确挑战。\n[12:51.85]Creativity thrives best when constrained."｜创造力在受到限制的情况下才能发挥得最好。"\n[12:55.97]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题12到15。\n[13:01.52]12. What do psychologists say people do when they are short of resources?｜12. 心理学家说，当人们缺乏资源时，他们会做什么？\n[13:20.23]13.What does the passage say about innovation?｜13. 关于创新，文章说了什么？\n[13:38.27]14.What did a 2015 study by Ravi Mehta and Meng Zhu find?｜14. 拉维·梅塔和朱萌在2015年做的一项研究发现了什么？\n[13:57.45]15.What did Marissa Mayer once write concerning creativity?｜15. 关于创造力，玛丽莎·梅耶尔曾经写过什么?\n[14:16.12]Section C Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions.｜C节 说明：在本节中，你将会听到三篇讲座或讲话，每篇讲座或讲话后面都有三个或四个问题。', NOW(), NOW()
