  @a_cet6_2024_12_1_18, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2024-12-1.mp3', 'Recording One\n讲座一\n\nAll societies are held together by seven universal moral rules, which include deferring to superiors and respecting the property of others, Oxford University has concluded.\n牛津大学得出结论说，所有社会都是由7条普遍的道德准则维系在一起的，其中包括服从上级和尊重他人的财产。\n\nAlthough many cultures are moving towards more liberal, less hierarchical organizations, the new research suggests that traditional power structures and basic values of charity and brotherhood are the cornerstones of successful societies.\n尽管许多文化正朝着更自由、层级更少的组织发展，但新的研究表明，传统的权力结构以及慈善和兄弟情谊的基本价值观是成功社会的基石。\n\nThe huge study of 60 different cultures around the world found that all communities operate under seven basic moral codes.\n这项对全球60种不同文化的大规模研究发现，所有团体都在7条基本道德准则下运作。\n\nThose universal rules are: help your family, help your group, return favors, be brave, defer to superiors, divide resources fairly, and respect the property of others.\n这些普遍规则是：帮助你的家庭、帮助你的团队、回报恩惠、勇敢、服从上级、公平分配资源和尊重他人的财产。\n\nThe character traits held for every kind of community, be they traditional hunter gatherers or advanced civilizations, helping to uphold civilized society and foster social cooperation, researchers found.\n研究人员发现，这些性格特征适用于每一种团体，无论是传统的狩猎采集者还是先进的文明社会，帮助他们维护文明社会，促进社会合作。\n\n“Everyone everywhere shares a common moral code,” said Dr.Oliver Scott Curry, lead author and senior researcher at Oxford.\n牛津大学高级研究员、该研究的第一作者奥利弗·斯科特·库里博士说：“世界各地的每个人都有共同的道德准则。\n\n“These seven moral rules appear to be universal across cultures, because people face the same social problems.\n这七条道德准则似乎在不同文化中都是普遍存在的，因为人们面临着同样的社会问题。\n\nEven if some of these traits look right-wing or conservative, for example, deferring to authority, left-wing people will still have a group loyalty and deference to someone or something.\n即使其中一些特征看起来是右翼或保守的，例如，服从权威，左翼人士仍然会对某人或某事有群体忠诚和尊重。\n\nThis shows there really is more that unites us than divides us.”\n这表明团结我们的因素真的比分裂我们的因素要多。”\n\nThe study published in Current Anthropology is the largest and most comprehensive and widespread survey of morals ever conducted and aimed to find out whether different societies had different versions of morality.\n发表在《当代人类学》杂志上的这项研究是迄今为止规模最大、最全面、最广泛的道德调查，其目的是找出不同的社会是否有不同版本的道德观念。\n\nThe team accessed the world''s best archives to analyze accounts from more than 600 sources of 60 societies around the world.\n该团队访问了世界上最好的档案以分析600多个来自全球60个社会的账户。\n\nThey found that the seven rules were considered morally good in all societies and were observed across all continents, religions and politics.\n他们发现，这七条准则在所有社会中都被认为是合乎道德的，并且在所有大陆、宗教和政治中都得到遵守。\n\nHowever, some communities valued certain rules more highly than others.\n然而，有些团体比其他团体更重视某些规则。\n\nDr.Curry hopes that this research helps to promote mutual understanding between people of different cultures and appreciation of what we have in common and how and why we differ.\n库里博士希望这项研究有助于促进不同文化背景的人之间的相互理解，并欣赏我们的共同点以及我们如何以及为什么不同。\n\nCo-author professor Harvey Whitehouse said social scientists should now set about testing the theory in the field, rather than relying on old data.\n合著者哈维·怀特豪斯教授说，社会科学家现在应该着手在实地检验这一理论，而不是依靠旧数据。\n\n“This study was based on historical descriptions of cultures around the world,” he said.\n他说：“这项研究是基于对世界各地文化的历史描述。\n\n“These descriptions were made independent of and prior to the theory we''re testing.\n这些描述是独立于且先于我们测试的理论的。\n\nIn future, if social scientists are serious about testing theories of morality, they will need to gather new data, more systematically, out in the field.”\n未来，如果社会科学家认真对待道德理论的测试，他们就需要实地更系统地收集新的数据。”\n\nQuestions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n\n16. What do we learn from the conclusion of the Oxford University researchers?\n16. 我们从牛津大学研究人员的结论中了解到什么？\n\n17.What did the new study by Oxford researchers aim to do?\n17. 牛津大学研究人员的这项新研究的目的是什么？\n\n18.What does Harvey Whitehouse think social scientists should do to test theories of morality?\n18. 哈维·怀特豪斯认为社会科学家应该如何检验道德理论？', 197, 1666.14, 880.4