SELECT
  @a_cet6_2024_12_1_21, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2024-12-1.mp3', 'Recording Two\n讲座二\n\nWhen it comes to food, color is money.\n说到食物，颜色就是金钱。\n\nFood companies know that better color gives better prices, so they ensure that their products are attractive in color to bolster earnings.\n食品公司知道颜色越好价格越好，所以他们确保他们的产品颜色越有吸引力，以增加收入。\n\nOranges, for example, have to meet specific color standards.\n例如，橙子必须符合特定的颜色标准。\n\nAnd oranges are just the beginning.\n而橙子只是一个开始。\n\nThere are color standards for foods including French fries, tomatoes, pumpkins, olives, honey and cherries.\n包括炸薯条、西红柿、南瓜、橄榄、蜂蜜和樱桃在内的食品都有颜色标准。\n\nThe attention to color is for good reason.\n对颜色的关注是有充分理由的。\n\nFor all the talk of the tongue, our eyes really might be the most important part of the eating experience.\n尽管我们都在谈论舌头，但我们的眼睛确实可能是饮食体验中最重要的部分。\n\nA great number of our brain functions are dedicated to processing vision, while only a percent or two is given over to taste faculties, and the result is that color actually changes how we taste food.\n我们大脑的很多功能都是用来处理视觉的，而只有百分之一到百分之二的功能是用来处理味觉的，结果就是颜色实际上改变了我们品尝食物的方式。\n\nScientists have demonstrated many times the importance of color to taste.\n科学家们已经多次证明了颜色对味觉的重要性。\n\nIn a 1980s study, for example, test participants were asked to name the flavor of the beverage they were drinking without being able to see that beverage.\n例如，在20世纪80年代的一项研究中，测试参与者被要求在看不到饮料的情况下说出他们正在喝的饮料的味道。\n\nOnly one in five was able to identify it as orange.\n只有五分之一的人能识别出它是橙子的。\n\nBut when they were allowed to see what they were drinking which was orange in color, each of them identified the orange flavor.\n但是当被允许看到他们喝的是橙色的东西时，他们每个人都认出了橙子的味道。\n\nAnd when a drink that was lime flavor was colored orange, nearly half of respondents identified the flavor of the beverage as orange.\n当一种酸橙味的饮料被做成橙色时，近一半的受访者认为这种饮料的味道是橙色的。\n\nNone made that mistake when the beverage was green in color.\n当饮料是绿色的时候，没有人会犯这样的错误。\n\nWhat''s happening when someone fails to identify the flavor of a beverage they can''t see or that has an abnormal color?\n当人们无法识别他们看不见的或者颜色不正常的饮料的味道时，发生什么？\n\nIs it that participants can''t discern what the flavor is, and so rely on visual cues?\n是参与者无法辨别味道，因此依赖视觉线索？\n\nOr does color actually change the experience of taste?\n或者颜色真的改变了味觉体验？\n\nThe key to these questions is the effect of color on expectation.\n这些问题的关键是颜色对期望的影响。\n\nThe brain makes predictions using color.\n大脑通过颜色进行预测。\n\nThese predictions rely on expectations that come from experience.\n这些预测依赖于来自经验的期望。\n\nResearch shows that we''re all born liking sweet things and desire to eat them, but we aren''t born knowing what the sweet things we crave look like.\n研究表明，我们生来就喜欢甜食，渴望吃甜食,但我们并不是生来就知道我们渴望的甜食是什么样子的。\n\nSo we need to learn this over time.\n所以我们需要慢慢学习。\n\nThe brain gets information from the environment.\n大脑从环境中获取信息。\n\nAnd we learn, for example, that fruits go from green and sour to red and ripe and sweet.\n例如，我们知道水果从绿色发酸变成红色、成熟和发甜。\n\nThus, if we see a green berry, we equate its color with a sour taste, and just looking at a red fruit will activate our expectations that it will taste sweet and delicious.\n因此，如果我们看到一个绿色的浆果，我们就会把它的颜色与酸的味道画等号，而只要看到红色的水果，我们就会期望它吃起来甜美可口。\n\nQuestions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n\n19. What does the speaker say about our eyes?\n19. 关于我们的眼睛，讲话者说了什么？\n\n20.What does color actually do according to this lecture?\n20. 根据这篇讲座，颜色到底有什么作用？\n\n21.What does the brain use color to do?\n21.大脑利用颜色来做什么？', 163, 1666.14, 1142.02, 1304.73, 1346.93, 1365.35, NULL, NULL, 'cet6-2024-12-1-g06', 'LECTURE', '[19:02.02]Recording Two｜讲座二\n[19:04.23]When it comes to food, color is money.｜说到食物，颜色就是金钱。\n[19:08.00]Food companies know that better color gives better prices, so they ensure that their products are attractive in color to bolster earnings.｜食品公司知道颜色越好价格越好，所以他们确保他们的产品颜色越有吸引力，以增加收入。\n[19:17.21]Oranges, for example, have to meet specific color standards.｜例如，橙子必须符合特定的颜色标准。\n[19:22.00]And oranges are just the beginning.｜而橙子只是一个开始。\n[19:24.17]There are color standards for foods including French fries, tomatoes, pumpkins, olives, honey and cherries.｜包括炸薯条、西红柿、南瓜、橄榄、蜂蜜和樱桃在内的食品都有颜色标准。\n[19:33.90]The attention to color is for good reason.｜对颜色的关注是有充分理由的。\n[19:36.92]For all the talk of the tongue, our eyes really might be the most important part of the eating experience.｜尽管我们都在谈论舌头，但我们的眼睛确实可能是饮食体验中最重要的部分。\n[19:44.01]A great number of our brain functions are dedicated to processing vision, while only a percent or two is given over to taste faculties, and the result is that color actually changes how we taste food.｜我们大脑的很多功能都是用来处理视觉的，而只有百分之一到百分之二的功能是用来处理味觉的，结果就是颜色实际上改变了我们品尝食物的方式。\n[19:58.72]Scientists have demonstrated many times the importance of color to taste.｜科学家们已经多次证明了颜色对味觉的重要性。\n[20:04.64]In a 1980s study, for example, test participants were asked to name the flavor of the beverage they were drinking without being able to see that beverage.｜例如，在20世纪80年代的一项研究中，测试参与者被要求在看不到饮料的情况下说出他们正在喝的饮料的味道。\n[20:14.35]Only one in five was able to identify it as orange.｜只有五分之一的人能识别出它是橙子的。\n[20:18.91]But when they were allowed to see what they were drinking which was orange in color, each of them identified the orange flavor.｜但是当被允许看到他们喝的是橙色的东西时，他们每个人都认出了橙子的味道。\n[20:26.00]And when a drink that was lime flavor was colored orange, nearly half of respondents identified the flavor of the beverage as orange.｜当一种酸橙味的饮料被做成橙色时，近一半的受访者认为这种饮料的味道是橙色的。\n[20:34.34]None made that mistake when the beverage was green in color.｜当饮料是绿色的时候，没有人会犯这样的错误。\n[20:38.56]What''s happening when someone fails to identify the flavor of a beverage they can''t see or that has an abnormal color?｜当人们无法识别他们看不见的或者颜色不正常的饮料的味道时，发生什么？\n[20:45.93]Is it that participants can''t discern what the flavor is, and so rely on visual cues?｜是参与者无法辨别味道，因此依赖视觉线索？\n[20:51.83]Or does color actually change the experience of taste?｜或者颜色真的改变了味觉体验？\n[20:55.94]The key to these questions is the effect of color on expectation.｜这些问题的关键是颜色对期望的影响。\n[21:00.82]The brain makes predictions using color.｜大脑通过颜色进行预测。\n[21:04.05]These predictions rely on expectations that come from experience.｜这些预测依赖于来自经验的期望。\n[21:08.96]Research shows that we''re all born liking sweet things and desire to eat them, but we aren''t born knowing what the sweet things we crave look like.｜研究表明，我们生来就喜欢甜食，渴望吃甜食,但我们并不是生来就知道我们渴望的甜食是什么样子的。\n[21:18.94]So we need to learn this over time.｜所以我们需要慢慢学习。\n[21:21.33]The brain gets information from the environment.｜大脑从环境中获取信息。\n[21:25.03]And we learn, for example, that fruits go from green and sour to red and ripe and sweet.｜例如，我们知道水果从绿色发酸变成红色、成熟和发甜。\n[21:31.87]Thus, if we see a green berry, we equate its color with a sour taste, and just looking at a red fruit will activate our expectations that it will taste sweet and delicious.｜因此，如果我们看到一个绿色的浆果，我们就会把它的颜色与酸的味道画等号，而只要看到红色的水果，我们就会期望它吃起来甜美可口。\n[21:44.73]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n[21:50.00]19. What does the speaker say about our eyes?｜19. 关于我们的眼睛，讲话者说了什么？\n[22:08.34]20.What does color actually do according to this lecture?｜20. 根据这篇讲座，颜色到底有什么作用？\n[22:26.93]21.What does the brain use color to do?｜21.大脑利用颜色来做什么？', NOW(), NOW()
