SELECT
  @a_cet6_2024_12_2_09, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2024-12-2.mp3', 'Passage One\n短文一\n\nIncreased television time for young children has long been linked with poorer progress in some areas of development.\n长期以来，幼儿看电视时间的增加与某些发育领域的进展较差有关。\n\nOne possible way to counter those negative effects is talking while watching the screen.\n有一种方法可能减弱这些负面影响，那就是边看电视边对话。\n\nAs a new study suggests, the more parents engaged in conversation with preschoolers during TV time, the more likely those children were to have higher curiosity levels when they reached kindergarten.\n一项新的研究表明，看电视时，父母在学龄前儿童对话的次数越多，这些孩子在上幼儿园时好奇心水平就越高。\n\nThis was particularly true for children with socioeconomic disadvantages.\n这对于社会经济地位较低家庭的儿童来说尤其如此。\n\n“Our findings reinforce the importance of parent conversation to promote early childhood development and curiosity,” said lead author Prachi Shah at University of Michigan Health C.S. Mott Children''s Hospital.\n该项研究的主要作者，密歇根大学健康C.S.莫特儿童医院的朴拉琪·沙阿说：“我们的研究结果强调了父母的话语对促进幼儿发展和好奇心的重要性。”\n\nResearchers assessed hours of daily television exposure and frequency of parent screen-time conversation among 1,500 preschoolers, and then measured early childhood curiosity in kindergarten.\n研究人员对1500名学龄前儿童每天看电视的时间和与父母在这段时间内对话的频率进行了评估，然后检测了这些儿童在幼儿园中表现出的好奇心。\n\nThe study specifically focused on curiosity levels, which are associated with enhanced learning and higher academic achievement in reading and math at kindergarten, and behavioral-developmental benefits, especially for children from families with lower socioeconomic status.\n这项研究特别关注好奇心水平，因为好奇心水平与学习能力的提高、幼儿园阅读和数学学习成绩的提高以及行为发育方面的益处有关，尤其是对来自社会经济地位较低家庭的儿童而言。\n\nWhile many young children are now growing up with digital media exposure through mobile devices like tablets and phones, television remains a dominant screen activity, accounting for 72% of all screen time.\n虽然现在许多幼儿在成长过程中通过平板电脑和手机等移动设备与数字媒体进行接触，但屏幕活动的主体仍然是电视，看电视的时间占所有屏幕时间的72%。\n\nTVs are in 98% of all homes, keeping television exposure a relevant developmental context in young children.\n98%的家庭都有电视，这使电视成为了幼儿的相关成长背景。\n\nExcessive media exposure, including television, can displace exploratory activities such as play and parent-child interactions, which are believed to be key to cultivating curiosity in kids.\n过多地接触电视等媒体前可能会取代游戏和亲子互动等探索性活动，而这些活动被认为是培养孩子好奇心的关键。\n\n“Our findings suggest the importance of parents finding opportunities to foster conversational exchanges in daily routines with their young children—including while watching television,” Shah said.\n沙阿说：“我们的研究结果证明了父母在日常生活中——包括看电视时——找机会与年幼的孩子进行对话交流的重要性。”\n\nQuestions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题9到11。\n\n9. What do we learn from a new study about young children?\n9. 从一项关于幼儿的新研究中我们能够了解到什么？\n\n10.What did Prachi Shah''s study specifically focus on?\n10. 朴拉琪·沙阿的研究特别关注的是什么？\n\n11.What does the passage say about excessive media exposure?\n11. 这篇文章对过多接触媒体有何看法？', 136, 1640.31, 457.10, 593.30, 598.47, 615.79, 467.01, 485.77, 'cet6-2024-12-2-g03', 'PASSAGE', '[07:37.10]Passage One｜短文一\n[07:38.83]Increased television time for young children has long been linked with poorer progress in some areas of development.｜长期以来，幼儿看电视时间的增加与某些发育领域的进展较差有关。\n[07:47.01]One possible way to counter those negative effects is talking while watching the screen.｜有一种方法可能减弱这些负面影响，那就是边看电视边对话。\n[07:52.88]As a new study suggests, the more parents engaged in conversation with preschoolers during TV time, the more likely those children were to have higher curiosity levels when they reached kindergarten.｜一项新的研究表明，看电视时，父母在学龄前儿童对话的次数越多，这些孩子在上幼儿园时好奇心水平就越高。\n[08:06.07]This was particularly true for children with socioeconomic disadvantages.｜这对于社会经济地位较低家庭的儿童来说尤其如此。\n[08:12.09]“Our findings reinforce the importance of parent conversation to promote early childhood development and curiosity,” said lead author Prachi Shah at University of Michigan Health C.S. Mott Children''s Hospital.｜该项研究的主要作者，密歇根大学健康C.S.莫特儿童医院的朴拉琪·沙阿说：“我们的研究结果强调了父母的话语对促进幼儿发展和好奇心的重要性。”\n[08:27.95]Researchers assessed hours of daily television exposure and frequency of parent screen-time conversation among 1,500 preschoolers, and then measured early childhood curiosity in kindergarten.｜研究人员对1500名学龄前儿童每天看电视的时间和与父母在这段时间内对话的频率进行了评估，然后检测了这些儿童在幼儿园中表现出的好奇心。\n[08:42.36]The study specifically focused on curiosity levels, which are associated with enhanced learning and higher academic achievement in reading and math at kindergarten, and behavioral-developmental benefits, especially for children from families with lower socioeconomic status.｜这项研究特别关注好奇心水平，因为好奇心水平与学习能力的提高、幼儿园阅读和数学学习成绩的提高以及行为发育方面的益处有关，尤其是对来自社会经济地位较低家庭的儿童而言。\n[09:01.37]While many young children are now growing up with digital media exposure through mobile devices like tablets and phones, television remains a dominant screen activity, accounting for 72% of all screen time.｜虽然现在许多幼儿在成长过程中通过平板电脑和手机等移动设备与数字媒体进行接触，但屏幕活动的主体仍然是电视，看电视的时间占所有屏幕时间的72%。\n[09:16.40]TVs are in 98% of all homes, keeping television exposure a relevant developmental context in young children.｜98%的家庭都有电视，这使电视成为了幼儿的相关成长背景。\n[09:25.35]Excessive media exposure, including television, can displace exploratory activities such as play and parent-child interactions, which are believed to be key to cultivating curiosity in kids.｜过多地接触电视等媒体前可能会取代游戏和亲子互动等探索性活动，而这些活动被认为是培养孩子好奇心的关键。\n[09:39.67]“Our findings suggest the importance of parents finding opportunities to foster conversational exchanges in daily routines with their young children—including while watching television,” Shah said.｜沙阿说：“我们的研究结果证明了父母在日常生活中——包括看电视时——找机会与年幼的孩子进行对话交流的重要性。”\n[09:53.30]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题9到11。\n[09:58.47]9. What do we learn from a new study about young children?｜9. 从一项关于幼儿的新研究中我们能够了解到什么？\n[10:16.09]10.What did Prachi Shah''s study specifically focus on?｜10. 朴拉琪·沙阿的研究特别关注的是什么？\n[10:35.61]11.What does the passage say about excessive media exposure?｜11. 这篇文章对过多接触媒体有何看法？', NOW(), NOW()
