SELECT
  @a_cet6_2024_12_2_22, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2024-12-2.mp3', 'Recording Three\n讲座三\n\nWe all know that the sense of smell is powerful.\n我们都知道，嗅觉是很强大的。\n\nThe delicious scent of bread, for example, can act like a time machine.\n例如，面包的香味就像一台时光机。\n\nOne sniff can bring back a memory from many years ago.\n闻一闻就能唤起多年前的记忆。\n\nIndeed, scent particles, in general, can revive memories that have been long forgotten.\n事实上，通常来说，气味微粒是可以唤起被遗忘已久的记忆的。\n\nBut why do smells sometimes trigger powerful memories, especially emotional ones?\n但是，为什么气味有时会引发强烈的记忆，特别是情感记忆呢？\n\nThe short answer is that the brain regions that handle smells, memories and emotions are linked.\n简而言之，（那是因为）处理气味、记忆和情绪的大脑区域是相互关联的。\n\nA scent is a chemical particle that floats in through the nose and into the region of the brain responsible for smell, where the sensation is first processed into a form that the brain can read.\n气味是一种化学微粒，它穿过鼻腔飘入到大脑中负责嗅觉的区域，在那里，这种感官先是被处理成大脑可以读取的形式。\n\nBrain cells then carry that information to the area of the brain where emotions are processed, and then to an adjacent area of the brain where learning and memory formation take place.\n然后，脑细胞将这些信息传递到大脑中处理情绪的区域，再传递到大脑中邻近的区域，在那里进行学习和形成记忆。\n\nScents are the only sensations that travel such a direct path to the emotional and memory centers of the brain.\n气味是唯一能直达大脑情感和记忆中枢的感官。\n\nThat results in an intimate connection between emotions, memories and scents, which is why memories triggered by scents are experienced as more emotional than those triggered by sight or sound.\n它引发了情绪、记忆和气味之间的密切联系，这就是气味引发的记忆比景象或声音引发的记忆更有感染力的原因。\n\nLet''s take a closer look at how those memories are activated.\n让我们仔细看看这些记忆是如何被激活的。\n\nUsually, when a person smells something that''s connected to a meaningful event in their past, they''ll first have an emotional response to the sensation and then a memory might follow.\n通常，当一个人闻到与过去有意义的事件相关联的味道时，他们首先会对这种知觉产生情绪反应，然后可能会引发回忆。\n\nBut sometimes, the memory doesn''t come to the surface.\n但有时回忆不会浮现。\n\nThe person might feel the emotion of something that happened in the past but won''t remember what they experienced.\n这个人可能会感受到与过去发生的某件事情相关的情绪，但不会记得自己经历了什么。\n\nWhat causes this baffling phenomenon? The explanation is context.\n是什么导致了这种令人困惑的现象呢？答案是“情境”。\n\nImagine this scenario—a person is walking down the street smelling a scent that they first encountered decades ago, which activates an emotional response.\n想象一下这样的场景，一个人走在街上，闻到了几十年前第一次闻到过的气味，这激活了他的情绪反应。\n\nIf the person had first come across that smell in a very different context, it will be much more difficult to recover the associated memory, because the brain uses the context to give meaning to the information and find that memory.\n如果这个人第一次闻到这种气味是在完全不同的情境下，那么要找回与之相关的记忆就会困难得多，因为大脑是利用情境来赋予信息意义并找到那段记忆的。\n\nThe special nature of memories brought back by scents notwithstanding, they have the same drawbacks as other memories.\n尽管由气味唤起的记忆具有特殊性，但它与其他记忆一样有缺点。\n\nThey aren''t always accurate, and they can change over time.\n它们并不总是准确的，而且会随着时间的推移而改变。\n\nIn fact, each time we remember something, that memory can become distorted.\n事实上，每次我们回忆起某事时，那段记忆都会变得扭曲。\n\nBut, because of the strong emotional associations these memories evoke, people who remember something due to a scent are often convinced that the memories are accurate, even when they aren''t.\n但是，由于这些回忆可以唤起强烈的情感联系，那些因为气味而记住某些事物的人往往会相信这些记忆是准确的，虽然事实并非如此。\n\nQuestions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题22至25。\n\n22. What question does the speaker try to answer in this talk?\n22. 讲话者试图在讲座中回答什么问题？\n\n23.What is said to result in an intimate connection between emotions, memories and scents?\n23. 是什么导致了情感、记忆和气味之间的密切联系？\n\n24.What does the brain use to give meaning to information and find a memory?\n24. 大脑是用什么来给信息赋予意义并找到记忆的？\n\n25.What drawback do memories of scents share with other memories?\n25. 气味记忆与其他记忆的共同缺点是什么？', 169, 1640.31, 1378.11, 1546.97, 1552.90, 1571.51, 1399.99, 1405.74, 'cet6-2024-12-2-g07', 'LECTURE', '[22:58.11]Recording Three｜讲座三\n[23:01.47]We all know that the sense of smell is powerful.｜我们都知道，嗅觉是很强大的。\n[23:04.85]The delicious scent of bread, for example, can act like a time machine.｜例如，面包的香味就像一台时光机。\n[23:09.33]One sniff can bring back a memory from many years ago.｜闻一闻就能唤起多年前的记忆。\n[23:13.57]Indeed, scent particles, in general, can revive memories that have been long forgotten.｜事实上，通常来说，气味微粒是可以唤起被遗忘已久的记忆的。\n[23:19.99]But why do smells sometimes trigger powerful memories, especially emotional ones?｜但是，为什么气味有时会引发强烈的记忆，特别是情感记忆呢？\n[23:26.04]The short answer is that the brain regions that handle smells, memories and emotions are linked.｜简而言之，（那是因为）处理气味、记忆和情绪的大脑区域是相互关联的。\n[23:33.34]A scent is a chemical particle that floats in through the nose and into the region of the brain responsible for smell, where the sensation is first processed into a form that the brain can read.｜气味是一种化学微粒，它穿过鼻腔飘入到大脑中负责嗅觉的区域，在那里，这种感官先是被处理成大脑可以读取的形式。\n[23:46.13]Brain cells then carry that information to the area of the brain where emotions are processed, and then to an adjacent area of the brain where learning and memory formation take place.｜然后，脑细胞将这些信息传递到大脑中处理情绪的区域，再传递到大脑中邻近的区域，在那里进行学习和形成记忆。\n[23:58.38]Scents are the only sensations that travel such a direct path to the emotional and memory centers of the brain.｜气味是唯一能直达大脑情感和记忆中枢的感官。\n[24:05.84]That results in an intimate connection between emotions, memories and scents, which is why memories triggered by scents are experienced as more emotional than those triggered by sight or sound.｜它引发了情绪、记忆和气味之间的密切联系，这就是气味引发的记忆比景象或声音引发的记忆更有感染力的原因。\n[24:19.16]Let''s take a closer look at how those memories are activated.｜让我们仔细看看这些记忆是如何被激活的。\n[24:24.01]Usually, when a person smells something that''s connected to a meaningful event in their past, they''ll first have an emotional response to the sensation and then a memory might follow.｜通常，当一个人闻到与过去有意义的事件相关联的味道时，他们首先会对这种知觉产生情绪反应，然后可能会引发回忆。\n[24:35.86]But sometimes, the memory doesn''t come to the surface.｜但有时回忆不会浮现。\n[24:39.69]The person might feel the emotion of something that happened in the past but won''t remember what they experienced.｜这个人可能会感受到与过去发生的某件事情相关的情绪，但不会记得自己经历了什么。\n[24:46.28]What causes this baffling phenomenon? The explanation is context.｜是什么导致了这种令人困惑的现象呢？答案是“情境”。\n[24:51.97]Imagine this scenario—a person is walking down the street smelling a scent that they first encountered decades ago, which activates an emotional response.｜想象一下这样的场景，一个人走在街上，闻到了几十年前第一次闻到过的气味，这激活了他的情绪反应。\n[25:02.31]If the person had first come across that smell in a very different context, it will be much more difficult to recover the associated memory, because the brain uses the context to give meaning to the information and find that memory.｜如果这个人第一次闻到这种气味是在完全不同的情境下，那么要找回与之相关的记忆就会困难得多，因为大脑是利用情境来赋予信息意义并找到那段记忆的。\n[25:16.76]The special nature of memories brought back by scents notwithstanding, they have the same drawbacks as other memories.｜尽管由气味唤起的记忆具有特殊性，但它与其他记忆一样有缺点。\n[25:24.85]They aren''t always accurate, and they can change over time.｜它们并不总是准确的，而且会随着时间的推移而改变。\n[25:28.42]In fact, each time we remember something, that memory can become distorted.｜事实上，每次我们回忆起某事时，那段记忆都会变得扭曲。\n[25:34.53]But, because of the strong emotional associations these memories evoke, people who remember something due to a scent are often convinced that the memories are accurate, even when they aren''t.｜但是，由于这些回忆可以唤起强烈的情感联系，那些因为气味而记住某些事物的人往往会相信这些记忆是准确的，虽然事实并非如此。\n[25:46.97]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题22至25。\n[25:52.90]22. What question does the speaker try to answer in this talk?｜22. 讲话者试图在讲座中回答什么问题？\n[26:11.81]23.What is said to result in an intimate connection between emotions, memories and scents?｜23. 是什么导致了情感、记忆和气味之间的密切联系？\n[26:34.83]24.What does the brain use to give meaning to information and find a memory?｜24. 大脑是用什么来给信息赋予意义并找到记忆的？\n[26:54.33]25.What drawback do memories of scents share with other memories?｜25. 气味记忆与其他记忆的共同缺点是什么？', NOW(), NOW()
