SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_06_1_10, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-06-1.mp3', 'Passage One\n短文一\n\nWhen a person tries to access a memory, their brains quickly examine everything stored in it to find the relevant information.\n当一个人尝试获取一段记忆时，他们的大脑会快速检查存储的所有信息以寻找相关内容。\n\nBut as we age, many of us have difficulty retrieving memories.\n但随着我们年龄增长，许多人的记忆检索会出现困难。\n\nIn a review published in the journal Trends in Cognitive Sciences on February 11th, researchers propose an explanation for why this might be happening:\n2月11日发表于《认知科学趋势》期刊的一篇综述中，研究人员对此现象提出了解释：\n\nthe brains of older adults allocate more space to accumulated knowledge and have more material to navigate when attempting to access memories.\n老年人的大脑为累积知识分配了更多存储空间，导致检索记忆时需要筛选更多材料。\n\nWhile this wealth of prior knowledge can make memory retrieval challenging, the researchers say it has its upsides—this life experience can aid with creativity and decision-making.\n尽管这种丰富的先前知识可能会使记忆提取更具挑战性，但研究人员说这有它的优点——这些人生经验能提升创造力和决策力。\n\nResearchers Tarek Amer, Jordana Wynn and Lynn Hasher looked at several behavioral and brain imaging studies, which show that older adults have difficulty suppressing information that is no longer relevant and that when searching for a specific memory, they often retrieve other, irrelevant memories along with it.\n研究人员塔里克·阿梅尔、乔丹娜·韦恩和林恩·哈希尔查阅了多项行为学与脑成像研究，发现老年人难以抑制无关信息，且在搜索特定记忆时常常连带检索出其他的，不相关记忆。\n\nThe studies also showed that when given a cognitive task, older adults rely more heavily on previous knowledge than younger adults do.\n研究还表明，执行认知任务时，老年人比年轻人更依赖既往知识。\n\nWhile the researchers focus primarily on the difficulties that these disorderly crowded memories may pose, they also highlight a few situations in which these same memory patterns may be useful.\n虽然研究主要关注这种混乱拥挤的记忆模式可能造成的困难，但他们还强调了这些相同的记忆模式可能有用的一些情况。\n\n“Evidence suggests that older adults show preserved, and at times enhanced, creativity as a function of enriched memories,” the researchers write.\n研究人员写道：“有证据表明，由于记忆丰富，老年人的创造力得以保持，有时还得到了增强。”\n\nThey further hypothesize that older adults may be well served by their prior knowledge when it comes to decision-making, where they can pull on their accumulated wisdom.\n他们进一步推测，在需要运用经验智慧的决策场景中，老年人的先前的知识可能成为显著优势。\n\nQuestions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题9到11。\n\n9. What did the researchers do in their review in the journal Trends in Cognitive Sciences?\n9. 研究人员在《认知科学趋势》期刊的综述中做了什么？\n\n10.What do several behavioral and brain imaging studies show?\n10. 多项行为学与脑成像研究表明了什么？\n\n11.What does evidence suggest regarding older adults?\n11. 关于老年人，证据表明了什么？', 117, 1557.69, 449.55, 566.30, 590.98, 609.34, 497.65, 520.63, 'cet6-2025-06-1-g03', 'PASSAGE', '[07:29.55]Passage One｜短文一\n[07:31.27]When a person tries to access a memory, their brains quickly examine everything stored in it to find the relevant information.｜当一个人尝试获取一段记忆时，他们的大脑会快速检查存储的所有信息以寻找相关内容。\n[07:39.86]But as we age, many of us have difficulty retrieving memories.｜但随着我们年龄增长，许多人的记忆检索会出现困难。\n[07:44.65]In a review published in the journal Trends in Cognitive Sciences on February 11th, researchers propose an explanation for why this might be happening:｜2月11日发表于《认知科学趋势》期刊的一篇综述中，研究人员对此现象提出了解释：\n[07:55.58]the brains of older adults allocate more space to accumulated knowledge and have more material to navigate when attempting to access memories.｜老年人的大脑为累积知识分配了更多存储空间，导致检索记忆时需要筛选更多材料。\n[08:05.61]While this wealth of prior knowledge can make memory retrieval challenging, the researchers say it has its upsides—this life experience can aid with creativity and decision-making.｜尽管这种丰富的先前知识可能会使记忆提取更具挑战性，但研究人员说这有它的优点——这些人生经验能提升创造力和决策力。\n[08:17.65]Researchers Tarek Amer, Jordana Wynn and Lynn Hasher looked at several behavioral and brain imaging studies, which show that older adults have difficulty suppressing information that is no longer relevant and that when searching for a specific memory, they often retrieve other, irrelevant memories along with it.｜研究人员塔里克·阿梅尔、乔丹娜·韦恩和林恩·哈希尔查阅了多项行为学与脑成像研究，发现老年人难以抑制无关信息，且在搜索特定记忆时常常连带检索出其他的，不相关记忆。\n[08:40.93]The studies also showed that when given a cognitive task, older adults rely more heavily on previous knowledge than younger adults do.｜研究还表明，执行认知任务时，老年人比年轻人更依赖既往知识。\n[08:50.66]While the researchers focus primarily on the difficulties that these disorderly crowded memories may pose, they also highlight a few situations in which these same memory patterns may be useful.｜虽然研究主要关注这种混乱拥挤的记忆模式可能造成的困难，但他们还强调了这些相同的记忆模式可能有用的一些情况。\n[09:04.07]“Evidence suggests that older adults show preserved, and at times enhanced, creativity as a function of enriched memories,” the researchers write.｜研究人员写道：“有证据表明，由于记忆丰富，老年人的创造力得以保持，有时还得到了增强。”\n[09:14.93]They further hypothesize that older adults may be well served by their prior knowledge when it comes to decision-making, where they can pull on their accumulated wisdom.｜他们进一步推测，在需要运用经验智慧的决策场景中，老年人的先前的知识可能成为显著优势。\n[09:26.30]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题9到11。\n[09:31.51]9. What did the researchers do in their review in the journal Trends in Cognitive Sciences?｜9. 研究人员在《认知科学趋势》期刊的综述中做了什么？\n[09:50.98]10.What do several behavioral and brain imaging studies show?｜10. 多项行为学与脑成像研究表明了什么？\n[10:09.64]11.What does evidence suggest regarding older adults?｜11. 关于老年人，证据表明了什么？', NOW(), NOW()
