  (`question_id`, `audio_url`, `transcript`, `duration_seconds`, `audio_total_seconds`, `audio_start_seconds`, `audio_end_seconds`, `question_start_seconds`, `question_end_seconds`, `answer_start_seconds`, `answer_end_seconds`, `group_key`, `listening_type`, `lrc_content`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_06_1_16, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-06-1.mp3', 'Recording One\n录音一\n\nRecent studies show that self-control and persistence increase academic outcomes independent of IQ.\n最新研究表明，自控力与毅力能提升学业表现，而这与智商无关。\n\nEven our personal beliefs about effort can affect academic outcomes.\n我们对努力的个人信念甚至可以影响学业成果。\n\nChildren who think effort leads to achievement outperform those who believe ability is a fixed trait.\n相信努力带来成就的孩子，表现优于那些认为能力是固定特质的孩子。\n\nGiven the link between persistence and academic success, decisions about effort are particularly important in childhood.\n鉴于毅力与学业成功的关联，关于努力的选择在童年阶段尤为重要。\n\nYet, relatively little research has explored how young children learn what''s worth the effort.\n然而，关于幼儿如何学习何事值得努力的研究却相对匮乏。\n\nWe all know that infants are keen observers of the social world.\n众所周知，婴儿是社会世界的敏锐观察者。\n\nBut they''re not just idly watching; they are tiny learning machines.\n但他们并非被动观看；他们是高效的小型学习机器。\n\nThey can generalize such abstract concepts as causal relationships and social roles from just a few examples.\n他们能从少量例子中归纳因果关系、社会角色等抽象概念。\n\nEven a 15-month-old infant can outperform a high-level computer in such tasks.\n即使是15个月大的婴儿，在此类任务中的表现也能超越高性能计算机。\n\nCould infants also make broad, generalizable inferences from a few examples when it comes to effort?\n那么面对努力时，婴儿是否也能从有限示例中做出广泛推论？\n\nIf so, then maybe persistence isn''t simply a character trait.\n若真如此，那么或许毅力并非固定性格特质。\n\nMaybe it''s flexible and adaptable based on social context.\n也许它是能根据社会环境灵活调整的。\n\nTo explore this question, we showed 15-month-old babies one of two things: an experimenter working hard to achieve two different goals, or an experimenter who effortlessly reached each goal.\n为探究这个问题，我们向15个月大的婴儿展示两种场景之中的一个场景：一个实验者努力达成两个不同目标，或一个实验者轻松达成每个目标。\n\nThen we introduced the baby to a novel music toy that seemed as if it could be activated by pushing a big button on top.\n我们随后向婴儿展示一个看似通过按下顶部大按钮激活的音乐玩具。\n\nThe button could be pressed down but didn''t actually activate anything.\n该按钮可按下但实际无法激活任何东西。\n\nOut of sight of the babies, we turned on the music toy with a hidden button so that they heard the toy making music.\n在婴儿视线外，我们通过隐藏按钮启动音乐玩具，让他们听见玩具播放音乐。\n\nWe gave the babies the music toy and left the room.\n我们将玩具交给婴儿，然后离开房间。\n\nThen analysts, who didn''t know which condition each baby was in, watched videotapes of the experiment and counted how many times babies tried to activate the toy by pressing the button.\n然后，不知道每个婴儿处于哪种情况的分析人员观看了实验的录像带，并计算了婴儿试图通过按按钮激活玩具的次数。\n\nAcross one study and a preregistered duplication, babies who had seen an adult persist and succeed pushed the button about twice as many times as those who saw an adult effortlessly succeed.\n经过一项研究和预先注册的重复研究中，观看成年人坚持并成功场景的婴儿，其按压按钮的次数是观看成年人轻松成功场景组婴儿的两倍。\n\nIn other words, babies learned that effort was valuable after watching just two examples of an adult working hard and succeeding.\n换句话说，婴儿仅通过两个成年人努力取得成功的例子，就领悟到努力的价值。\n\nPart of what''s exciting about this finding is that the babies didn''t just imitate the adult''s actions; instead, they generalized the value of effort to a novel task.\n这一发现令人兴奋的部分原因在于婴儿并非简单模仿成年人的动作，相反，他们将努力的价值类推到新任务中。\n\nThe experimenter never demonstrated pushing a button or trying to make music.\n实验者从未演示过按压按钮或尝试播放音乐。\n\nInstead the babies learned from different examples of effortful actions that the new toy probably also required persistence.\n而婴儿却从不同的努力行为中推断新玩具可能同样需要坚持。\n\nQuestions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n\n16. What does the speaker say even our personal beliefs about effort can do?\n16. 讲话者说，我们对努力的个人信念甚至可以做什么？\n\n17.How did the researchers turn on the music toy in their experiment?\n17. 研究人员在他们的实验中是如何打开音乐玩具的？\n\n18.What could the infants do in the experiment according to the speaker?\n18. 根据讲话者所述，在实验中，婴儿可以做什么？', 181, 1557.69, 845.75, 1026.94, 1032.65, 1051.40, 855.54, 866.87, 'cet6-2025-06-1-g05', 'LECTURE', '[14:05.75]Recording One｜录音一\n[14:08.02]Recent studies show that self-control and persistence increase academic outcomes independent of IQ.｜最新研究表明，自控力与毅力能提升学业表现，而这与智商无关。\n[14:15.54]Even our personal beliefs about effort can affect academic outcomes.｜我们对努力的个人信念甚至可以影响学业成果。\n[14:20.19]Children who think effort leads to achievement outperform those who believe ability is a fixed trait.｜相信努力带来成就的孩子，表现优于那些认为能力是固定特质的孩子。\n[14:27.17]Given the link between persistence and academic success, decisions about effort are particularly important in childhood.｜鉴于毅力与学业成功的关联，关于努力的选择在童年阶段尤为重要。\n[14:35.67]Yet, relatively little research has explored how young children learn what''s worth the effort.｜然而，关于幼儿如何学习何事值得努力的研究却相对匮乏。\n[14:42.71]We all know that infants are keen observers of the social world.｜众所周知，婴儿是社会世界的敏锐观察者。\n[14:46.84]But they''re not just idly watching; they are tiny learning machines.｜但他们并非被动观看；他们是高效的小型学习机器。\n[14:52.00]They can generalize such abstract concepts as causal relationships and social roles from just a few examples.｜他们能从少量例子中归纳因果关系、社会角色等抽象概念。\n[15:00.14]Even a 15-month-old infant can outperform a high-level computer in such tasks.｜即使是15个月大的婴儿，在此类任务中的表现也能超越高性能计算机。\n[15:06.30]Could infants also make broad, generalizable inferences from a few examples when it comes to effort?｜那么面对努力时，婴儿是否也能从有限示例中做出广泛推论？\n[15:13.06]If so, then maybe persistence isn''t simply a character trait.｜若真如此，那么或许毅力并非固定性格特质。\n[15:17.93]Maybe it''s flexible and adaptable based on social context.｜也许它是能根据社会环境灵活调整的。\n[15:23.94]To explore this question, we showed 15-month-old babies one of two things: an experimenter working hard to achieve two different goals, or an experimenter who effortlessly reached each goal.｜为探究这个问题，我们向15个月大的婴儿展示两种场景之中的一个场景：一个实验者努力达成两个不同目标，或一个实验者轻松达成每个目标。\n[15:38.12]Then we introduced the baby to a novel music toy that seemed as if it could be activated by pushing a big button on top.｜我们随后向婴儿展示一个看似通过按下顶部大按钮激活的音乐玩具。\n[15:46.97]The button could be pressed down but didn''t actually activate anything.｜该按钮可按下但实际无法激活任何东西。\n[15:52.36]Out of sight of the babies, we turned on the music toy with a hidden button so that they heard the toy making music.｜在婴儿视线外，我们通过隐藏按钮启动音乐玩具，让他们听见玩具播放音乐。\n[16:00.09]We gave the babies the music toy and left the room.｜我们将玩具交给婴儿，然后离开房间。\n[16:04.32]Then analysts, who didn''t know which condition each baby was in, watched videotapes of the experiment and counted how many times babies tried to activate the toy by pressing the button.｜然后，不知道每个婴儿处于哪种情况的分析人员观看了实验的录像带，并计算了婴儿试图通过按按钮激活玩具的次数。\n[16:16.75]Across one study and a preregistered duplication, babies who had seen an adult persist and succeed pushed the button about twice as many times as those who saw an adult effortlessly succeed.｜经过一项研究和预先注册的重复研究中，观看成年人坚持并成功场景的婴儿，其按压按钮的次数是观看成年人轻松成功场景组婴儿的两倍。\n[16:30.92]In other words, babies learned that effort was valuable after watching just two examples of an adult working hard and succeeding.｜换句话说，婴儿仅通过两个成年人努力取得成功的例子，就领悟到努力的价值。\n[16:40.60]Part of what''s exciting about this finding is that the babies didn''t just imitate the adult''s actions; instead, they generalized the value of effort to a novel task.｜这一发现令人兴奋的部分原因在于婴儿并非简单模仿成年人的动作，相反，他们将努力的价值类推到新任务中。\n[16:51.98]The experimenter never demonstrated pushing a button or trying to make music.｜实验者从未演示过按压按钮或尝试播放音乐。\n[16:57.50]Instead the babies learned from different examples of effortful actions that the new toy probably also required persistence.｜而婴儿却从不同的努力行为中推断新玩具可能同样需要坚持。\n[17:06.94]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n[17:12.65]16. What does the speaker say even our personal beliefs about effort can do?｜16. 讲话者说，我们对努力的个人信念甚至可以做什么？\n[17:31.70]17.How did the researchers turn on the music toy in their experiment?｜17. 研究人员在他们的实验中是如何打开音乐玩具的？\n[17:50.89]18.What could the infants do in the experiment according to the speaker?｜18. 根据讲话者所述，在实验中，婴儿可以做什么？', NOW(), NOW()
