SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_06_1_19, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-06-1.mp3', 'Recording Two\n录音二\n\nIf chronic lateness has taken over your life and turned into a defining trait of who you are, it''s likely that you''re losing out on job offers, recreational opportunities, friendships, and more.\n若长期迟到已成为你的生活常态甚至个人标签，你可能正在错失工作机会、娱乐机会、友谊，甚至更多。\n\nIf you are showing up late to your own life, get organized, prioritize punctuality and address any underlying causes.\n当你连自己的人生都姗姗来迟时，就必须学会规划时间、重视守时，并解决任何潜在原因。\n\nWhen you have to be somewhere, try to arrive 15 to 30 minutes before you need to.\n当你必须去某个地方时，尝试提前15至30分钟到达。\n\nIf you always run late, that means you must be failing to account for something you always do.\n若你总是迟到，说明你必然漏算了某些固定环节。\n\nAnticipate that by giving yourself extra time to arrive.\n预留额外时间才能规避这种情况。\n\nKeep track of whether or not you actually do arrive early.\n记录下你是否真的提前到了。\n\nYou might find that leaving early gets you to places exactly on time.\n你会发现提前出发往往才能准时抵达。\n\nSet two alarms. Set one alarm that lets you know it''s time to drop what you''re doing, and a second alarm that means you need to walk out the door.\n设置两个闹钟。第一个提醒你该停止手头事务，第二个意味着必须出门。\n\nObey the alarms. As soon as the first alarm goes off, stop what you''re doing.\n务必遵守闹钟。第一个闹铃响后立即停下手头工作。\n\nIf it''s something you''ll be coming back to, like a project for work, make a note to remind yourself where you left off.\n若是你要回来继续做的事情，如工作项目，做个标记提醒自己在哪里停下了。\n\nGrab everything you need and make sure you know how to get where you''re going.\n带上所需物品并确认你知道如何到达目的地。\n\nAim to be out the door and on your way before the second alarm rings.\n目标是在第二个闹钟响起前出门。\n\nThis will only work if you take the alarms seriously, so make sure you react as soon as you hear them.\n此方法奏效的前提是你认真对待闹钟，所以必须做到闻声即动。\n\nPrepare for the day ahead. Have all of your notes and materials organized well in advance of each event so that all you have to do is grab them and go when it''s the actual day.\n提前筹备日程。为每个事项预先整理好所有资料，确保当日拿起来就能走。\n\nIf mornings are a struggle for you, do as much as you can to ready yourself the night before.\n如果你清晨总是手忙脚乱，那么在前一夜就应该尽可能多地做好准备。\n\nBefore you go to bed, lay out your clothes and pack your bag for the next day.\n睡前搭配好次日着装，收拾妥当随身物品。\n\nLearn to leave time in between tasks and meetings.\n学会在任务和会议间留出时间。\n\nIt soon becomes unbearable to shift between one meeting and another without space in between.\n连续会议无缝切换很快会令人不堪重负。\n\nIf you over schedule yourself, however, you''ll arrive late as soon as you hit a bump.\n不管怎样，如果你把日程安排得过满，一旦遇到波折就会迟到。\n\nAs well as giving you space, this time also acts as a buffer between events which can be “borrowed” from should you be held up in a prior meeting, allowing you to still get to the next one on time.\n除了给你空间，这段时间还可以作为事件之间的缓冲，如果你在之前的会议中被耽搁了，你可以借用一下，让你仍然可以按时到达下一个会议。\n\nCalculate transit time between activities, and then add 10 to 30 minutes for unexpected delays.\n计算活动间的过渡时间，然后额外预留10至30分钟应对突发延误。\n\nQuestions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n\n19. What is the speaker''s purpose of giving this talk?\n19. 讲话者做此演讲的目的是什么？\n\n20.What should you do if mornings are a struggle for you?\n20. 如果清晨总是手忙脚乱，你应该怎么做？\n\n21.According to the speaker, what can we do to combat unexpected delays?\n21. 根据讲话者，我们应该做什么来应对突发的延误？', 157, 1557.69, 1089.76, 1246.69, 1252.45, 1269.24, 1091.59, 1105.70, 'cet6-2025-06-1-g06', 'LECTURE', '[18:09.76]Recording Two｜录音二\n[18:11.59]If chronic lateness has taken over your life and turned into a defining trait of who you are, it''s likely that you''re losing out on job offers, recreational opportunities, friendships, and more.｜若长期迟到已成为你的生活常态甚至个人标签，你可能正在错失工作机会、娱乐机会、友谊，甚至更多。\n[18:26.00]If you are showing up late to your own life, get organized, prioritize punctuality and address any underlying causes.｜当你连自己的人生都姗姗来迟时，就必须学会规划时间、重视守时，并解决任何潜在原因。\n[18:34.88]When you have to be somewhere, try to arrive 15 to 30 minutes before you need to.｜当你必须去某个地方时，尝试提前15至30分钟到达。\n[18:41.31]If you always run late, that means you must be failing to account for something you always do.｜若你总是迟到，说明你必然漏算了某些固定环节。\n[18:47.69]Anticipate that by giving yourself extra time to arrive.｜预留额外时间才能规避这种情况。\n[18:52.01]Keep track of whether or not you actually do arrive early.｜记录下你是否真的提前到了。\n[18:56.49]You might find that leaving early gets you to places exactly on time.｜你会发现提前出发往往才能准时抵达。\n[19:02.02]Set two alarms. Set one alarm that lets you know it''s time to drop what you''re doing, and a second alarm that means you need to walk out the door.｜设置两个闹钟。第一个提醒你该停止手头事务，第二个意味着必须出门。\n[19:12.42]Obey the alarms. As soon as the first alarm goes off, stop what you''re doing.｜务必遵守闹钟。第一个闹铃响后立即停下手头工作。\n[19:18.40]If it''s something you''ll be coming back to, like a project for work, make a note to remind yourself where you left off.｜若是你要回来继续做的事情，如工作项目，做个标记提醒自己在哪里停下了。\n[19:25.30]Grab everything you need and make sure you know how to get where you''re going.｜带上所需物品并确认你知道如何到达目的地。\n[19:30.34]Aim to be out the door and on your way before the second alarm rings.｜目标是在第二个闹钟响起前出门。\n[19:35.64]This will only work if you take the alarms seriously, so make sure you react as soon as you hear them.｜此方法奏效的前提是你认真对待闹钟，所以必须做到闻声即动。\n[19:43.47]Prepare for the day ahead. Have all of your notes and materials organized well in advance of each event so that all you have to do is grab them and go when it''s the actual day.｜提前筹备日程。为每个事项预先整理好所有资料，确保当日拿起来就能走。\n[19:56.08]If mornings are a struggle for you, do as much as you can to ready yourself the night before.｜如果你清晨总是手忙脚乱，那么在前一夜就应该尽可能多地做好准备。\n[20:02.55]Before you go to bed, lay out your clothes and pack your bag for the next day.｜睡前搭配好次日着装，收拾妥当随身物品。\n[20:08.06]Learn to leave time in between tasks and meetings.｜学会在任务和会议间留出时间。\n[20:12.04]It soon becomes unbearable to shift between one meeting and another without space in between.｜连续会议无缝切换很快会令人不堪重负。\n[20:18.90]If you over schedule yourself, however, you''ll arrive late as soon as you hit a bump.｜不管怎样，如果你把日程安排得过满，一旦遇到波折就会迟到。\n[20:25.16]As well as giving you space, this time also acts as a buffer between events which can be “borrowed” from should you be held up in a prior meeting, allowing you to still get to the next one on time.｜除了给你空间，这段时间还可以作为事件之间的缓冲，如果你在之前的会议中被耽搁了，你可以借用一下，让你仍然可以按时到达下一个会议。\n[20:38.37]Calculate transit time between activities, and then add 10 to 30 minutes for unexpected delays.｜计算活动间的过渡时间，然后额外预留10至30分钟应对突发延误。\n[20:46.69]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n[20:52.45]19. What is the speaker''s purpose of giving this talk?｜19. 讲话者做此演讲的目的是什么？\n[21:09.54]20.What should you do if mornings are a struggle for you?｜20. 如果清晨总是手忙脚乱，你应该怎么做？\n[21:27.83]21.According to the speaker, what can we do to combat unexpected delays?｜21. 根据讲话者，我们应该做什么来应对突发的延误？', NOW(), NOW()
