SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_06_1_22, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-06-1.mp3', 'Recording Three\n录音三\n\nDo you think Bostonians put out the welcome mat?\n你认为波士顿人会欢迎你吗？\n\nDo you find other cities to be more or less polite?\n你觉得其他城市更讲礼貌还是更没礼貌？\n\nDifferent cities have different feels for visitors.\n不同城市会给游客留下截然不同的印象。\n\nSome are friendly and open; some are formal and distant.\n有的友善开朗，有的刻板疏离。\n\nBoston tends to seem hurried and sometimes a little rude.\n波士顿往往给人匆忙急躁，有时略显粗鲁的感觉。\n\nThat''s what some tourists have said.\n这是许多游客都提到过的。\n\nOne study found that people here are less willing to volunteer to help strangers than those residents of other US cities.\n一项研究发现，相比美国其他城市居民，波士顿人更不愿意主动帮助陌生人。\n\nTake a close look next time you''re in Boston, and here are some of the things you''ll probably see:\n下次造访波士顿时仔细观察，这里是一些你可能会看到的场景：\n\npeople smoking in the subway stations despite the frequent reminders not to do so;\n地铁站里对频繁出现的禁烟标识视若无睹的吸烟者；\n\npedestrians walking when the crosswalk lights say not to do so, ignoring the cars;\n人行横道红灯时依然穿行、无视来往车辆的行人；\n\ncar drivers speeding, making a loud noise with the horn and occasionally cursing at each other;\n鸣笛震天、偶尔互相咒骂的超速司机；\n\ndrivers who can''t be bothered to find a place to park, but instead, simply turn on their blinker and stop in the middle of the street.\n懒得寻找车位、直接打双闪停在路中央的司机。\n\nSometimes Bostonians seem like they''re just too busy to be polite.\n有时波士顿人看起来像是忙得根本顾不上礼节。\n\nWhy is their city like that?\n为什么他们的城市是那样的？\n\nFormer US representative Chester Atkins says it''s because the Puritan founding fathers were themselves impatient and bad-tempered, and had little time for courtesy.\n前国会议员切斯特·阿特金斯认为，这是因为清教徒创立者们自身就没耐心且脾气不好，无暇讲究客套。\n\nThat culture has carried down to the present day, he believes.\n他认为这种文化延续至今。\n\nBoston College professor Thomas O''Connor thinks a contributing factor is Boston''s history as a city where each new group of immigrants was treated with suspicion and exclusion.\n波士顿学院的托马斯·奥康纳教授认为，一个促成因素是波士顿作为一个城市的历史，历史上每批新移民在此都遭遇猜忌排斥。\n\nThat''s given Boston a sense of clannishness and reserve, he thinks.\n他认为这给了波士顿一种排外、保守的感觉。\n\n“The idea of a stranger greeting another stranger is simply not part of the local culture,” he says.“People will begin a conversation with someone they know, but not with a stranger.”\n他解释道：“陌生人之间互相问候根本不属于本地文化，人们只愿意与认识的人而不愿意与陌生人交谈。”\n\nRalph Whitehead, a professor of journalism at the University of Massachusetts, at Amherst, thinks it''s because Boston''s upper class used manners to make class distinctions.\n马萨诸塞大学阿默斯特分校的拉尔夫·怀特海德教授认为，这是因为波士顿上流社会曾用繁文缛节标榜阶级差异。\n\n“By being snobbish about manners, Proper Bostonians made other people scornful of courtesy,” he says.\n他说正统波士顿人对礼节过分挑剔的做法反而让其他人鄙视礼仪。\n\nSome say that''s just the way Boston is, and that the city will never change.\n有人说这就是波士顿的底色，这个城市永远无法改变。\n\nStill, other cities with reputations for worse rudeness are trying hard to change their images.\n但那些以粗鲁著称更甚的城市正在努力改头换面。\n\nParis is one example where people are trying to be friendly.\n巴黎就是一个开始努力变得友好的例子。\n\nNew York City is another—there the city is trying to persuade subway riders to be more considerate.\n纽约是另一个——该市正努力劝说地铁乘客更加体贴。\n\nIf those cities can make the effort, maybe Boston should as well.\n要是这些城市都能做出努力，波士顿或许也可以。\n\nQuestions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题22到25。\n\n22. What do visitors find about the cities they''ve been to?\n22. 关于自己去过的城市，游客有何发现？\n\n23.What did one study find about Bostonians compared with residents of other US cities?\n23. 关于波士顿人，相比美国其他城市的居民，一项研究发现了什么？\n\n24.What does Thomas O''Connor say about the idea of strangers greeting one another?\n24. 关于陌生人互相问候的想法，托马斯·奥康纳说了什么？\n\n25.What does the speaker think Boston should do?\n25. 讲话者认为波士顿应该做什么？', 164, 1557.69, 1307.63, 1472.04, 1477.94, 1495.71, 1317.81, 1320.90, 'cet6-2025-06-1-g07', 'LECTURE', '[21:47.63]Recording Three｜录音三\n[21:50.80]Do you think Bostonians put out the welcome mat?｜你认为波士顿人会欢迎你吗？\n[21:54.22]Do you find other cities to be more or less polite?｜你觉得其他城市更讲礼貌还是更没礼貌？\n[21:57.81]Different cities have different feels for visitors.｜不同城市会给游客留下截然不同的印象。\n[22:01.20]Some are friendly and open; some are formal and distant.｜有的友善开朗，有的刻板疏离。\n[22:06.43]Boston tends to seem hurried and sometimes a little rude.｜波士顿往往给人匆忙急躁，有时略显粗鲁的感觉。\n[22:10.58]That''s what some tourists have said.｜这是许多游客都提到过的。\n[22:13.25]One study found that people here are less willing to volunteer to help strangers than those residents of other US cities.｜一项研究发现，相比美国其他城市居民，波士顿人更不愿意主动帮助陌生人。\n[22:22.32]Take a close look next time you''re in Boston, and here are some of the things you''ll probably see:｜下次造访波士顿时仔细观察，这里是一些你可能会看到的场景：\n[22:28.16]people smoking in the subway stations despite the frequent reminders not to do so;｜地铁站里对频繁出现的禁烟标识视若无睹的吸烟者；\n[22:33.82]pedestrians walking when the crosswalk lights say not to do so, ignoring the cars;｜人行横道红灯时依然穿行、无视来往车辆的行人；\n[22:39.77]car drivers speeding, making a loud noise with the horn and occasionally cursing at each other;｜鸣笛震天、偶尔互相咒骂的超速司机；\n[22:46.77]drivers who can''t be bothered to find a place to park, but instead, simply turn on their blinker and stop in the middle of the street.｜懒得寻找车位、直接打双闪停在路中央的司机。\n[22:55.33]Sometimes Bostonians seem like they''re just too busy to be polite.｜有时波士顿人看起来像是忙得根本顾不上礼节。\n[22:59.76]Why is their city like that?｜为什么他们的城市是那样的？\n[23:02.12]Former US representative Chester Atkins says it''s because the Puritan founding fathers were themselves impatient and bad-tempered, and had little time for courtesy.｜前国会议员切斯特·阿特金斯认为，这是因为清教徒创立者们自身就没耐心且脾气不好，无暇讲究客套。\n[23:12.63]That culture has carried down to the present day, he believes.｜他认为这种文化延续至今。\n[23:16.56]Boston College professor Thomas O''Connor thinks a contributing factor is Boston''s history as a city where each new group of immigrants was treated with suspicion and exclusion.｜波士顿学院的托马斯·奥康纳教授认为，一个促成因素是波士顿作为一个城市的历史，历史上每批新移民在此都遭遇猜忌排斥。\n[23:29.17]That''s given Boston a sense of clannishness and reserve, he thinks.｜他认为这给了波士顿一种排外、保守的感觉。\n[23:34.07]“The idea of a stranger greeting another stranger is simply not part of the local culture,” he says.“People will begin a conversation with someone they know, but not with a stranger.”｜他解释道：“陌生人之间互相问候根本不属于本地文化，人们只愿意与认识的人而不愿意与陌生人交谈。”\n[23:45.50]Ralph Whitehead, a professor of journalism at the University of Massachusetts, at Amherst, thinks it''s because Boston''s upper class used manners to make class distinctions.｜马萨诸塞大学阿默斯特分校的拉尔夫·怀特海德教授认为，这是因为波士顿上流社会曾用繁文缛节标榜阶级差异。\n[23:56.97]“By being snobbish about manners, Proper Bostonians made other people scornful of courtesy,” he says.｜他说正统波士顿人对礼节过分挑剔的做法反而让其他人鄙视礼仪。\n[24:04.39]Some say that''s just the way Boston is, and that the city will never change.｜有人说这就是波士顿的底色，这个城市永远无法改变。\n[24:09.66]Still, other cities with reputations for worse rudeness are trying hard to change their images.｜但那些以粗鲁著称更甚的城市正在努力改头换面。\n[24:16.05]Paris is one example where people are trying to be friendly.｜巴黎就是一个开始努力变得友好的例子。\n[24:19.74]New York City is another—there the city is trying to persuade subway riders to be more considerate.｜纽约是另一个——该市正努力劝说地铁乘客更加体贴。\n[24:27.45]If those cities can make the effort, maybe Boston should as well.｜要是这些城市都能做出努力，波士顿或许也可以。\n[24:32.04]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题22到25。\n[24:37.94]22. What do visitors find about the cities they''ve been to?｜22. 关于自己去过的城市，游客有何发现？\n[24:56.01]23.What did one study find about Bostonians compared with residents of other US cities?｜23. 关于波士顿人，相比美国其他城市的居民，一项研究发现了什么？\n[25:16.66]24.What does Thomas O''Connor say about the idea of strangers greeting one another?｜24. 关于陌生人互相问候的想法，托马斯·奥康纳说了什么？\n[25:36.60]25.What does the speaker think Boston should do?｜25. 讲话者认为波士顿应该做什么？', NOW(), NOW()
