he idea of a stranger greeting another stranger is simply not part of the local culture,” he says.“People will begin a conversation with someone they know, but not with a stranger.”｜他解释道：“陌生人之间互相问候根本不属于本地文化，人们只愿意与认识的人而不愿意与陌生人交谈。”\n[23:45.50]Ralph Whitehead, a professor of journalism at the University of Massachusetts, at Amherst, thinks it''s because Boston''s upper class used manners to make class distinctions.｜马萨诸塞大学阿默斯特分校的拉尔夫·怀特海德教授认为，这是因为波士顿上流社会曾用繁文缛节标榜阶级差异。\n[23:56.97]“By being snobbish about manners, Proper Bostonians made other people scornful of courtesy,” he says.｜他说正统波士顿人对礼节过分挑剔的做法反而让其他人鄙视礼仪。\n[24:04.39]Some say that''s just the way Boston is, and that the city will never change.｜有人说这就是波士顿的底色，这个城市永远无法改变。\n[24:09.66]Still, other cities with reputations for worse rudeness are trying hard to change their images.｜但那些以粗鲁著称更甚的城市正在努力改头换面。\n[24:16.05]Paris is one example where people are trying to be friendly.｜巴黎就是一个开始努力变得友好的例子。\n[24:19.74]New York City is another—there the city is trying to persuade subway riders to be more considerate.｜纽约是另一个——该市正努力劝说地铁乘客更加体贴。\n[24:27.45]If those cities can make the effort, maybe Boston should as well.｜要是这些城市都能做出努力，波士顿或许也可以。\n[24:32.04]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题22到25。\n[24:37.94]22. What do visitors find about the cities they''ve been to?｜22. 关于自己去过的城市，游客有何发现？\n[24:56.01]23.What did one study find about Bostonians compared with residents of other US cities?｜23. 关于波士顿人，相比美国其他城市的居民，一项研究发现了什么？\n[25:16.66]24.What does Thomas O''Connor say about the idea of strangers greeting one another?｜24. 关于陌生人互相问候的想法，托马斯·奥康纳说了什么？\n[25:36.60]25.What does the speaker think Boston should do?｜25. 讲话者认为波士顿应该做什么？', NOW(), NOW()
