SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_06_2_16, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-06-2.mp3', 'Recording One\n录音一\n\nMy current research is mainly looking at factors like stress and how would it affect our immune system.\n我目前的研究主要关注压力等因素及其对我们免疫系统的影响。\n\nSo the current projects that I am quite excited about are looking at older people and factors that are important to them.\n所以现在我最感兴趣是几个针对老年群体及其关键健康因素的课题。\n\nIn one project, we are looking at hip fracture in older people, which is a common event, and we are trying to understand the impact of depression.\n在其中一个课题中，我们聚焦老年人髋部骨折这一常见问题，并且，我们重点探索抑郁症的影响。\n\nOften people in hospital with a hip fracture develop depression, and these are the people that then go on to become feeble a lot more quickly than others.\n髋部骨折住院患者往往会出现抑郁症状，这些患者的虚弱速度明显快于其他患者。\n\nThey get infections; often, these infections lead to death.\n他们发生感染，这些感染常常导致死亡。\n\nSo we''re trying to slow down that progression to feebleness.\n因此我们正努力延缓这种衰弱进程。\n\nSo if we can better understand how these factors interact, then we can actually start doing something about it.\n所以，如果能更好地理解这些因素的相互作用机制，我们就能开始着手解决这些问题。\n\nThe impact that my research will have in Birmingham, first of all, is obviously for older people, because these are the people that are having hip fractures and developing depression.\n我的研究对伯明翰地区的影响首先体现在老年人身上，因为他们患有髋部骨折和抑郁症。\n\nAnd we''re trying to improve their health and their quality of life.\n我们致力于改善他们的健康状况和生活品质。\n\nBut this isn''t just a problem in Birmingham or even in the UK.\n但这不仅是伯明翰的问题，也不仅是英国的问题。\n\nThis is a problem globally. People are aging.\n这是一个全球性的问题。人们在变老。\n\nThere''re a lot more people in the older generation who are living for longer, but not necessarily staying healthy for a large part of their lives.\n越来越多的老年人寿命延长，但他们生命的很大一部分时间里却往往不能保持健康状态。\n\nSo the last part of their life can often be full of ill health and we''re trying to understand factors that can contribute to the quality of life later on.\n因此，他们的晚年健康状况欠佳，而我们正致力于了解那些影响晚年生活质量的因素。\n\nI think the thing that excites me most about my research is that it feels real and relevant.\n我觉得最令我兴奋的是这项研究让人感觉真实且具有相关性。\n\nLots of academics are probably very excited about their research, but it might not be that relevant to those around them.\n虽然很多学者都对自己的研究兴奋不已，但他们的研究却可能与周围人的生活并不相关。\n\nWhereas I feel that the work I do is relevant to everyone, because everyone is aging.\n而我认为我所做的研究与每个人都息息相关，因为每个人都会老去。\n\nHealth is an issue for everyone, regardless of their age.\n健康是所有人都关心的问题，不论年龄几何。\n\nAnd what I''m trying to do is understand the factors that affect health and how we can improve the quality of life and people.\n我所做的就是试图了解影响健康的因素，以及我们该如何改善人们的生活质量。\n\nSo that''s one of the things that really excites me about it.\n这就是我对这项研究充满热情的原因之一。\n\nOne of the other things is rather than just taking one perspective—so rather than looking at aging and health just from the perspective of the psychologist—we are now bringing together a lot of different techniques and a lot of different viewpoints to actually look at the sort of different factors affecting health.\n另一件事在于，我们不再只是从单一的角度出发——不再只是从心理学家的角度来看待衰老和健康问题，而是整合了许多技术手段和不同观点来研究影响健康的各种因素。\n\nQuestions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n\n16. What does the speaker say is quite common among older people?\n16. 讲话者说什么在老年人群中很常见？\n\n17. What does the speaker think excites her most about her research?\n17. 讲话者认为研究的哪个方面让她最为兴奋？\n\n18. How does the speaker approach her research project?\n18. 讲话者是如何开展她的研究项目的？', 152, 1567.64, 871.73, 1023.78, 1029.28, 1047.14, 888.04, 897.02, 'cet6-2025-06-2-g05', 'LECTURE', '[14:31.73]Recording One｜录音一\n[14:34.05]My current research is mainly looking at factors like stress and how would it affect our immune system.｜我目前的研究主要关注压力等因素及其对我们免疫系统的影响。\n[14:40.78]So the current projects that I am quite excited about are looking at older people and factors that are important to them.｜所以现在我最感兴趣是几个针对老年群体及其关键健康因素的课题。\n[14:48.04]In one project, we are looking at hip fracture in older people, which is a common event, and we are trying to understand the impact of depression.｜在其中一个课题中，我们聚焦老年人髋部骨折这一常见问题，并且，我们重点探索抑郁症的影响。\n[14:57.32]Often people in hospital with a hip fracture develop depression, and these are the people that then go on to become feeble a lot more quickly than others.｜髋部骨折住院患者往往会出现抑郁症状，这些患者的虚弱速度明显快于其他患者。\n[15:07.56]They get infections; often, these infections lead to death.｜他们发生感染，这些感染常常导致死亡。\n[15:12.06]So we''re trying to slow down that progression to feebleness.｜因此我们正努力延缓这种衰弱进程。\n[15:16.14]So if we can better understand how these factors interact, then we can actually start doing something about it.｜所以，如果能更好地理解这些因素的相互作用机制，我们就能开始着手解决这些问题。\n[15:23.49]The impact that my research will have in Birmingham, first of all, is obviously for older people, because these are the people that are having hip fractures and developing depression.｜我的研究对伯明翰地区的影响首先体现在老年人身上，因为他们患有髋部骨折和抑郁症。\n[15:35.60]And we''re trying to improve their health and their quality of life.｜我们致力于改善他们的健康状况和生活品质。\n[15:39.71]But this isn''t just a problem in Birmingham or even in the UK.｜但这不仅是伯明翰的问题，也不仅是英国的问题。\n[15:44.63]This is a problem globally. People are aging.｜这是一个全球性的问题。人们在变老。\n[15:48.24]There''re a lot more people in the older generation who are living for longer, but not necessarily staying healthy for a large part of their lives.｜越来越多的老年人寿命延长，但他们生命的很大一部分时间里却往往不能保持健康状态。\n[15:57.50]So the last part of their life can often be full of ill health and we''re trying to understand factors that can contribute to the quality of life later on.｜因此，他们的晚年健康状况欠佳，而我们正致力于了解那些影响晚年生活质量的因素。\n[16:07.92]I think the thing that excites me most about my research is that it feels real and relevant.｜我觉得最令我兴奋的是这项研究让人感觉真实且具有相关性。\n[16:14.43]Lots of academics are probably very excited about their research, but it might not be that relevant to those around them.｜虽然很多学者都对自己的研究兴奋不已，但他们的研究却可能与周围人的生活并不相关。\n[16:22.08]Whereas I feel that the work I do is relevant to everyone, because everyone is aging.｜而我认为我所做的研究与每个人都息息相关，因为每个人都会老去。\n[16:27.96]Health is an issue for everyone, regardless of their age.｜健康是所有人都关心的问题，不论年龄几何。\n[16:32.00]And what I''m trying to do is understand the factors that affect health and how we can improve the quality of life and people.｜我所做的就是试图了解影响健康的因素，以及我们该如何改善人们的生活质量。\n[16:40.00]So that''s one of the things that really excites me about it.｜这就是我对这项研究充满热情的原因之一。\n[16:43.70]One of the other things is rather than just taking one perspective—so rather than looking at aging and health just from the perspective of the psychologist—we are now bringing together a lot of different techniques and a lot of different viewpoints to actually look at the sort of different factors affecting health.｜另一件事在于，我们不再只是从单一的角度出发——不再只是从心理学家的角度来看待衰老和健康问题，而是整合了许多技术手段和不同观点来研究影响健康的各种因素。\n[17:03.78]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n[17:09.28]16. What does the speaker say is quite common among older people?｜16. 讲话者说什么在老年人群中很常见？\n[17:27.44]17. What does the speaker think excites her most about her research?｜17. 讲话者认为研究的哪个方面让她最为兴奋？\n[17:46.41]18. How does the speaker approach her research project?｜18. 讲话者是如何开展她的研究项目的？', NOW(), NOW()
