SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_06_2_21, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-06-2.mp3', 'Recording Two\n录音二\n\nAmericans are obsessed with celebrity chefs.\n美国人痴迷于明星主厨。\n\nWe talk about them and try to eat like them.\n我们谈论他们，模仿他们的饮食方式。\n\nBut could we learn something more from them than recipes and techniques?\n但除了食谱和烹饪技巧，我们是否能从他们身上学到更多呢？\n\nAccording to Marketdata Enterprises, Americans spend nearly $10 billion a year on self-help and personal organization products.\n据Marketdata Enterprises统计，美国人每年在自助和个人组织产品上花费近100亿美元。\n\nThe market is huge, partly because most colleges and graduate schools don''t teach basic organization.\n这个市场如此庞大，部分原因在于大多数高校和研究生院都不教授基础的组织能力。\n\nBut cooking schools and professional kitchens do.\n而烹饪学校和专业厨房却会进行传授。\n\nPerhaps the principles of cooking organization can be extended to help even those of us who aren''t top chefs.\n或许，这种烹饪组织原则也能帮助我们这些不是顶级厨师的人提升效率。\n\nThe system that makes kitchens function is called mise-en-place, or, literally, “put in place”.\n这个让厨房运转的体系被称为“mise-en-place”，或者，字面意思“放置到位”。\n\nIt''s a French phrase that means to gather and arrange the ingredients and tools needed for cooking.\n这是个法语短语，指的是备齐并整理烹饪所需的食材和工具。\n\nBut for many professional cooks, the phrase means something deeper.\n但对许多专业厨师而言，这个短语有着更深层的含义。\n\nSome cooks call it their religion.\n有些厨师将其视为信仰。\n\nIt helps them coordinate vast amounts of labor and material, and transforms the lives of its practitioners through focus and self-discipline.\n这有助于他们协调大量人力和物料，通过专注和自律彻底改变从业者的生活。\n\nStudents at the Culinary Institute of America view it as a way of life.\n美国烹饪学院的学生将其视为一种生活方式。\n\nIt''s a way of concentrating their mind to only focus on the aspects that they need to be working on at the moment, to kind of rid themselves of distractions.\n这是一种让人全神贯注于当下需要处理的事务，摒除一切干扰的方法。\n\nAnd it''s a habit that some students carry with them even when they''re not in the kitchen.\n也是一种一些学生甚至在离开厨房后仍旧带着的习惯。\n\nThey mise-en-place their life.\n他们用mise-en-place的方式管理人生。\n\nThey set up their books for class, they set up their chef whites, they shine their shoes, they know everything that they need every step of the day.\n他们整理课本、准备厨师服、擦亮皮鞋，对每一天每个步骤所需的物品都了然于胸。\n\nThen how does one attain this level of organization?\n那么怎样才能达到这种组织水平呢？\n\nThe key to high level organization is the mindset.\n高水平组织的关键在于思维方式。\n\nCooks can easily do 6 hours of preparation for a 3-hour dinner shift.\n厨师可以轻松地为3小时的晚餐轮班做6小时的准备工作。\n\nMise-en-place forces cooks to account for every minute of their time and every movement.\nmise-en-place要求厨师们对每一分钟和每个动作都了然于胸。\n\n“Every component of one single dish is in one single corner so their hand literally moves inches,” explains Dwayne Lipuma, an instructor at the Culinary Institute of America.\n“将一道菜的所有配料都放在同一个角落，这样他们的手就真的只需要移动几英寸。”美国烹饪学院的导师德韦恩·利普玛解释道。\n\n“Once students set up their station, I should be able to cover up their eyes. And they should know that their knives are always here, their oil is always right here, their salt and pepper is always right here.”\n“学生布置好操作台后，我就算把他们的眼睛蒙上，他们也应该能确切地知道刀具、油、盐、胡椒等物品各自的老地方。”\n\nBut practiced at its highest level, mise-en-place says that time is precious. Resources are precious.Space is precious. Your self-respect and the respect of others are precious. Use them wisely.\n但当mise-en-place达到最高境界时，它告诉我们：时间宝贵、资源宝贵、空间宝贵、自尊与尊重他人宝贵。要善用这一切。\n\nIsn''t that a philosophy for our time?\n这不正是我们这个时代需要的哲学吗？\n\nQuestions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n\n19. What does the speaker say about most colleges and graduate schools in America?\n19. 关于美国大多数高校和研究生院，讲话者说了什么？\n\n20. How do students at the Culinary Institute of America view mise-en-place or “put in place”?\n20. 美国烹饪学院的学生如何看待mise-on-place或“放置到位”？\n\n21. What does the speaker advise us to do at the end of the talk?\n21. 讲话者在讲座结束时建议我们做什么？', 167, 1567.64, 1084.43, 1251.27, 1298.42, 1316.69, 1232.81, 1247.96, 'cet6-2025-06-2-g06', 'LECTURE', '[18:04.43]Recording Two｜录音二\n[18:06.24]Americans are obsessed with celebrity chefs.｜美国人痴迷于明星主厨。\n[18:10.32]We ta