  (`question_id`, `audio_url`, `transcript`, `duration_seconds`, `audio_total_seconds`, `audio_start_seconds`, `audio_end_seconds`, `question_start_seconds`, `question_end_seconds`, `answer_start_seconds`, `answer_end_seconds`, `group_key`, `listening_type`, `lrc_content`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_06_2_24, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-06-2.mp3', 'Recording Three\n录音三\n\nWhy do we stop making goals for ourselves and stop progressing in life?\n为什么我们不再为自己设定目标，生活中也不想进步？\n\nOne reason is the belief that we should accept who we are and not constantly strive to improve ourselves.\n原因之一在于人们认为应当安于现状，而非持续追求自我提升。\n\nThe mistake we make with this is that whilst self-acceptance is imperative, it should actually be used as the basis for growth.\n这种认知的错误在于：自我接纳固然重要，但它实际上应该被用作成长的基础。\n\nWhen we are not growing, learning and seeking improvements in our lives, we tend to stagnate, accepting what it is and stop believing we can be better.\n当我们的人生中不再有成长、学习和追求进步时，我们就会陷入停滞状态，安于现状并不再相信自己能变得更好。\n\nA key tool for creating goals and progressing is using our imagination.\n想象力是设定目标和取得进步的关键工具。\n\nYet as is so often the case, by the time we''ve reached midlife, we''ve stopped dreaming about what we want to achieve and feel quite negatively about goal-setting.\n然而，最常见的情况是，人到中年后，我们不再梦想取得何种成就，对目标设定也持消极态度。\n\nInstead of feeling confidence, happiness and satisfaction from achieving our goals, we feel we are always falling short.\n我们总是感到失败，无法从达成目标中获得自信、快乐和满足。\n\nThat leads to disappointment and we stop dreaming.\n这会导致我们感觉失望，继而停止梦想。\n\nWe stop imagining the life we''d like to have or the person we''d like to be, quickly finding ourselves shattered by the pain of our reality.\n我们不再憧憬理想的生活或自己想成为的样子，很快就发现自己会被现实的痛苦击垮。\n\nUp until now, unless we work in the creative industry, we''ve found the idea of using “play” a bit childish.\n现在，除非从事创意行业，我们都觉得使用“玩”这个词有些幼稚。\n\nIn the West, we are mostly operating from our heads, the rational mind, but it''s beginning to be better understood, because using our imagination is actually one of the most powerful things we could ever do.\n西方社会大多依赖大脑、理性思维，但现在它开始得到更好的理解，因为运用想象力恰恰是我们所能做的最有力的事情之一。\n\nThe problem with finding ourselves irritated for not achieving the goal is because the goal always changes and we''re always adapting, whether we realize it or not.\n未能达成目标之所以令人恼火，是因为无论我们是否有所察觉，目标本身总在变化，我们也总是在不断地适应。\n\nWhat happens, therefore, is that we end up feeling like we are never going to reach our goals.\n因此，我们总会觉得自己永远无法实现目标。\n\nAnd herein lies one of the biggest misconceptions and reasons for failure, dissatisfaction, and unhappiness.\n这正是造就失败、不满和不幸的最大误区和原因之一。\n\nWe measure against the goal rather than where we''ve come from.\n我们总是把目标当作参照物，而不是以起点来进行对比。\n\nDan Sullivan calls this “the gap and the gain”.\n丹·沙利文称之为“差距与进步”。\n\nWe measure always against the goal, which we never feel we''re reaching, rather than the gain.\n我们总是拿尚未达成的目标来衡量，而不是聚焦收益。\n\nThat is where we''ve come from.\n但那恰恰是我们的起点。\n\nIn measuring where we''ve come from, rather than where we''re trying to get to, we see results.\n衡量始自哪里，而不是去往何处，我们才能看到结果。\n\nWe see things we''ve taken for granted or not noticed.\n我们才能看到被认为理所应当或是被忽视的东西。\n\nIt''s a simple, yet profound change in perception, and what I love about it is the power it has to instantly shift us up a gear.\n这种认知转变简单却深刻，我最钟爱的一点就是它能立即提升我们的思维状态。\n\nIt''s so, so simple, yet has an immediate, positive impact on our thinking.\n它如此，如此简单，却可以对我们的思维产生立竿见影的积极影响。\n\nQuestions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题22到25。\n\n22. What should self-acceptance be used as according to the speaker?\n22. 根据讲话者的说法，自我接纳应当被用作什么？\n\n23. What do people tend to do when they have reached midlife?\n23. 人到中年后往往会出现什么倾向？\n\n24. Why do we find ourselves irritated for not achieving the goal?\n24. 为什么我们会发现自己因未能达成目标而恼火？\n\n25. What does the speaker say has an immediate, positive impact on our thinking?\n25. 讲话者说什么能对我们的思维产生立竿见影的积极影响？', 165, 1567.64, 1316.99, 1482.00, 1523.98, 1542.37, 1400.63, 1424.72, 'cet6-2025-06-2-g07', 'LECTURE', '[21:56.99]Recording Three｜录音三\n[21:59.20]Why do we stop making goals for ourselves and stop progressing in life?｜为什么我们不再为自己设定目标，生活中也不想进步？\n[22:04.76]One reason is the belief that we should accept who we are and not constantly strive to improve ourselves.｜原因之一在于人们认为应当安于现状，而非持续追求自我提升。\n[22:11.65]The mistake we make with this is that whilst self-acceptance is imperative, it should actually be used as the basis for growth.｜这种认知的错误在于：自我接纳固然重要，但它实际上应该被用作成长的基础。\n[22:20.56]When we are not growing, learning and seeking improvements in our lives, we tend to stagnate, accepting what it is and stop believing we can be better.｜当我们的人生中不再有成长、学习和追求进步时，我们就会陷入停滞状态，安于现状并不再相信自己能变得更好。\n[22:30.93]A key tool for creating goals and progressing is using our imagination.｜想象力是设定目标和取得进步的关键工具。\n[22:37.08]Yet as is so often the case, by the time we''ve reached midlife, we''ve stopped dreaming about what we want to achieve and feel quite negatively about goal-setting.｜然而，最常见的情况是，人到中年后，我们不再梦想取得何种成就，对目标设定也持消极态度。\n[22:49.09]Instead of feeling confidence, happiness and satisfaction from achieving our goals, we feel we are always falling short.｜我们总是感到失败，无法从达成目标中获得自信、快乐和满足。\n[22:58.10]That leads to disappointment and we stop dreaming.｜这会导致我们感觉失望，继而停止梦想。\n[23:02.10]We stop imagining the life we''d like to have or the person we''d like to be, quickly finding ourselves shattered by the pain of our reality.｜我们不再憧憬理想的生活或自己想成为的样子，很快就发现自己会被现实的痛苦击垮。\n[23:12.06]Up until now, unless we work in the creative industry, we''ve found the idea of using “play” a bit childish.｜现在，除非从事创意行业，我们都觉得使用“玩”这个词有些幼稚。\n[23:20.63]In the West, we are mostly operating from our heads, the rational mind, but it''s beginning to be better understood, because using our imagination is actually one of the most powerful things we could ever do.｜西方社会大多依赖大脑、理性思维，但现在它开始得到更好的理解，因为运用想象力恰恰是我们所能做的最有力的事情之一。\n[23:34.87]The problem with finding ourselves irritated for not achieving the goal is because the goal always changes and we''re always adapting, whether we realize it or not.｜未能达成目标之所以令人恼火，是因为无论我们是否有所察觉，目标本身总在变化，我们也总是在不断地适应。\n[23:45.02]What happens, therefore, is that we end up feeling like we are never going to reach our goals.｜因此，我们总会觉得自己永远无法实现目标。\n[23:50.57]And herein lies one of the biggest misconceptions and reasons for failure, dissatisfaction, and unhappiness.｜这正是造就失败、不满和不幸的最大误区和原因之一。\n[23:58.43]We measure against the goal rather than where we''ve come from.｜我们总是把目标当作参照物，而不是以起点来进行对比。\n[24:02.94]Dan Sullivan calls this “the gap and the gain”.｜丹·沙利文称之为“差距与进步”。\n[24:06.53]We measure always against the goal, which we never feel we''re reaching, rather than the gain.｜我们总是拿尚未达成的目标来衡量，而不是聚焦收益。\n[24:13.40]That is where we''ve come from.｜但那恰恰是我们的起点。\n[24:16.00]In measuring where we''ve come from, rather than where we''re trying to get to, we see results.｜衡量始自哪里，而不是去往何处，我们才能看到结果。\n[24:22.06]We see things we''ve taken for granted or not noticed.｜我们才能看到被认为理所应当或是被忽视的东西。\n[24:25.84]It''s a simple, yet profound change in perception, and what I love about it is the power it has to instantly shift us up a gear.｜这种认知转变简单却深刻，我最钟爱的一点就是它能立即提升我们的思维状态。\n[24:35.20]It''s so, so simple, yet has an immediate, positive impact on our thinking.｜它如此，如此简单，却可以对我们的思维产生立竿见影的积极影响。\n[24:42.00]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题22到25。\n[24:47.84]22. What should self-acceptance be used as according to the speaker?｜22. 根据讲话者的说法，自我接纳应当被用作什么？\n[25:06.58]23. What do people tend to do when they have reached midlife?｜23. 人到中年后往往会出现什么倾向？\n[25:23.98]24. Why do we find ourselves irritated for not achieving the goal?｜24. 为什么我们会发现自己因未能达成目标而恼火？\n[25:42.67]25. What does the speaker say has an immediate, positive impact on our thinking?｜25. 讲话者说什么能对我们的思维产生立竿见影的积极影响？', NOW(), NOW()
