  @a_cet6_2025_12_1_09, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-12-1.mp3', 'Passage One\n短文一\n\nOne thing I''ve learned in a quarter-century of management is that there are ample gray areas in human behavior, and that there may not always be clear right-or-wrong solutions to employee disputes.\n在二十五年的管理生涯中，我学到一点：人类行为中存在大量模糊地带，对待员工纠纷，往往没有绝对清晰的对或错的解决方案。\n\nWhen two people have different versions of the same event, it can be hard to know whom to believe.\n当两个人对同一件事的描述不同时，很难知道该相信谁。\n\nOne such circumstance always comes back to me.\n我总是会想起这样一次经历。\n\nIt happened years ago, and it''s a measure of the forgettable nature of the conflict that I no longer even recall what the dispute was about, but I definitely recall the resolution.\n事情发生在多年前，那场冲突本身没什么让人难忘的地方，我甚至都不记得那次争执是关于什么的了，但我清楚记得解决方法。\n\nTwo talented young executives, one of whom I was managing, had vastly different accounts of the same event, and it seemed likely we''d never get to the truth.\n两位才华出众的年轻主管（其中一位归我管理），对同一件事的说法大相径庭，我们似乎永远也无法弄清真相。\n\nWe reached the deadlock, and the manager of the other individual said,\n我们陷入了僵局，另一位主管的直属经理说：\n\n"Here''s something I learned from an excellent manager I once worked for.\n"以下是我从我跟过的一位优秀经理那里学到的东西。\n\nHe used to say, ''When in doubt, assume positive intent.''"\n他总是说：''有疑虑的时候，先假定对方是出于好意。''"\n\nAnd just like that, we had a path forward.\n就这样，我们找到了向前推进的办法。\n\nMy counterpart and I agreed we might never ascertain the actual facts of the matter; quite likely there were elements of truth in both employees'' stories.\n我和那位经理一致认为，我们或许永远无法查明事情的全部真相；很有可能两位员工的说法都有真实成分。\n\nWe agreed to monitor their actions and relationship going forward, but to intervene no further.\n我们决定观察他们的工作表现和相处情况，但不再干预。\n\nWe assumed the best about both of them and moved on.\n我们假定二人都是出于好意，便将此事翻篇儿了。\n\nThe result was that we were able to put the conflict behind us.\n结果是，我们成功地将这场矛盾抛在脑后。\n\nIn fact, their problems didn''t recur.\n事实上，他们之后再也没有出现过那类问题。\n\nOver the years, this general approach has proved useful.\n这些年来，这种处理方式被证明非常有用。\n\nWhenever the truth is obscure, assuming positive intent and agreeing to move on can be a constructive decision.\n每当真相难以厘清时，假定对方是出于好意，并选择翻篇儿，总会是一个富有建设性的决定。\n\nQuestions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题9到11。\n\n9. When can it be hard to know whom to believe?\n9.什么时候会很难知道该相信谁？\n\n10. What does the speaker definitely recall about the dispute mentioned as an example?\n10.对于作为例子提及的那次争执，讲话者清楚地记得什么？\n\n11. What did the speaker and his counterpart do when the two employees gave different accounts of the same event?\n11.当两位员工对同一件事的说辞不同时，讲话者和对方经理做了什么？', 99, 1543.76, 450.36, 549.68, 553.96, 571.10, 464.64, 470.54, 'cet6-2025-12-1-g03', 'PASSAGE', '[07:30.36]Passage One｜短文一\n[07:31.96]One thing I''ve learned in a quarter-century of management is that there are ample gray areas in human behavior, and that there may not always be clear right-or-wrong solutions to employee disputes.｜在二十五年的管理生涯中，我学到一点：人类行为中存在大量模糊地带，对待员工纠纷，往往没有绝对清晰的对或错的解决方案。\n[07:44.64]When two people have different versions of the same event, it can be hard to know whom to believe.｜当两个人对同一件事的描述不同时，很难知道该相信谁。\n[07:50.84]One such circumstance always comes back to me.｜我总是会想起这样一次经历。\n[07:53.88]It happened years ago, and it''s a measure of the forgettable nature of the conflict that I no longer even recall what the dispute was about, but I definitely recall the resolution.｜事情发生在多年前，那场冲突本身没什么让人难忘的地方，我甚至都不记得那次争执是关于什么的了，但我清楚记得解决方法。\n[08:05.48]Two talented young executives, one of whom I was managing, had vastly different accounts of the same event, and it seemed likely we''d never get to the truth.｜两位才华出众的年轻主管（其中一位归我管理），对同一件事的说法大相径庭，我们似乎永远也无法弄清真相。\n[08:15.40]We reached the deadlock, and the manager of the other individual said,｜我们陷入了僵局，另一位主管的直属经理说：\n[08:19.64]"Here''s something I learned from an excellent manager I once worked for.｜"以下是我从我跟过的一位优秀经理那里学到的东西。\n[08:23.68]He used to say, ''When in doubt, assume positive intent.''"｜他总是说：''有疑虑的时候，先假定对方是出于好意。''"\n[08:28.36]And just like that, we had a path forward.｜就这样，我们找到了向前推进的办法。\n[08:31.68]My counterpart and I agreed we might never ascertain the actual facts of the matter; quite likely there were elements of truth in both employees'' stories.｜我和那位经理一致认为，我们或许永远无法查明事情的全部真相；很有可能两位员工的说法都有真实成分。\n[08:42.44]We agreed to monitor their actions and relationship going forward, but to intervene no further.｜我们决定观察他们的工作表现和相处情况，但不再干预。\n[08:48.12]We assumed the best about both of them and moved on.｜我们假定二人都是出于好意，便将此事翻篇儿了。\n[08:51.68]The result was that we were able to put the conflict behind us.｜结果是，我们成功地将这场矛盾抛在脑后。\n[08:55.20]In fact, their problems didn''t recur.｜事实上，他们之后再也没有出现过那类问题。\n[08:58.28]Over the years, this general approach has proved useful.｜这些年来，这种处理方式被证明非常有用。\n[09:01.64]Whenever the truth is obscure, assuming positive intent and agreeing to move on can be a constructive decision.｜每当真相难以厘清时，假定对方是出于好意，并选择翻篇儿，总会是一个富有建设性的决定。\n[09:09.68]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题9到11。\n[09:13.96]9. When can it be hard to know whom to believe?｜9.什么时候会很难知道该相信谁？\n[09:31.40]10. What does the speaker definitely recall about the dispute mentioned as an example?｜10.对于作为例子提及的那次争执，讲话者清楚地记得什么？\n[09:50.96]11. What did the speaker and his counterpart do when the two employees gave different accounts of the same event?｜11.当两位员工对同一件事的说辞不同时，讲话者和对方经理做了什么？', NOW(), NOW()
