SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_12_1_15, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-12-1.mp3', 'Passage Two\n短文二\n\nFor some Americans, going out to dinner is a treat, planned and budgeted for.\n对一些美国人来说，外出就餐是一次特别款待，是需要规划并纳入预算。\n\nFor others, it''s just a daily occurrence.\n对另一些人而言，这不过是日常实践。\n\nAnd that second group of people is becoming the majority as the number of Americans who enjoy cooking is declining.\n而且，随着喜欢做饭的美国人越来越少，第二组人群正在成为大多数。\n\nIn the early 2000s, researcher Eddie Yoon conducted a survey to ascertain Americans'' attitudes to cooking.\n21世纪初，研究人员埃迪·尹开展了一项调查，旨在了解美国人对烹饪的态度。\n\nThe survey found that 15% of Americans loved to cook, 50% said they hated to cook and 35% reported mixed feelings.\n调查发现，15%的美国人热爱做饭，50%表示讨厌做饭，另有35%好恶不一。\n\nWhen Yoon conducted the same survey 15 years later, only 10% of respondents reported a love of cooking, 45% said they hated it and 45% had mixed feelings.\n15年后，埃迪·尹再次进行了相同的调查，此时仅有10%的受访者表示喜欢做饭，45%讨厌做饭，45%好恶不一。\n\nYoon asserts cooking is akin to sewing.\n尹认为，烹饪和缝纫很相似。\n\nAs recently as the early 20th century, many Americans sewed their own clothing.\n就在20世纪初，许多美国人还会自己缝制衣服；\n\nToday, the vast majority of Americans buy clothing; only a tiny minority still sew, and they do it mainly as a hobby.\n如今，绝大多数美国人都会直接买成衣，只有极少数人仍会自己缝纫，而这也仅仅是他们的爱好。\n\nYoon believes the number of home cooks will diminish in a similar fashion.\n尹认为，在家做饭的人的数量也会以类似的趋势减少。\n\nCurrently, the average American household spends over $3,000 per year on dining out.\n目前，美国家庭年均外出就餐支出超过3000美元。\n\nHowever, what people save in convenience, they lose in cash.\n然而，赢得了便利，损失了金钱。\n\nTake buying lunch on workdays for example.\n以工作日购买午餐为例。\n\nPeople spend, on average, $10 per meal when they buy lunch.\n人们平均每餐要花掉10美元来购买午餐。\n\nMaking your own lunch, meanwhile, will cost less.\n与此同时，自己做午餐，成本要低得多。\n\nOne survey found people only spend on average $6.30 when they bring lunch prepared at home.\n有调查发现，人们在家备好午餐带去（单位），平均每餐仅花费6.3美元。\n\nAmericans'' growing aversion to spending time in the kitchen signifies a host of challenges for the grocery industry.\n美国民众越来越不喜欢把时间花在厨房里，这意味着食品杂货业将面临一系列挑战。\n\nIt''s likely to detrimentally impact family finances as well.\n这也可能对家庭财务造成不利影响。\n\nQuestions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题12到15。\n\n12. What do an increasing number of Americans do according to the passage?\n12.根据短文内容，越来越多的美国人在做什么？\n\n13. What does Eddie Yoon think cooking is akin to?\n13.埃迪·尹认为烹饪与什么相似？\n\n14. What will Americans be able to do by making their own lunch?\n14.美国人自己准备午餐能实现什么？\n\n15. What does the passage say signifies a host of challenges for America''s grocery industry？\n15.短文指出，什么意味着美国食品杂货业将面临一些列挑战？\n\nSection C\nC节', 120, 1543.76, 611.72, 731.52, 789.76, 809.76, NULL, NULL, 'cet6-2025-12-1-g04', 'PASSAGE', '[10:11.72]Passage Two｜短文二\n[10:13.80]For some Americans, going out to dinner is a treat, planned and budgeted for.｜对一些美国人来说，外出就餐是一次特别款待，是需要规划并纳入预算。\n[10:19.08]For others, it''s just a daily occurrence.｜对另一些人而言，这不过是日常实践。\n[10:22.24]And that second group of people is becoming the majority as the number of Americans who enjoy cooking is declining.｜而且，随着喜欢做饭的美国人越来越少，第二组人群正在成为大多数。\n[10:29.92]In the early 2000s, researcher Eddie Yoon conducted a survey to ascertain Americans'' attitudes to cooking.｜21世纪初，研究人员埃迪·尹开展了一项调查，旨在了解美国人对烹饪的态度。\n[10:38.20]The survey found that 15% of Americans loved to cook, 50% said they hated to cook and 35% reported mixed feelings.｜调查发现，15%的美国人热爱做饭，50%表示讨厌做饭，另有35%好恶不一。\n[10:49.72]When Yoon conducted the same survey 15 years later, only 10% of respondents reported a love of cooking, 45% said they hated it and 45% had mixed feelings.｜15年后，埃迪·尹再次进行了相同的调查，此时仅有10%的受访者表示喜欢做饭，45%讨厌做饭，45%好恶不一。\n[11:04.28]Yoon asserts cooking is akin to sewing.｜尹认为，烹饪和缝纫很相似。\n[11:07.56]As recently as the early 20th century, many Americans sewed their own clothing.｜就在20世纪初，许多美国人还会自己缝制衣服；\n[11:13.64]Today, the vast majority of Americans buy clothing; only a tiny minority still sew, and they do it mainly as a hobby.｜如今，绝大多数美国人都会直接买成衣，只有极少数人仍会自己缝纫，而这也仅仅是他们的爱好。\n[11:22.68]Yoon believes the number of home cooks will diminish in a similar fashion.｜尹认为，在家做饭的人的数量也会以类似的趋势减少。\n[11:28.46]Currently, the average American household spends over $3,000 per year on dining out.｜目前，美国家庭年均外出就餐支出超过3000美元。\n[11:35.22]However, what people save in convenience, they lose in cash.｜然而，赢得了便利，损失了金钱。\n[11:39.67]Take buying lunch on workdays for example.｜以工作日购买午餐为例。\n[11:43.26]People spend, on average, $10 per meal when they buy lunch.｜人们平均每餐要花掉10美元来购买午餐。\n[11:47.78]Making your own lunch, meanwhile, will cost less.｜与此同时，自己做午餐，成本要低得多。\n[11:51.10]One survey found people only spend on average $6.30 when they bring lunch prepared at home.｜有调查发现，人们在家备好午餐带去（单位），平均每餐仅花费6.3美元。\n[11:58.86]Americans'' growing aversion to spending time in the kitchen signifies a host of challenges for the grocery industry.｜美国民众越来越不喜欢把时间花在厨房里，这意味着食品杂货业将面临一系列挑战。\n[12:06.24]It''s likely to detrimentally impact family finances as well.｜这也可能对家庭财务造成不利影响。\n[12:11.52]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的短文回答问题12到15。\n[12:15.84]12. What do an increasing number of Americans do according to the passage?｜12.根据短文内容，越来越多的美国人在做什么？\n[12:34.36]13. What does Eddie Yoon think cooking is akin to?｜13.埃迪·尹认为烹饪与什么相似？\n[12:51.94]14. What will Americans be able to do by making their own lunch?｜14.美国人自己准备午餐能实现什么？\n[13:09.76]15. What does the passage say signifies a host of challenges for America''s grocery industry？｜15.短文指出，什么意味着美国食品杂货业将面临一些列挑战？\n[13:29.70]Section C｜C节', NOW(), NOW()
