SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_12_1_20, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-12-1.mp3', 'Recording Two\n讲座二\n\nYou''ve probably been with someone who ignored you while interacting with his or her smartphone.\n你可能遇到过这样的人，他或她只顾着玩智能手机而忽略了你。\n\nAnd you''ve probably engaged in this behavior yourself.\n你自己或许也有过这种行为。\n\nIn a recent survey, nine out of ten respondents said they''d used their smartphone during their most recent social activity.\n在最近的一项调查中，十分之九的受访者都表示，他们在最近一次社交活动中使用过智能手机。\n\nThere''s also evidence that this behavior is socially harmful.\n也有证据表明这种行为对社交有害。\n\nIt can leave people less satisfied with their face-to-face interactions.\n它会降低人们对面对面交流的满意度。\n\nAnd it can generate feelings of resentment and jealousy.\n而且它还能引发不满和嫉妒情绪。\n\nNow, the Journal of Applied Social Psychology has published a new study exploring the reasons for these effects.\n如今，《应用社会心理学杂志》发表了一项新研究，旨在探究这些影响背后的原因。\n\nThe study included 128 mostly female student participants.\n该研究涉及了128名参与者，其中大部分是女生。\n\nThe participants watched a three-minute animation in which two cartoon figures interacted and engaged in a conversation.\n参与者观看了一段3分钟的动画，动画中有两个卡通人物进行互动和对话。\n\nThe conversation couldn''t be heard but could be detected through mouth movements.\n对话内容听不到，但能通过嘴部动作看出来。\n\nParticipants had to imagine themselves as the closest figure in the video and then make judgments about the conversations and how they felt about their conversation partners.\n参与者想象自己是视频中离自己最近的那个卡通人物，对对话内容以及自己对对话伙伴的感受做出判断。\n\nIn one version of the experiment, the phone of the participants'' conversation partner remained unused on the table throughout the conversation.\n在一个实验版本中，参与者的对话伙伴的手机全程放在桌上，从未使用；\n\nIn another version, their partner''s attention was quickly drawn to something on their phone and they spent the rest of the time interacting with their phone.\n在另一个实验版本中，对话伙伴的注意力很快被手机上的内容吸引，之后的时间都在玩手机；\n\nA third version involved the participants'' partner alternating between periods of interaction with their phone and periods where they ignored their phone.\n第三个版本中，对话伙伴在看手机和不看手机两种状态之间切换。\n\nIf you''ve experienced a similar situation, you probably won''t be surprised by the results of the experiments.\n如果你有过类似经历，或许不会对实验结果感到惊讶。\n\nThe participants had a negative view of phone use during social interactions.\n参与者对在社交互动中使用手机的行为持有负面看法。\n\nThe more it was present, the worse they considered both the quality of the interaction and the quality of the relationship.\n手机出现的频率越高，他们对互动质量和关系质量的评价就越低。\n\nThe researchers believed that focusing on one''s phone instead of the person one is with has this effect because it''s seen as a form of social exclusion.\n研究人员认为，专注于手机而非身边的人会产生这种影响是因为这种行为被视为一种社交排斥。\n\nThey assert that the behavior is analogous to when people have no choice but to eat alone in the school cafeteria.\n他们表示，这种行为类似于人们别无选择，只能独自在学校食堂吃饭的场景。\n\nThe twist is that this exclusion occurs while sharing a physical encounter and having a conversation.\n不同之处在于，这种排斥发生在双方共处一室、进行对话的过程中。\n\nResearchers found that the behavior had adverse effects on participants'' sense of belonging, need for meaning and self-esteem.\n研究人员发现，这种行为对参与者的归属感、意义需求感和自尊心都产生了不利影响。\n\nThese negative effects are similar to those caused by traditional forms of social exclusion.\n这些负面影响与传统形式的社交排斥所造成的影响有相似之处。\n\nThe new findings are important because they elaborate our understanding of a subtle but common aspect of our daily experience.\n这项新发现意义重大，因为它让我们对日常生活中一个细微却常见的现象有了更深入的理解。\n\nThis common behavior can create vicious cycles of detachment even as we try to connect with others.\n即使我们试图与他人建立联系，这种常见的行为也可能引发疏离感的恶性循环。\n\nQuestions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n\n19. What does the speaker say about using one''s smartphone during a face-to-face conversation?\n19.关于在面对面交谈中使用智能手机的行为，讲话者提到了什么？\n\n20. How did the participants in the new study respond regarding phone use during social interactions?\n20.新研究的参与者对在社交互动中使用手机的行为有何反应？\n\n21. What does the speaker say can result from the common behavior of using phones during face-to-face conversations?\n21.对于面对面交谈时使用手机这种常见行为，讲话者说它可能造成什么后果？', 176, 1543.76, 1053.68, 1229.90, 1254.60, 1274.50, 1159.84, 1164.58, 'cet6-2025-12-1-g06', 'LECTURE', '[17:33.68]Recording Two｜讲座二\n[17:35.96]You''ve probably been with someone who ignored you while interacting with his or her smartphone.｜你可能遇到过这样的人，他或她只顾着玩智能手机而忽略了你。\n[17:41.60]And you''ve probably engaged in this behavior yourself.｜你自己或许也有过这种行为。\n[17:45.12]In a recent survey, nine out of ten respondents said they''d used their smartphone during their most recent social activity.｜在最近的一项调查中，十分之九的受访者都表示，他们在最近一次社交活动中使用过智能手机。\n[17:53.68]There''s also evidence that this behavior is socially harmful.｜也有证据表明这种行为对社交有害。\n[17:57.48]It can leave people less satisfied with their face-to-face interactions.｜它会降低人们对面对面交流的满意度。\n[18:02.00]And it can generate feelings of resentment and jealousy.｜而且它还能引发不满和嫉妒情绪。\n[18:06.48]Now, the Journal of Applied Social Psychology has published a new study exploring the reasons for these effects.｜如今，《应用社会心理学杂志》发表了一项新研究，旨在探究这些影响背后的原因。\n[18:14.82]The study included 128 mostly female student participants.｜该研究涉及了128名参与者，其中大部分是女生。\n[18:20.10]The participants watched a three-minute animation in which two cartoon figures interacted and engaged in a conversation.｜参与者观看了一段3分钟的动画，动画中有两个卡通人物进行互动和对话。\n[18:28.10]The conversation couldn''t be heard but could be detected through mouth movements.｜对话内容听不到，但能通过嘴部动作看出来。\n[18:33.82]Participants had to imagine themselves as the closest figure in the video and then make judgments about the conversations and how they felt about their conversation partners.｜参与者想象自己是视频中离自己最近的那个卡通人物，对对话内容以及自己对对话伙伴的感受做出判断。\n[18:45.12]In one version of the experiment, the phone of the participants'' conversation partner remained unused on the table throughout the conversation.｜在一个实验版本中，参与者的对话伙伴的手机全程放在桌上，从未使用；\n[18:53.72]In another version, their partner''s attention was quickly drawn to something on their phone and they spent the rest of the time interacting with their phone.｜在另一个实验版本中，对话伙伴的注意力很快被手机上的内容吸引，之后的时间都在玩手机；\n[19:02.72]A third version involved the participants'' partner alternating between periods of interaction with their phone and periods where they ignored their phone.｜第三个版本中，对话伙伴在看手机和不看手机两种状态之间切换。\n[19:12.60]If you''ve experienced a similar situation, you probably won''t be surprised by the results of the experiments.｜如果你有过类似经历，或许不会对实验结果感到惊讶。\n[19:19.84]The participants had a negative view of phone use during social interactions.｜参与者对在社交互动中使用手机的行为持有负面看法。\n[19:24.88]The more it was present, the worse they considered both the quality of the interaction and the quality of the relationship.｜手机出现的频率越高，他们对互动质量和关系质量的评价就越低。\n[19:33.22]The researchers believed that focusing on one''s phone instead of the person one is with has this effect because it''s seen as a form of social exclusion.｜研究人员认为，专注于手机而非身边的人会产生这种影响是因为这种行为被视为一种社交排斥。\n[19:43.82]They assert that the behavior is analogous to when people have no choice but to eat alone in the school cafeteria.｜他们表示，这种行为类似于人们别无选择，只能独自在学校食堂吃饭的场景。\n[19:51.42]The twist is that this exclusion occurs while sharing a physical encounter and having a conversation.｜不同之处在于，这种排斥发生在双方共处一室、进行对话的过程中。\n[19:59.02]Researchers found that the behavior had adverse effects on participants'' sense of belonging, need for meaning and self-esteem.｜研究人员发现，这种行为对参与者的归属感、意义需求感和自尊心都产生了不利影响。\n[20:07.88]These negative effects are similar to those caused by traditional forms of social exclusion.｜这些负面影响与传统形式的社交排斥所造成的影响有相似之处。\n[20:14.16]The new findings are important because they elaborate our understanding of a subtle but common aspect of our daily experience.｜这项新发现意义重大，因为它让我们对日常生活中一个细微却常见的现象有了更深入的理解。\n[20:22.20]This common behavior can create vicious cycles of detachment even as we try to connect with others.｜即使我们试图与他人建立联系，这种常见的行为也可能引发疏离感的恶性循环。\n[20:29.90]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n[20:34.94]19. What does the speaker say about using one''s smartphone during a face-to-face conversation?｜19.关于在面对面交谈中使用智能手机的行为，讲话者提到了什么？\n[20:54.60]20. How did the participants in the new study respond regarding phone use during social interactions?｜20.新研究的参与者对在社交互动中使用手机的行为有何反应？\n[21:14.80]21. What does the speaker say can result from the common behavior of using phones during face-to-face conversations?｜21.对于面对面交谈时使用手机这种常见行为，讲话者说它可能造成什么后果？', NOW(), NOW()
