SELECT
  @a_cet6_2025_12_2_19, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2025-12-2.mp3', 'Recording Two\n讲座二\n\nReading with a young child is important.\n与幼儿共读很重要。\n\nBut does it matter whether you read from an electronic book or traditional print?\n但阅读电子书还是传统纸质书是否有影响呢？\n\nAs any parent knows, small children are generally keen on screens.\n正如所有父母所知，幼儿通常更喜欢电子屏幕。\n\nSo the finding from a new study that very young children enjoy electronic books more than printed picture books may not come as a huge surprise—but the following additional findings might:\n因此，一项新研究发现幼儿更喜欢电子图画书而非纸质图画书，可能并不令人惊讶——但以下额外发现或许会：\n\nboth parents and children behaved differently when reading electronic versus printed picture books.\n父母和孩子在阅读电子图画书与纸质图画时的行为表现有所不同。\n\nAnd the children who read the electronic books learned more.\n而且阅读电子书的孩子们学到了更多东西。\n\nPrevious studies of young children have suggested that electronic books, which incorporate extra features such as dictionaries and images that depict story events, may have advantages over print.\n先前针对幼儿的研究表明，配有词典、故事情节图解等附加功能的电子书可能比纸质书更具优势。\n\nIn fact, multiple studies have found that electronic books support story comprehension and vocabulary gains beyond what is provided by printed books.\n实际上，多项研究发现电子书在促进故事理解和词汇增长方面优于纸质书。\n\nBut there''s also work suggesting that children sometimes invest less mental effort in learning from electronic books, and that parents talk less about content when sharing such books.\n但也有研究指出，孩子们有时在通过电子书学习时投入的精力较少，而家长在陪读电子书时对内容的讨论也较少。\n\nThe study we''re looking at today aims to give us a better understanding of how good electronic books are for young children.\n我们今天讨论的这项研究旨在让我们更好地了解电子书对幼儿的益处。\n\nFor this study, researchers videoed children aged 17 to 26 months reading with one of their parents.\n在这项研究中，研究人员录制了17至26个月大的幼儿与父亲或母亲共读的视频。\n\nEach parent-child pair was randomly assigned to read either two educational electronic books or two printed books with the same content.\n每对亲子随机分配阅读两本教育类电子书或两本内容相同的纸质书。\n\nWhile the parent read the words in the printed books aloud, the electronic books had audio of voice actors reading the text.\n父亲或母亲大声朗读纸质书，而电子书则由配音演员朗读文本。\n\nThe researchers found that parents who read the printed books pointed more frequently to pages than parents who read the electronic books.\n研究人员发现，阅读纸质书的父母比阅读电子书的父母更频繁地用手指向书页。\n\nBut the opposite was true for the children.\n但孩子们的情况恰恰相反。\n\nChildren who read the electronic books also commented more on the content and learned more new words than the children who read printed books.\n阅读电子书的孩子们比阅读纸质书的孩子们对内容的评论更多，学会的新词汇更多。\n\nThis greater learning is ascribed to greater engagement with the electronic books.\n这种更强的学习效果归因于孩子们对电子书更高的参与度。\n\nIt''s important to note that the electronic books in the study were simple.\n值得注意的是，研究中的电子书很简单。\n\nWhile they included background music, animation and sound effects, there were no other extra features.\n虽然包含背景音乐、动画和音效，但没有其他额外功能。\n\nIt''s also important to note that there''s research on older children, finding that electronic books don''t always bolster learning.\n同样重要的是，针对年龄较大儿童的研究发现，电子书并不总能促进学习。\n\nParents and educators need to beware.\n父母和教育者需要小心。\n\nExtra features in electronic books may increase children''s participation with them;\n电子书的附加功能可能会提高儿童的参与度；\n\nhowever, if these features don''t draw attention to the educational content, they may not serve as a supportive feature.\n然而，如果不能引导他们关注教育内容，这些功能则可能无法起到帮助性的作用。\n\nQuestions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n\n19. What finding from the new study might come as a huge surprise?\n19.这项新研究的什么发现可能会令人大为惊讶？\n\n20. What do we learn about previous studies of young children reading electronic books?\n20.关于先前对幼儿阅读电子书的研究，我们了解到什么？\n\n21. What extra features should be included in electronic books to bolster children''s learning?\n21.电子书应包含哪些附加功能来促进孩子们的学习？', 171, 1554.89, 1088.80, 1259.68, 1264.88, 1283.76, 1121.56, 1126.00, 'cet6-2025-12-2-g06', 'LECTURE', '[18:08.80]Recording Two｜讲座二\n[18:10.96]Reading with a young child is important.｜与幼儿共读很重要。\n[18:13.36]But does it matter whether you read from an electronic book or traditional print?｜但阅读电子书还是传统纸质书是否有影响呢？\n[18:18.12]As any parent knows, small children are generally keen on screens.｜正如所有父母所知，幼儿通常更喜欢电子屏幕。\n[18:22.76]So the finding from a new study that very young children enjoy electronic books more than printed picture books may not come as a huge surprise—but the following additional findings might:｜因此，一项新研究发现幼儿更喜欢电子图画书而非纸质图画书，可能并不令人惊讶——但以下额外发现或许会：\n[18:35.07]both parents and children behaved differently when reading electronic versus printed picture books.｜父母和孩子在阅读电子图画书与纸质图画时的行为表现有所不同。\n[18:41.56]And the children who read the electronic books learned more.｜而且阅读电子书的孩子们学到了更多东西。\n[18:46.30]Previous studies of young children have suggested that electronic books, which incorporate extra features such as dictionaries and images that depict story events, may have advantages over print.｜先前针对幼儿的研究表明，配有词典、故事情节图解等附加功能的电子书可能比纸质书更具优势。\n[18:58.98]In fact, multiple studies have found that electronic books support story comprehension and vocabulary gains beyond what is provided by printed books.｜实际上，多项研究发现电子书在促进故事理解和词汇增长方面优于纸质书。\n[19:09.28]But there''s also work suggesting that children sometimes invest less mental effort in learning from electronic books, and that parents talk less about content when sharing such books.｜但也有研究指出，孩子们有时在通过电子书学习时投入的精力较少，而家长在陪读电子书时对内容的讨论也较少。\n[19:21.48]The study we''re looking at today aims to give us a better understanding of how good electronic books are for young children.｜我们今天讨论的这项研究旨在让我们更好地了解电子书对幼儿的益处。\n[19:29.12]For this study, researchers videoed children aged 17 to 26 months reading with one of their parents.｜在这项研究中，研究人员录制了17至26个月大的幼儿与父亲或母亲共读的视频。\n[19:37.04]Each parent-child pair was randomly assigned to read either two educational electronic books or two printed books with the same content.｜每对亲子随机分配阅读两本教育类电子书或两本内容相同的纸质书。\n[19:47.12]While the parent read the words in the printed books aloud, the electronic books had audio of voice actors reading the text.｜父亲或母亲大声朗读纸质书，而电子书则由配音演员朗读文本。\n[19:55.36]The researchers found that parents who read the printed books pointed more frequently to pages than parents who read the electronic books.｜研究人员发现，阅读纸质书的父母比阅读电子书的父母更频繁地用手指向书页。\n[20:04.12]But the opposite was true for the children.｜但孩子们的情况恰恰相反。\n[20:07.04]Children who read the electronic books also commented more on the content and learned more new words than the children who read printed books.｜阅读电子书的孩子们比阅读纸质书的孩子们对内容的评论更多，学会的新词汇更多。\n[20:16.48]This greater learning is ascribed to greater engagement with the electronic books.｜这种更强的学习效果归因于孩子们对电子书更高的参与度。\n[20:22.64]It''s important to note that the electronic books in the study were simple.｜值得注意的是，研究中的电子书很简单。\n[20:26.88]While they included background music, animation and sound effects, there were no other extra features.｜虽然包含背景音乐、动画和音效，但没有其他额外功能。\n[20:33.88]It''s also important to note that there''s research on older children, finding that electronic books don''t always bolster learning.｜同样重要的是，针对年龄较大儿童的研究发现，电子书并不总能促进学习。\n[20:42.40]Parents and educators need to beware.｜父母和教育者需要小心。\n[20:45.24]Extra features in electronic books may increase children''s participation with them;｜电子书的附加功能可能会提高儿童的参与度；\n[20:50.56]however, if these features don''t draw attention to the educational content, they may not serve as a supportive feature.｜然而，如果不能引导他们关注教育内容，这些功能则可能无法起到帮助性的作用。\n[20:59.68]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n[21:04.88]19. What finding from the new study might come as a huge surprise?｜19.这项新研究的什么发现可能会令人大为惊讶？\n[21:24.06]20. What do we learn about previous studies of young children reading electronic books?｜20.关于先前对幼儿阅读电子书的研究，我们了解到什么？\n[21:43.92]21. What extra features should be included in electronic books to bolster children''s learning?｜21.电子书应包含哪些附加功能来促进孩子们的学习？', NOW(), NOW()
