SELECT
  @a_cet6_2026_06_1_24, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2026-06-1.mp3', 'Recording Three\n录音三\n\nAutonomous cars that can take over from drivers if they are tired or caught in the heat of an argument are being developed in Sweden.\n瑞典正在研发一种自动驾驶汽车：当驾驶员疲惫不堪、或是在争执中情绪激动时，车辆可以自动接管驾驶权。\n\nIf a driver is not in a fit state to be on the road, the car automatically takes control of the wheel to prevent accidents.\n一旦检测到驾驶员状态不适宜驾车上路，车辆会自动接管方向盘，避免交通事故发生。\n\nDr. Anna Anund from the Swedish National Road and Transport Research Institute, who is leading the project, said a driver''s state can be measured using a number of sensors, including heart-rate monitors and eye sensors which track where a driver''s gaze is.\n该项目负责人、瑞典国家道路与交通研究所的安娜·阿农德博士表示，可以借助心率监测器、追踪驾驶员视线的眼部传感器等多种传感器来判断驾驶员的状态。\n\nShe said the aim was “to really make sure that the transition from the driver to the vehicle is done in a safe way, so we can take into consideration the driver''s state, and so we do not give control to a driver who is under pressure of fatigue, stress, anxiety and so on.”\n她指出，项目目标是“确保驾驶员与车辆之间的安全交接，对驾驶员状态进行评估，从而让疲惫、紧张、焦虑的驾驶员不再掌控车辆”。\n\nAccording to data from the European Commission, Sweden has the safest roads in the EU, with the UK a close second.\n欧盟委员会的数据显示，瑞典是欧盟道路安全系数最高的国家，英国紧随其后。\n\nHowever, the number of road accidents in both has largely levelled out since 2010.\n不过，从2010年起，两国的交通事故数量基本趋于稳定。\n\nDriver fatigue is thought to be responsible for as many as a fifth of road crashes across the EU, although there are certain times of the day when more accidents happen.\n人们认为欧盟多达五分之一的道路交通事故是由疲劳驾驶引发的，尽管一天中某些时段事故发生率更高。\n\nAt 6 a.m., for example, drivers are said to be 20 times more likely to have an accident than at 10 a.m.\n例如清晨6点时，驾驶员发生事故的概率是上午10点的20倍。\n\nWhile most experts agree driverless technology should help bring down accident numbers in the long term, some have questioned whether it is safe.\n长远来看，自动驾驶技术能减少事故数量，多数专家对此表示认同，但也有人对这项技术的安全性提出质疑。\n\nThe researchers have said the technology they are developing is seeking to bridge the gap between Level 3 on the autonomy scale, in which drivers have to be ready to take control of the car at any point, and Level 4, in which driving is automated.\n研究团队表示，此次研发的技术旨在弥合自动驾驶L3级与L4级标准之间的差距：L3级自动驾驶要求驾驶员随时准备接管车辆；L4级则可实现完全自动驾驶。\n\nDaniel Ruiz, the chief executive of the UK''s connected and autonomous vehicle program Meridian, said: “There is a strong debate going on about how it is possible to make a Level 3 car completely safe.”\n英国互联与自动驾驶项目Meridian首席执行官丹尼尔·鲁伊斯表示：“关于如何使L3级自动驾驶汽车实现完全安全，目前存在激烈争议。”\n\nDespite advances in technology, we still have some way to go in shifting public sentiment.\n尽管技术在进步，公众认知的转变仍任重道远。\n\nWith an increase in attention-grabbing accidents recently, drivers now view self-driving cars as less safe than they used to.\n随着近期引发关注的事故增多，驾驶员认为自动驾驶汽车的安全性已不如从前。\n\nQuestions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的讲座回答问题22到25。\n\n22. What are researchers doing in Sweden?\n22. 瑞典的研究人员目前正在做什么？\n\n23. What did the researchers'' project aim to do according to Dr. Anna Anund?\n23. 根据安娜·阿农德博士的说法，研究人员的项目旨在做什么？\n\n24. What is thought to be responsible for a fifth of road accidents across the European Union?\n24. 人们认为是什么导致了欧盟五分之一的道路交通事故？\n\n25. Why do drivers now view self-driving cars as less safe than they used to?\n25. 为什么驾驶员现在觉得自动驾驶汽车不如以往安全？', 165, 1535.54, 1282.17, 1447.62, 1491.25, 1510.93, NULL, NULL, 'cet6-2026-06-1-g07', 'LECTURE', '[21:22.17]Recording Three｜录音三\n[21:23.88]Autonomous cars that can take over from drivers if they are tired or caught in the heat of an argument are being developed in Sweden.｜瑞典正在研发一种自动驾驶汽车：当驾驶员疲惫不堪、或是在争执中情绪激动时，车辆可以自动接管驾驶权。\n[21:46.63]If a driver is not in a fit state to be on the road, the car automatically takes control of the wheel to prevent accidents.｜一旦检测到驾驶员状态不适宜驾车上路，车辆会自动接管方向盘，避免交通事故发生。\n[21:55.21]Dr. Anna Anund from the Swedish National Road and Transport Research Institute, who is leading the project, said a driver''s state can be measured using a number of sensors, including heart-rate monitors and eye sensors which track where a driver''s gaze is.｜该项目负责人、瑞典国家道路与交通研究所的安娜·阿农德博士表示，可以借助心率监测器、追踪驾驶员视线的眼部传感器等多种传感器来判断驾驶员的状态。\n[22:13.67]She said the aim was “to really make sure that the transition from the driver to the vehicle is done in a safe way, so we can take into consideration the driver''s state, and so we do not give control to a driver who is under pressure of fatigue, stress, anxiety and so on.”｜她指出，项目目标是“确保驾驶员与车辆之间的安全交接，对驾驶员状态进行评估，从而让疲惫、紧张、焦虑的驾驶员不再掌控车辆”。\n[22:32.63]According to data from the European Commission, Sweden has the safest roads in the EU, with the UK a close second.｜欧盟委员会的数据显示，瑞典是欧盟道路安全系数最高的国家，英国紧随其后。\n[22:41.63]However, the number of road accidents in both has largely levelled out since 2010.｜不过，从2010年起，两国的交通事故数量基本趋于稳定。\n[22:48.27]Driver fatigue is thought to be responsible for as many as a fifth of road crashes across the EU, although there are certain times of the day when more accidents happen.｜人们认为欧盟多达五分之一的道路交通事故是由疲劳驾驶引发的，尽管一天中某些时段事故发生率更高。\n[23:00.03]At 6 a.m., for example, drivers are said to be 20 times more likely to have an accident than at 10 a.m.｜例如清晨6点时，驾驶员发生事故的概率是上午10点的20倍。\n[23:08.22]While most experts agree driverless technology should help bring down accident numbers in the long term, some have questioned whether it is safe.｜长远来看，自动驾驶技术能减少事故数量，多数专家对此表示认同，但也有人对这项技术的安全性提出质疑。\n[23:18.72]The researchers have said the technology they are developing is seeking to bridge the gap between Level 3 on the autonomy scale, in which drivers have to be ready to take control of the car at any point, and Level 4, in which driving is automated.｜研究团队表示，此次研发的技术旨在弥合自动驾驶L3级与L4级标准之间的差距：L3级自动驾驶要求驾驶员随时准备接管车辆；L4级则可实现完全自动驾驶。\n[23:36.37]Daniel Ruiz, the chief executive of the UK''s connected and autonomous vehicle program Meridian, said: “There is a strong debate going on about how it is possible to make a Level 3 car completely safe.”｜英国互联与自动驾驶项目Meridian首席执行官丹尼尔·鲁伊斯表示：“关于如何使L3级自动驾驶汽车实现完全安全，目前存在激烈争议。”\n[23:51.69]Despite advances in technology, we still have some way to go in shifting public sentiment.｜尽管技术在进步，公众认知的转变仍任重道远。\n[23:58.61]With an increase in attention-grabbing accidents recently, drivers now view self-driving cars as less safe than they used to.｜随着近期引发关注的事故增多，驾驶员认为自动驾驶汽车的安全性已不如从前。\n[24:07.62]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的讲座回答问题22到25。\n[24:14.21]22. What are researchers doing in Sweden?｜22. 瑞典的研究人员目前正在做什么？\n[24:30.56]23. What did the researchers'' project aim to do according to Dr. Anna Anund?｜23. 根据安娜·阿农德博士的说法，研究人员的项目旨在做什么？\n[24:51.25]24. What is thought to be responsible for a fifth of road accidents across the European Union?｜24. 人们认为是什么导致了欧盟五分之一的道路交通事故？\n[25:11.23]25. Why do drivers now view self-driving cars as less safe than they used to?｜25. 为什么驾驶员现在觉得自动驾驶汽车不如以往安全？', NOW(), NOW()
