  @a_cet6_2026_06_2_05, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2026-06-2.mp3', 'Conversation Two\n对话二\n\nW: Hey there, Paul, how''s it going?\n女：嘿，保罗，最近怎么样？\n\nM: Hi, Suzanne.\n男：嗨，苏珊娜。\n\nJust keeping busy with the usual stuff—filling prescriptions, talking to patients about their medications, that kind of thing.\n就是忙着平常那些事——按处方配药、跟病人聊他们的药物之类的。\n\nHow about you?\n你呢？\n\nW: Same here, although I did have an interesting case come in this morning.\n女：我也是，不过今天早上我确实接了一个有趣的病例。\n\nA patient came in inquiring about a medication that had a potential interaction with another medication she was already taking.\n一位病人来咨询一种药物，这种药与她已经在服用的另一种药可能存在相互作用。\n\nM: Oh, that''s always tricky.\n男：哦，那总是很棘手。\n\nWhat did you do?\n你怎么办的？\n\nW: I explained the issue to her as best I could, and recommended she double-check with her doctor.\n女：我尽可能地把问题解释给她听，并建议她再跟她的医生确认一下。\n\nAnd you know what?\n你知道吗？\n\nShe actually followed my advice and the doctor ended up switching the patient''s medication.\n她真的听了我的建议，医生最终给这位病人换了药。\n\nM: Ah, that''s great!\n男：啊，那太好了！\n\nYou must feel quite accomplished!\n你一定觉得很有成就感吧！\n\nW: Totally! These moments are the highlights of my routine work, employing my pharmaceutical expertise to the benefit of people''s health and well-being.\n女：太对了！这些时刻是我日常工作中的亮点，运用我的药学专业知识来造福人们的健康和幸福。\n\nM: I fully agree.\n男：我完全同意。\n\nUnfortunately, the opposite is true when the patient is too stubborn and they refuse to listen to us.\n不幸的是，当病人太固执、拒绝听我们的话时，情况就相反了。\n\nW: So true!\n女：太对了！\n\nIn other developments, we''ve had a lot of people coming in to get their flu shots, which is always good to see.\n其他方面，最近有很多人来打流感疫苗，这总是件好事。\n\nM: Good, good.\n男：好，好。\n\nI''ve been working on a new medication therapy management program for our patients with chronic conditions.\n我一直致力于一个针对慢性病患者的药物治疗管理的新项目。\n\nIt helps patients manage their medications more effectively.\n它能帮助病人更有效地管理他们的药物。\n\nW: That sounds interesting.\n女：听起来很有趣。\n\nM: We review all of their medications and make sure that they''re taking them as prescribed.\n男：我们会审查他们的所有药物，并确保他们按照医嘱用药。\n\nWe also look for any potential drug interactions or side effects.\n我们也会排查任何潜在的药物相互作用或副作用。\n\nPatient response has so far been very positive.\n到目前为止，病人的反馈非常积极。\n\nSome have reported seeing improvements in their health, which is really rewarding to see.\n有些人说他们的健康状况有所改善，这真的很有成就感。\n\nW: That''s great to hear.\n女：听到这个真好。\n\nDo you use text message reminders?\n你们用短信提醒吗？\n\nM: Yes, absolutely.\n男：是的，当然用。\n\nW: It''s a great idea.\n女：这是个好主意。\n\nMedication non-adherence is a big problem, especially for older patients.\n不遵医嘱用药是一个大问题，尤其是对老年患者来说。\n\nM: We''re still in the early stages of the program, but I''m hopeful that it will make a real difference for our patients.\n男：我们这个项目还处在早期阶段，但我希望它能给我们的病人带来真正的改变。\n\nQuestions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的对话回答问题5到8。\n\n5. What do we learn about the speakers?\n5. 关于两位讲话者，我们了解到什么？\n\n6. What does the woman say about an interesting case she had?\n6. 关于自己遇到的一个有趣的病例，女士说了什么？\n\n7. What does the man say he has been working on?\n7. 男士说他一直致力于什么？\n\n8. What is a big problem, especially for older patients, according to the woman?\n8. 据这位女士所说，什么是一个大问题，尤其是对老年患者而言？\n\nSection B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages.\nB节 说明：在本节中，你将会听到两篇短文。', 122, 1568.77, 218.26, 340.60, 345.25, 361.48, NULL, NULL, 'cet6-2026-06-2-g02', 'LONG_DIALOGUE', '[03:38.26]Conversation Two｜对话二\n[03:41.74]W: Hey there, Paul, how''s it going?｜女：嘿，保罗，最近怎么样？\n[03:43.83]M: Hi, Suzanne.｜男：嗨，苏珊娜。\n[03:45.02]Just keeping busy with the usual stuff—filling prescriptions, talking to patients about their medications, that kind of thing.｜就是忙着平常那些事——按处方配药、跟病人聊他们的药物之类的。\n[03:52.99]How about you?｜你呢？\n[03:54.13]W: Same here, although I did have an interesting case come in this morning.｜女：我也是，不过今天早上我确实接了一个有趣的病例。\n[03:58.93]A patient came in inquiring about a medication that had a potential interaction with another medication she was already taking.｜一位病人来咨询一种药物，这种药与她已经在服用的另一种药可能存在相互作用。\n[04:06.81]M: Oh, that''s always tricky.｜男：哦，那总是很棘手。\n[04:08.79]What did you do?｜你怎么办的？\n[04:10.00]W: I explained the issue to her as best I could, and recommended she double-check with her doctor.｜女：我尽可能地把问题解释给她听，并建议她再跟她的医生确认一下。\n[04:16.23]And you know what?｜你知道吗？\n[04:17.35]She actually followed my advice and the doctor ended up switching the patient''s medication.｜她真的听了我的建议，医生最终给这位病人换了药。\n[04:22.54]M: Ah, that''s great!｜男：啊，那太好了！\n[04:23.94]You must feel quite accomplished!｜你一定觉得很有成就感吧！\n[04:25.71]W: Totally! These moments are the highlights of my routine work, employing my pharmaceutical expertise to the benefit of people''s health and well-being.｜女：太对了！这些时刻是我日常工作中的亮点，运用我的药学专业知识来造福人们的健康和幸福。\n[04:35.06]M: I fully agree.｜男：我完全同意。\n[04:36.30]Unfortunately, the opposite is true when the patient is too stubborn and they refuse to listen to us.｜不幸的是，当病人太固执、拒绝听我们的话时，情况就相反了。\n[04:42.46]W: So true!｜女：太对了！\n[04:43.63]In other developments, we''ve had a lot of people coming in to get their flu shots, which is always good to see.｜其他方面，最近有很多人来打流感疫苗，这总是件好事。\n[04:49.66]M: Good, good.｜男：好，好。\n[04:50.89]I''ve been working on a new medication therapy management program for our patients with chronic conditions.｜我一直致力于一个针对慢性病患者的药物治疗管理的新项目。\n[04:56.77]It helps patients manage their medications more effectively.｜它能帮助病人更有效地管理他们的药物。\n[05:00.62]W: That sounds interesting.｜女：听起来很有趣。\n[05:02.20]M: We review all of their medications and make sure that they''re taking them as prescribed.｜男：我们会审查他们的所有药物，并确保他们按照医嘱用药。\n[05:07.39]We also look for any potential drug interactions or side effects.｜我们也会排查任何潜在的药物相互作用或副作用。\n[05:11.98]Patient response has so far been very positive.｜到目前为止，病人的反馈非常积极。\n[05:15.48]Some have reported seeing improvements in their health, which is really rewarding to see.｜有些人说他们的健康状况有所改善，这真的很有成就感。\n[05:20.50]W: That''s great to hear.｜女：听到这个真好。\n[05:21.75]Do you use text message reminders?｜你们用短信提醒吗？\n[05:25.13]M: Yes, absolutely.｜男：是的，当然用。\n[05:26.49]W: It''s a great idea.｜女：这是个好主意。\n[05:28.24]Medication non-adherence is a big problem, especially for older patients.｜不遵医嘱用药是一个大问题，尤其是对老年患者来说。\n[05:33.47]M: We''re still in the early stages of the program, but I''m hopeful that it will make a real difference for our patients.｜男：我们这个项目还处在早期阶段，但我希望它能给我们的病人带来真正的改变。\n[05:40.60]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的对话回答问题5到8。\n[05:45.25]5. What do we learn about the speakers?｜5. 关于两位讲话者，我们了解到什么？\n[06:01.78]6. What does the woman say about an interesting case she had?｜6. 关于自己遇到的一个有趣的病例，女士说了什么？\n[06:20.50]7. What does the man say he has been working on?｜7. 男士说他一直致力于什么？\n[06:37.04]8. What is a big problem, especially for older patients, according to the woman?｜8. 据这位女士所说，什么是一个大问题，尤其是对老年患者而言？\n[06:56.09]Section B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages.｜B节 说明：在本节中，你将会听到两篇短文。', NOW(), NOW()
