SELECT
  @a_cet6_2026_06_2_16, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2026-06-2.mp3', 'Recording One\n录音一\n\nMany people, myself included, have multiple areas of life they would like to improve.\n很多人，包括我自己在内，在生活中都有多个想要改进的方面。\n\nFor example, I would like to reach more people with my writing, to lift heavier weights at the gym, and to start practicing mindfulness more consistently.\n例如，我希望通过写作影响更多人，在健身房举起更重的重量，以及开始更持续地练习正念。\n\nThose are just a few of the goals I find desirable, and you probably have a long list yourself.\n这些只是我想要达成的一部分目标，而你可能也有一长串自己的清单。\n\nThe problem is, even if we are committed to working hard on our goals, our natural tendency is to return to our old habits at some point.\n问题是，即使我们下定决心为目标努力，我们仍然会在某个时刻重拾旧习。\n\nMaking a permanent lifestyle change is really difficult.\n要做出永久性的生活方式改变真的很困难。\n\nRecently, I''ve come across a few studies that will make these difficult lifestyle changes a bit easier.\n最近我看到了一些能让这些困难的生活方式改变变得稍微容易一些的研究。\n\nAs you''ll see, however, the approach to mastering many areas of life is somewhat counterintuitive.\n不过你会发现，掌握生活中多个领域的方法，在某种程度上是反直觉的。\n\nResearch has shown that you are two to three times more likely to stick with your habits if you make a specific plan for when, where and how you will perform the behavior.\n研究表明，如果你为将在何时、何地以及如何执行某个行为制订一个具体的计划，你坚持自己习惯的可能性会提高两到三倍。\n\nFor example, in one study scientists asked people to fill out a sentence to specify during the next week they would do at least 20 minutes of vigorous exercise on which day, at what time of day, and at or in what place.\n例如，在一项研究中，科学家让人们填写一个句子，具体说明在接下来的一周里他们将在哪一天、什么时间、什么地点进行至少20分钟的剧烈运动。\n\nResearchers found that people who filled out this sentence were two to three times more likely to actually exercise compared to a control group who did not make plans for their future behavior.\n研究人员发现，与那些没有为未来行为制订计划的对照组相比，填写了这句话的人实际去锻炼的可能性要高出两到三倍。\n\nPsychologists call these specific plans “implementation intentions” because they state when, where, and how you intend to implement a particular behavior.\n心理学家称这些具体计划为“执行意向”，因为它们明确了你在何时、何地以及如何执行某个特定行为。\n\nHowever, follow-up research has discovered implementation intentions only work when you focus on one thing at a time.\n然而，后续研究发现，“执行意向”只有在人们一次只专注一件事时才有效。\n\nIn fact, researchers found that people who tried to accomplish multiple goals were less committed and less likely to succeed than those who focused on a single goal.\n事实上，研究人员发现，与专注于单一目标的人相比，试图同时完成多个目标的人投入程度更低，成功的可能性也更低。\n\nAnother science-based reason to focus on one thing at a time is that when you begin practicing a new habit, it requires a lot of conscious effort to remember to do it.\n另一个基于科学、支持每次只专注一件事的原因是，当你开始培养一个新习惯时，需要大量有意识的努力记得去做这件事。\n\nAfter a while, however, the pattern of behavior becomes easier.\n然而，过一段时间后，这种行为模式会变得更容易。\n\nEventually, your new habit becomes a normal routine and the process is more or less mindless and automatic.\n最终，你的新习惯会成为一种常规流程，这个过程几乎无需思考、自动完成。\n\nThe most important thing to note is that there is some “tipping point” at which new habits become more or less automatic.\n最重要的是要注意：存在某个“临界点”，到了这个点，新习惯会或多或少地变得自动。\n\nThe time it takes to build a habit depends on many factors including how difficult the habit is, your genetics, and more.\n养成一个习惯所需的时间取决于很多因素，包括这个习惯的难度、个人先天特质等等。\n\nQuestions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n\n16. What does the speaker say is really difficult?\n16. 讲话者说什么事情真的很困难？\n\n17. What did researchers find about people with implementation intentions?\n17. 关于有执行意向的人，研究人员发现了什么？\n\n18. According to the speaker, what is required of you when you begin practicing a new habit?\n18. 根据讲话者，当你开始培养一个新习惯时，你需要什么？', 162, 1568.77, 852.18, 1013.89, 1019.34, 1036.70, 886.50, 889.99, 'cet6-2026-06-2-g05', 'LECTURE', '[14:12.18]Recording One｜录音一\n[14:14.24]Many people, myself included, have multiple areas of life they would like to improve.｜很多人，包括我自己在内，在生活中都有多个想要改进的方面。\n[14:20.80]For example, I would like to reach more people with my writing, to lift heavier weights at the gym, and to start practicing mindfulness more consistently.｜例如，我希望通过写作影响更多人，在健身房举起更重的重量，以及开始更持续地练习正念。\n[14:30.62]Those are just a few of the goals I find desirable, and you probably have a long list yourself.｜这些只是我想要达成的一部分目标，而你可能也有一长串自己的清单。\n[14:36.94]The problem is, even if we are committed to working hard on our goals, our natural tendency is to return to our old habits at some point.｜问题是，即使我们下定决心为目标努力，我们仍然会在某个时刻重拾旧习。\n[14:46.50]Making a permanent lifestyle change is really difficult.｜要做出永久性的生活方式改变真的很困难。\n[14:50.29]Recently, I''ve come across a few studies that will make these difficult lifestyle changes a bit easier.｜最近我看到了一些能让这些困难的生活方式改变变得稍微容易一些的研究。\n[14:56.92]As you''ll see, however, the approach to mastering many areas of life is somewhat counterintuitive.｜不过你会发现，掌握生活中多个领域的方法，在某种程度上是反直觉的。\n[15:03.94]Research has shown that you are two to three times more likely to stick with your habits if you make a specific plan for when, where and how you will perform the behavior.｜研究表明，如果你为将在何时、何地以及如何执行某个行为制订一个具体的计划，你坚持自己习惯的可能性会提高两到三倍。\n[15:15.84]For example, in one study scientists asked people to fill out a sentence to specify during the next week they would do at least 20 minutes of vigorous exercise on which day, at what time of day, and at or in what place.｜例如，在一项研究中，科学家让人们填写一个句子，具体说明在接下来的一周里他们将在哪一天、什么时间、什么地点进行至少20分钟的剧烈运动。\n[15:33.38]Researchers found that people who filled out this sentence were two to three times more likely to actually exercise compared to a control group who did not make plans for their future behavior.｜研究人员发现，与那些没有为未来行为制订计划的对照组相比，填写了这句话的人实际去锻炼的可能性要高出两到三倍。\n[15:45.03]Psychologists call these specific plans “implementation intentions” because they state when, where, and how you intend to implement a particular behavior.｜心理学家称这些具体计划为“执行意向”，因为它们明确了你在何时、何地以及如何执行某个特定行为。\n[15:55.15]However, follow-up research has discovered implementation intentions only work when you focus on one thing at a time.｜然而，后续研究发现，“执行意向”只有在人们一次只专注一件事时才有效。\n[16:04.10]In fact, researchers found that people who tried to accomplish multiple goals were less committed and less likely to succeed than those who focused on a single goal.｜事实上，研究人员发现，与专注于单一目标的人相比，试图同时完成多个目标的人投入程度更低，成功的可能性也更低。\n[16:14.55]Another science-based reason to focus on one thing at a time is that when you begin practicing a new habit, it requires a lot of conscious effort to remember to do it.｜另一个基于科学、支持每次只专注一件事的原因是，当你开始培养一个新习惯时，需要大量有意识的努力记得去做这件事。\n[16:25.79]After a while, however, the pattern of behavior becomes easier.｜然而，过一段时间后，这种行为模式会变得更容易。\n[16:30.11]Eventually, your new habit becomes a normal routine and the process is more or less mindless and automatic.｜最终，你的新习惯会成为一种常规流程，这个过程几乎无需思考、自动完成。\n[16:37.20]The most important thing to note is that there is some “tipping point” at which new habits become more or less automatic.｜最重要的是要注意：存在某个“临界点”，到了这个点，新习惯会或多或少地变得自动。\n[16:45.38]The time it takes to build a habit depends on many factors including how difficult the habit is, your genetics, and more.｜养成一个习惯所需的时间取决于很多因素，包括这个习惯的难度、个人先天特质等等。\n[16:53.89]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题16到18。\n[16:59.34]16. What does the speaker say is really difficult?｜16. 讲话者说什么事情真的很困难？\n[17:17.00]17. What did researchers find about people with implementation intentions?｜17. 关于有执行意向的人，研究人员发现了什么？\n[17:35.66]18. According to the speaker, what is required of you when you begin practicing a new habit?｜18. 根据讲话者，当你开始培养一个新习惯时，你需要什么？', NOW(), NOW()
