SELECT
  @a_cet6_2026_06_2_19, '/audio/exams/listening-cet6-2026-06-2.mp3', 'Recording Two\n录音二\n\nIt was a lazy, hazy, crazy day on the dwarf planet Pluto when New Horizons, a spacecraft from Earth, flew by at a speed of 31,000 miles per hour two years ago.\n两年前，在一个慵懒、朦胧而又疯狂的日子里，来自地球的新视野号航天器以每小时31,000英里的速度飞越矮行星冥王星。\n\nThe spacecraft took a bunch of photos, made some quick measurements of Pluto''s atmosphere, and sent them all back here, giving planetary scientists their first up-close look of the distant dwarf planet.\n该航天器拍摄了大量照片，对冥王星的大气层进行了一些快速测量，并将它们传回地球，让行星科学家首次近距离观察到了这颗遥远的矮行星。\n\nOne of the more bizarre things they found was that the haze in Pluto''s atmosphere was much thicker than previously thought.\n他们发现的一个更奇怪的现象是，冥王星大气层中的雾霾比之前想象的要厚得多。\n\nThe planet also had an atmosphere much cooler than earlier estimates, topping out at -333.4 ℉.\n这颗行星的大气层温度也比早期估计的要低得多，最高温度为零下333.4华氏度。\n\nNow, a study published in Nature links those two atmospheric observations.\n现在，一项发表在《自然》杂志上的研究将这两个大气观测结果联系了起来。\n\nA computer model developed by planetary scientist Xi Zhang and colleagues shows the haze of tiny droplets in the upper atmosphere is likely scattering light from the Sun, preventing heat from reaching the planet below.\n由行星科学家张曦及其同事开发的计算机模型表明，上层大气中微小液滴形成的雾霾很可能散射了来自太阳的光线，阻止热量到达下方的行星。\n\nWe see hazy skies on Earth too, from time to time, whenever solid particles get suspended in the air—from water-based fogs, to ash from fires, to the toxic droplets that make up a thick smog.\n我们在地球上偶尔也会看到雾霾弥漫的天空，每当固体颗粒悬浮在空气中时（就会出现）——从水雾、火灾产生的灰烬，到构成浓密烟雾的有毒液滴。\n\nBut on Earth, the overall temperature of the planet is dominated by the distribution of gases in our atmosphere.\n但在地球上，行星的整体温度是由我们大气层中气体的分布决定的。\n\nOn Pluto, the authors suggest, haze might be more influential.\n作者们认为，在冥王星上，雾霾可能更具影响力。\n\nParticles can reflect light from the Sun here on Earth.\n在地球上，颗粒物也能反射太阳光。\n\nIt happens after volcanoes erupt, emitting clouds of particles that reflect the Sun''s rays into the atmosphere.\n这种情况发生在火山爆发之后，火山喷发出的颗粒云将太阳光线反射回大气层。\n\nSome people even propose imitating this phenomenon to alleviate the effects of climate change.\n一些人甚至提议模仿这种现象来缓解气候变化的影响。\n\nBut our planet isn''t dominated by that process, or at least it hasn''t been for a few billion years.\n但我们的星球并非由这一过程主导，或者说至少在过去几十亿年里不是这样。\n\nIf Pluto''s atmosphere is very different from ours and from that of other nearby planets, it could help scientists expand their understanding of planetary atmospheres.\n如果冥王星的大气层与地球以及附近其他行星的大气层非常不同，那将有助于科学家拓展对行星大气层的理解。\n\nBut right now, this is just a good guess based on data from New Horizons and a sophisticated computer model, not direct observations of the haze''s composition.\n但目前，这仅仅是基于“新视野号”数据和复杂计算机模型的一个不错的推测，而非对雾霾成分的直接观测。\n\nThere are other theories floating around to explain Pluto''s cool temperatures, including blaming the cold air on gases in the atmosphere.\n目前还有其他理论来解释冥王星的低温，包括将冷空气归因于大气中的气体。\n\nLuckily, the authors will get a chance to test their notions when the James Webb Space Telescope launches later.\n幸运的是，当詹姆斯·韦布空间望远镜稍后发射时，作者们将有机会验证他们的想法。\n\nThat observatory can detect different spectrums of light, which the hazy Plutonian atmosphere would be positively glowing with as it scattered sunlight.\n该天文台可以探测到不同光谱的光，而冥王星朦胧的大气层在散射阳光时必然会发出这些光谱的明亮光芒。\n\nThe researchers just have to wait and see if the light from the telescope validates their bright idea.\n研究人员只需要等着看望远镜传回的光线数据是否能验证他们的绝妙想法。\n\nQuestions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.\n请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n\n19. What was one of the more bizarre findings about Pluto''s atmosphere?\n19. 关于冥王星的大气层，更奇怪的一个发现是什么？\n\n20. What is said to determine the overall temperature on Earth?\n20. 据说是什么决定了地球的整体温度？\n\n21. What does the speaker say the researchers have to wait and see?\n21. 讲话者说，研究人员必须等着看什么？', 184, 1568.77, 1075.84, 1259.86, 1266.53, 1283.09, 1107.63, 1115.89, 'cet6-2026-06-2-g06', 'LECTURE', '[17:55.84]Recording Two｜录音二\n[17:58.25]It was a lazy, hazy, crazy day on the dwarf planet Pluto when New Horizons, a spacecraft from Earth, flew by at a speed of 31,000 miles per hour two years ago.｜两年前，在一个慵懒、朦胧而又疯狂的日子里，来自地球的新视野号航天器以每小时31,000英里的速度飞越矮行星冥王星。\n[18:13.16]The spacecraft took a bunch of photos, made some quick measurements of Pluto''s atmosphere, and sent them all back here, giving planetary scientists their first up-close look of the distant dwarf planet.｜该航天器拍摄了大量照片，对冥王星的大气层进行了一些快速测量，并将它们传回地球，让行星科学家首次近距离观察到了这颗遥远的矮行星。\n[18:27.63]One of the more bizarre things they found was that the haze in Pluto''s atmosphere was much thicker than previously thought.｜他们发现的一个更奇怪的现象是，冥王星大气层中的雾霾比之前想象的要厚得多。\n[18:36.19]The planet also had an atmosphere much cooler than earlier estimates, topping out at -333.4 ℉.｜这颗行星的大气层温度也比早期估计的要低得多，最高温度为零下333.4华氏度。\n[18:47.12]Now, a study published in Nature links those two atmospheric observations.｜现在，一项发表在《自然》杂志上的研究将这两个大气观测结果联系了起来。\n[18:53.24]A computer model developed by planetary scientist Xi Zhang and colleagues shows the haze of tiny droplets in the upper atmosphere is likely scattering light from the Sun, preventing heat from reaching the planet below.｜由行星科学家张曦及其同事开发的计算机模型表明，上层大气中微小液滴形成的雾霾很可能散射了来自太阳的光线，阻止热量到达下方的行星。\n[19:08.50]We see hazy skies on Earth too, from time to time, whenever solid particles get suspended in the air—from water-based fogs, to ash from fires, to the toxic droplets that make up a thick smog.｜我们在地球上偶尔也会看到雾霾弥漫的天空，每当固体颗粒悬浮在空气中时（就会出现）——从水雾、火灾产生的灰烬，到构成浓密烟雾的有毒液滴。\n[19:23.21]But on Earth, the overall temperature of the planet is dominated by the distribution of gases in our atmosphere.｜但在地球上，行星的整体温度是由我们大气层中气体的分布决定的。\n[19:30.01]On Pluto, the authors suggest, haze might be more influential.｜作者们认为，在冥王星上，雾霾可能更具影响力。\n[19:35.35]Particles can reflect light from the Sun here on Earth.｜在地球上，颗粒物也能反射太阳光。\n[19:39.38]It happens after volcanoes erupt, emitting clouds of particles that reflect the Sun''s rays into the atmosphere.｜这种情况发生在火山爆发之后，火山喷发出的颗粒云将太阳光线反射回大气层。\n[19:46.78]Some people even propose imitating this phenomenon to alleviate the effects of climate change.｜一些人甚至提议模仿这种现象来缓解气候变化的影响。\n[19:53.66]But our planet isn''t dominated by that process, or at least it hasn''t been for a few billion years.｜但我们的星球并非由这一过程主导，或者说至少在过去几十亿年里不是这样。\n[20:00.86]If Pluto''s atmosphere is very different from ours and from that of other nearby planets, it could help scientists expand their understanding of planetary atmospheres.｜如果冥王星的大气层与地球以及附近其他行星的大气层非常不同，那将有助于科学家拓展对行星大气层的理解。\n[20:12.17]But right now, this is just a good guess based on data from New Horizons and a sophisticated computer model, not direct observations of the haze''s composition.｜但目前，这仅仅是基于“新视野号”数据和复杂计算机模型的一个不错的推测，而非对雾霾成分的直接观测。\n[20:24.04]There are other theories floating around to explain Pluto''s cool temperatures, including blaming the cold air on gases in the atmosphere.｜目前还有其他理论来解释冥王星的低温，包括将冷空气归因于大气中的气体。\n[20:33.86]Luckily, the authors will get a chance to test their notions when the James Webb Space Telescope launches later.｜幸运的是，当詹姆斯·韦布空间望远镜稍后发射时，作者们将有机会验证他们的想法。\n[20:41.62]That observatory can detect different spectrums of light, which the hazy Plutonian atmosphere would be positively glowing with as it scattered sunlight.｜该天文台可以探测到不同光谱的光，而冥王星朦胧的大气层在散射阳光时必然会发出这些光谱的明亮光芒。\n[20:52.26]The researchers just have to wait and see if the light from the telescope validates their bright idea.｜研究人员只需要等着看望远镜传回的光线数据是否能验证他们的绝妙想法。\n[20:59.86]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.｜请根据你刚刚听到的录音回答问题19到21。\n[21:06.53]19. What was one of the more bizarre findings about Pluto''s atmosphere?｜19. 关于冥王星的大气层，更奇怪的一个发现是什么？\n[21:23.39]20. What is said to determine the overall temperature on Earth?｜20. 据说是什么决定了地球的整体温度？\n[21:42.99]21. What does the speaker say the researchers have to wait and see?｜21. 讲话者说，研究人员必须等着看什么？', NOW(), NOW()
